\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-GLOBAL MARKETS-European stocks fend off rate risks, yen droops again\b0\par\par\uc2 974 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par 13:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Refiles to remove typo from headline)\par \par \uc2 * European stocks bounce after two days of falls\par \par \uc2 * Fed looms over broader markets; risk of 100 bp hike next week\par \par \uc2 * China property stocks buoyed by hopes of official help\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk keeps dollar short of 145 yen barrier\par \par \uc2 By Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 15 (Reuters) - Stock markets were sluggish and the dollar and bond yields shuffled higher on Thursday as the likelihood of a further jump in global borrowing costs, including a possible 100 basis point U.S. rate hike next week, kept the bears on the prowl.\par \par \uc2 Europe's main bourses made a positive start after two days in the red, but the Japanese yen - pummelled to a 24-year low this month - drooped again as Tokyo posted a record trade deficit overnight.\par \par \uc2 It was a big day too for the crypto markets with a major software upgrade to the Ethereum blockchain dubbed the "Merge" taking place.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had refrained from providing more support overnight although there had been some welcome signs of support elsewhere for the country's battered property market.\par \par \uc2 The broader focus however remained squarely on the risk of rising interest rates and painfully high energy prices causing recessions.\par \par \uc2 Credit rating firm \uc2 Fitch\uc2  became the latest to slash its world economy forecasts while bond markets were watching the German yield curve flatten markedly - another classic recession indicator.\par \par \uc2 "We\u8217\'20\'19ve had something of a perfect storm for the global economy in recent months," \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Chief Economist \uc2 Brian Coulton\uc2  said, blaming "the gas crisis in Europe, a sharp acceleration in interest rate hikes and a deepening property slump in China".\par \par \uc2 The dollar, which has soared this year amid the jump in U.S. interest rates and global scramble for safety, was showing its strength again.\par \par \uc2 Expectations that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will raise rates another 75-100 basis points next week pushed the greenback back up 0.3% against the yen, after the yen jumped on Wednesday when \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  calls to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  desks triggered \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk.\par \par \uc2 The euro was again back below parity against the dollar. It was down 0.15% at $0.9964 and not too far from a 20-year low of $0.9864 hit last week. Britain's pound, which has also been hammered over the last month, likewise was 0.25% softer at $1.15115.\par \par \uc2 "The (\uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 ) steps were in reality the last efforts to halt JPY depreciation before actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," \uc2 MUFG\uc2 's European global markets research head \uc2 Derek Halpenny\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "But it is also highly likely that there is still a deep reluctance on the part of the authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added, on the view that such action might not be successful in the current environment.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 2011 and back then it was to restrain an overly strong yen.\par \par \uc2 FLAT AND FLATTENING\par \par \uc2 Tokyo isn't the only Asian capital concerned about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness. South Korea's won bounced off a near 13-year low overnight as it appeared that its authorities had been dishing out verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  again.\par \par \uc2 Among the main stock markets, MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan turned during the session to finish down 0.2%. The Nikkei rose 0.2% though, while the main Hong Kong property index surged over 4% after reports that some Chinese developers were finally being allowed to slash prices.\par \par \uc2 The world's second-largest economy narrowly avoided contracting in the second quarter as widespread COVID-19 lockdowns and the slumping property sector badly damaged consumer and business confidence.\par \par \uc2 With few signs China will significantly ease zero-COVID soon, some analysts expect the economy to grow by just 3% this year, which would be the slowest since 1976, excluding the 2.2% expansion during the initial COVID hit in 2020.\par \par \uc2 "Equity markets are presently in no-man's land," said Sean Darby, global equity strategist at Jefferies in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "Better macro news to support earnings is discounted as (there is) the need for further tightening to quash growth \u8211\'20\'13 while CPI prints are not declining fast enough," he said.\par \par \uc2 S&P 500 futures, Dow and Nasdaq futures were all broadly flat, pointing to a slow day on Wall Street later.\par \par \uc2 Fed funds futures, which were dumped along with stocks after Tuesday's stubbornly hot U.S. inflation reading but were helped by lower producer price figures on Thursday, imply a 30% chance of a 100 basis point rate hike next week. They have the benchmark U.S. interest rate as high as 4.3% by February.\par \par \uc2 Two-year U.S. yields, which track near-term rate expectations, edged up to 3.029%, bringing the rise for the week so far to 23 basis points in a seventh straight weekly gain.\par \par \uc2 European moves saw the 2-year German yield rise 2.5 bps to 1.435% leaving it just off its highest since July 2011. . Germany's 10-year yield, the euro zone's benchmark, rose 4.5 basis points (bps) to 1.746%.\par \par \uc2 ING analysts said that comments by \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief economist \uc2 Philip Lane\uc2  on Wednesday had endorsed the hawks' narrative. It "is another clue that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has experienced a significant shift in its reaction function," they wrote.\par \par \uc2 Later in the day European trade data is due and Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  meets Russia's \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in Uzbekistan.\par \par \uc2 In oil markets, Brent crude futures dipped 24 cents to $93.86 a barrel. Spot gold dropped 0.4% to $1,689 an ounce, having steadily slipped as the dollar and U.S. yields have gone up.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Stefano Rebaudo in Milan, Tom Westbrook in Singapore and Wayne Cole in Sydney) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-15T12:45:31.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220915ei9f01fxh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
