\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's solo  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  won't be that effective - ruling party official\b0\par\par\uc2 213 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par 05:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 15 (Reuters) - Japan lacks effective means to combat the yen's sharp falls, as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  unilaterally in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will likely have a limited impact in reversing its downtrend, Satsuki Katayama, head of a ruling party panel on financial affairs, said.\par \par \uc2 "Solo \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be that effective" in stemming sharp yen falls, which are driven by the interest-rate gap between the United States and Japan, she told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Raising Japan's ultra-low interest rates would also be difficult given the impact that could have on the country's 550 trillion yen ($3.84 trillion) of bank loans, Katayama, chairperson of the Liberal Democratic Party's (LDP) research commission on finance and banking systems, said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 A former \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) official, Katayama has deep expertise on financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities on Wednesday signalled the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the yen by conducting a rate check with banks, though many analysts doubt Tokyo will actually step in due to the difficulty of convincing its G7 counterparts.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 143.3300 yen) (Reporting by Kaori Kaneko, writing by Leika Kihara Editing by Chang-Ran Kim) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-15T04:00:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220915ei9f00dqh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex