rev \u8226\'20\'22 14 Sep 23:50 JP Aug Imports YY, 46.7% f'cast, 47.2% prev; Exports YY, 23.6% f'cast, 19.0% prev \u8226\'20\'22 14 Sep 23:50 JP Aug Trade Balance Total JPY, -2,398.2 bln f'cast, -1,436.8 bln prev, -1,433.9 bln r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 14 Sep 23:50 JP 10 Sep, w/e Foreign Bond Investment, JPY 149.1 bln prev; Foreign Stock Investment 230.5 bln prev \u8226\'20\'22 14 Sep 23:50 JP 10 Sep, w/e Foreign Invest JP Stock, JPY -704.2 bln prev; Foreign Invest JP Bonds 111.6 bln prev \u8226\'20\'22 15 Sep 01:30 AU Aug Employment, 35.0k f'cast, -40.9k prev \u8226\'20\'22 15 Sep 01:30 AU Aug Full Time Employment, -86.9k prev \u8226\'20\'22 15 Sep 01:30 AU Aug Participation Rate, 66.6% f'cast, 66.4% prev \u8226\'20\'22 15 Sep 01:30 AU Aug Unemployment Rate, 3.4% f'cast, 3.4% prev \u8226\'20\'22 15 Sep 01:30 AU Aug Reserve Assets Total, AUD 81,090 mln \u8226\'20\'22 15 Sep 04:30 JP Jul Tertiary Ind Act NSA, 4.4% prev Looking Ahead - Events, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 09:15 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Guindos gives keynote speech on "Euro area current policy challenges" at annual International Conference 2022 \u8226\'20\'22 09:30 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's McCaul participates in a panel in OMFIF-SPI symposium on developments in international standards for sustainable finance \u8226\'20\'22 12:00 \uc2 Portugal's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, \uc2 M\u225\'00\'E1rio Centeno\uc2 , speaks at a financial supervision conference in Lisbon Macro Themes In Play \u8226\'20\'22 The dollar index slipped 0.14% on Wednesday, dialing back the previous session's post-CPI gains after reports of \uc2 BOJ\uc2  rate-checking spooked the yen-selling market as USD/JPY approached the key 145 level. \u8226\'20\'22 The modest dip against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- especially the index's primary component the euro -- indicated that this was not yet a sea change in the dollar's months long rally driven by surging U.S. interest rates. \u8226\'20\'22 Front-end U.S. Eurodollar futures contracts slipped a touch, hinting at a higher Fed hike path. Fed funds contracts indicate the terminal U.S. rate is now expected in April 2023 at 4.39%, which should keep the USD anchored by recent highs. \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD headed toward the U.S. close up 0.2% at 0.9987, with traders unwilling to move too far away from parity. Front-end Euribor futures dipped as well, but the U.S. rate advantage over the euro zone tempered EUR gains. \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY was down 1% at 143.15 but 0.45 above its U.S. low at 142.56. \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears are keeping resistance at 145 intact, for now, but without a significant move below support at the Sept. 13 low of 141.60, traders are likely to revisit 24-year highs by 145 and test the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s intentions to defend the yen. \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/USD rallied after UK CPI dipped to 9.9%, a touch below the Reuters consensus forecast at 10.2%. Traders positioned for higher inflation unwound shorts put on after Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s U.S. CPI. \u8226\'20\'22 With U.S.-UK rates expected to diverge into year-end, any rallies likely serve as a selling opportunity unless the \uc2 BoE\uc2  becomes more aggressive or the Fed indicates a pivot to easier policy. \u8226\'20\'22 Early U.S. equity gains gave way to selling into the close. Treasury yields were a touch higher but well off early-U.S. highs, and the 2s-10s spread was at -37bp. \u8226\'20\'22 Souring risk into the close dragged bitcoin lower by 1.5% to $19.8k, while ETH slid 2% to 1,571. Gold dropped 0.33% to $1,696 while silver managed to gain 1.2% to $19.55. Commentary and Analysis EUR/USD-Shorts cede some ground as risk improves slightly \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD hit 1.00235 on EBS in Europe's am then dipped, NY opened near 1.0010 \u8226\'20\'22 Slide extended to 0.9971, fall aided by EUR/JPY drop to 142.33 on EBS \u8226\'20\'22 Pricing in of higher terminal Fed rate also weighed on EUR/USD \u8226\'20\'22 Only a muted reaction seen after US August PPI data \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD then lifted as US yields softened & US$ slid lower \u8226\'20\'22 Equity gains & USD/CNH dip below 6.9700 helped buoy EUR/USD \u8226\'20\'22 Pair sat near 1.0000 and was up +0.28% late in the session \u8226\'20\'22 Techs lean bearish; pair near falling 10- & 21-DMAs, monthly RSI falling \u8226\'20\'22 Fed, \uc2 ECB\uc2  expectations should help limit EUR/USD rallies USD/JPY bulls see red at 145 as talk of BoJ rate check stokes dollar sales \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY ending NorAm -1.1% at 143; Wednesday's range 144.97-142.55 \u8226\'20\'22 Pair hit on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears near 145, option interest tipped as well \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  signals chance of yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; market unconvinced \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears push USD/JPY toward pre-US-CPI lows \u8226\'20\'22 Support by rising 10-DMA (142.46), then Tuesday's pre-CPI low 141.60 \u8226\'20\'22 Bulls in control above 141.30, 50% Fib of 137.61-144.99 \u8226\'20\'22 Res 143.23 55-HMA, 21-HMA 143.66 and 24-yr high at 144.99 on Sep 7 Sterling bulls recoup some US CPI-related losses after UK CPI dip \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/USD firm into NY close +0.63% at 1.1570; NorAm range 1.1589-34 \u8226\'20\'22 Pair shrugs off surprise UK CPI dip, core rise \u8226\'20\'22 Sterling CPI bounce could easily revert to losses \u8226\'20\'22 Pair bid below 1.15 for now, straddles 10-DMA by 1.1546 \u8226\'20\'22 Support at Wednesday low 1.1480, Sep 8 low 1.1464, 2022 low at 1.1407 \u8226\'20\'22 Res at Wed high 1.1589, 55-HMA 1.1605, 21-DMA at 1.1677 \u8226\'20\'22 Bears remain in control below 1.1842, 50% Fib of 1.2277-1.1407 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/GBP -0.39% to 0.8639, Wed range 0.8683-27; higher UK terminal rate boosts GBP post-UK-CPI AUD/USD-Risk bounce help protect key support zone \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD fell to 0.6708 in early NY as US rates buoyed US$ \u8226\'20\'22 Dip buyers emerged ahead of the 0.6680/00 support zone however \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD rallied into positive territory & hit 0.6760, sat nearby late \u8226\'20\'22 Equity, commodity gains helped to lift AUD/USD \u8226\'20\'22 Erosion of early US yield gains softened the US$ \u8226\'20\'22 Daily bull hammer forms on AUD/USD chart, daily RSI is rising \u8226\'20\'22 Monthly RSI falling & pair holds below 50% Fib of 0.5510-0.8007 \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears push USD/JPY toward pre-US-CPI lows USD/JPY fell on Wednesday after familiar resistance before 145 and threatening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  signs from Japanese officials beat back the advance, though the yen remains vulnerable with interest-rate fundamentals still a major headwind. The USD/JPY drop of 1.4% to 142.65 showed traders' unease with the dollar's meteoric rise of 24% year-to-date against the yen, as well as concerns about the patience of Japanese officials. But Tuesday's U.S. core CPI rise and less-than-expected reduction in headline inflation has rekindled Fed rate-hike betting. The terminal rate for fed funds has risen to 4.40%, while Japanese rates remain anchored near zero, the cause of yen weakness with the BoJ sticking to its accommodative policies. Without a policy change, markets will probably test Tokyo's resolve. The outlook for the yen remains challenging even after the Fed reaches its peak rate level, since policymakers have said they will keep rates higher for longer, making U.S.-Japan convergence unlikely anytime soon. That will keep 145 under threat, with key psychological resistance at 150 the next stop if it breaks. Chart: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3RL0loV"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  Sterling CPI bounce could easily revert to losses GBP/USD rose on Wednesday after data showed an unexpected fall in headline UK inflation but cable's rise appeared to be a dead-cat bounce from the previous session's losses in the face of a dour outlook for Britain's economy. Cable's 0.6% rise to 1.1570 benefited from investors' willingness to shrug off a slight increase in core CPI, though economists still predict price growth will hit 11% by October. Though the government is expected to introduce energy price caps to cushion the blow to UK consumers, such action could also keep inflation higher for longer. Futures pricing for UK and U.S. interest rates indicate similar terminal rate expectations near 4.45% in H1 2023, though the Fed will arrive at its rate peak ahead of the BoE. With U.S. price growth near 200bps below the UK, the Fed can also begin addressing inflation-related growth issues earlier than the \uc2 BoE\uc2 , which should keep the dollar the more dominant in the GBP/USD equation. Risks remain tipped to the downside for GBP/USD, with the 2022 low at 1.1407 likely to give way as sterling bears target 1985 lows by 1.0520. Chart: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3U2aoaG"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  Chart of the Day (EUR/USD) EUR/USD clawed back some of the previous session's sharp losses on Wednesday, but yield spreads -- influenced by Fed and \uc2 ECB\uc2  expectations -- should limit gains, risking a slide to the December 2002 low. Investors have been raising terminal-rate expectations for the euro zone and U.S., but the Fed is still seen as more aggressive than the \uc2 ECB\uc2 , which should maintain the dollar's yield advantage over the euro. German-U.S. two-year spreads have trended tighter since early August but still favor the dollar by more than 230bp, presenting a EUR/USD headwind unless it narrows further. Italian-German spreads present an additional euro-negative risk before Italian elections on Sept. 25. A right-leaning coalition is expected to lead Italy, potentially driving tensions between Rome and Brussels that could increase uncertainty and widen Italian-German spreads over public debt concerns. With many investors looking for a test of the December 2002 low, EUR/USD rallies are likely to be sold, with a break potentially ushering in a dash toward bigger support near 0.9600. Charts: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3QH0s3I"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 & {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3UdesVU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-17T19:38:41.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220914ei9e03k35\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
