but timing tricky for bears The Australian dollar's fundamental backdrop versus the greenback is decidedly negative, but AUD bears have been burned by selling into weakness in recent months. The AUD/USD has fallen below 0.6700 twice in the last two months, hitting 0.6682 on July 14 only to rally nearly 7% to the 0.7136 peak on Aug 11. It touched 0.6699 on Sept 7, before rallying over 3% to 0.6915, ahead of Tuesday's U.S. CPI release. The hotter-than-expected CPI undermined the "inflation has peaked" narrative, which was the driving force behind rallies in risk assets and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite hawkish commentary and actions from the Federal Reserve and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The market is starting to price in a full percentage-point rate hike by the Fed at next week's meeting, with FEDWATCH indicating a 39% probability. This contrasts with the latest signals from \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  Governor \uc2 Philip Lowe\uc2  who hinted they may opt for smaller 25 bps hikes in the coming months. With the Fed clearly seeking to blunt demand and slow economic growth to below trend, and China's economy still struggling with COVID lockdowns, it is difficult to make a case for sustained rallies in equities, commodities and risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The AUD/USD technical outlook is negative after completing a bearish outside day on Tuesday, but the pair is oversold and may bounce in the near term. Buying AUD/USD put options or selling rallies towards the 10-day moving average at 0.6785 with a stop above the 21-day MA at 0.6845 is the favoured strategy for bears. \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's yuan fixing blackbox seems to be getting darker Consensus among market watchers is that the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  has been manipulating the yuan's daily benchmark. The apparent adjustment to the methodology for setting the daily midpoint remains unclear, muddying the waters for yuan investors. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  hasn't acknowledged that it's been adjusting the USD/CNY fixing via renewed use of the counter-cyclical factor, but when the CCF was re-introduced in 2018, it took several weeks after discrepancies were observed before they confirmed the fact. Over the last two weeks, the fix has deviated substantially from the well-established mechanism; Wednesday's fix was nearly 600 pips below some estimates. Before, the CCF was used to partially offset volatility due to large overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, if the yuan had ended the previous session weaker than its daily benchmark rate, inducing a counter-cyclical effect. Now, the USD/CNY fixing seems to be consistently depressed even when the market moves in favour of the yuan. This suggests a more heavy-handed approach to defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as interest rates rise elsewhere. Arguably, a weaker yuan should be more welcome, as China's trade-based economy withers. But Beijing has to balance export competitiveness with ensuring price pressures remain subdued. Out-of-control yuan depreciation won't be condoned, especially before the Communist Party Congress. If USD/CNY breaches the 7.0000 barrier, the ensuing deluge of bearish yuan bets, on top of the now unpredictable benchmark-setting methodology, would erode confidence in the renminbi as a future reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-17T05:00:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220914ei9e00jzh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
