\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen recovers lost ground on \uc2 BOJ\uc2  rate check, hints on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par 13:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO/LONDON, Sept 14 (Reuters) - The yen jumped over 1% on Wednesday after media reports the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted a rate check in apparent preparation for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while other majors tried to regain the ground they'd lost a day earlier on the surging dollar.\par \par \uc2 The dollar slid more than 1% to 142.9 yen after the Nikkei website reported the rate check, citing unidentified sources. In a rate check, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials call up dealers and ask for the price of buying or selling yen.\par \par \uc2 Reuters later confirmed the rate check with a market source.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the day, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had softened to as low as 144.97 per dollar, extending its sharp decline from the day before when an unexpected rise in the U.S. consumer price index (CPI), likely leading to further aggressive rate hikes from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , sent the greenback soaring.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which tracks the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against six main peers, jumped 1.5% on Tuesday, its largest daily percentage gain since March 2020.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  told reporters on Wednesday that recent yen moves have been "rapid and one-sided", adding that yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was among the government's options should such moves continue.\par \par \uc2 "If the market continues to sell the yen, there is more pressure for the (\b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) and \uc2 BOJ\uc2  to communicate to the market that the recent move has been too fast," said Masayuki Kichikawa, chief macro strategist at Sumitomo Mitsui DS Asset Management\par \par \uc2 However, actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a larger step.\par \par \uc2 "Currently, the dollar is becoming stronger and the yen weakening due to the big interest rate differentials between the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so it's hard (for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to be effective. That's why I think it's better to wait," said Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If the dollar rises above 145 yen, the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will rise to about 60% from 10% to 20% before, rather than becoming 100%."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a 24-year low of 144.99 last week.\par \par \uc2 As inflation is a small concern in Japan, authorities are keeping yields on Japanese government bonds pinned down to help with the economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, the yield on two-year Treasury notes , which typically reflects interest rate expectations, peaked at 3.804% on Wednesday, the highest since 2007. The 10-year yield last stood at 3.4410%.\par \par \uc2 Financial markets now have fully priced in an interest rate hike of at least 75 bps at the conclusion of the Fed's policy meeting next week, with a 30% probability of a super-sized, full percentage-point increase, according to the \uc2 CME\uc2 's Fedwatch tool.\par \par \uc2 The change followed red-hot inflation figures. A day earlier, the probability of a 100 bps hike was zero.\par \par \uc2 Other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were still hunkered down after yesterday's battering.\par \par \uc2 The euro managed to climb back above parity with the dollar to $1.0014, up 0.47%, but continued to reel from Tuesday's 1.5% fall, and was not far from last week's 20-year low of $0.9864.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Commission\uc2 's unveiling of a series of proposals to curb the energy price spike that has rocked Europe and weakened the euro, also gave the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  some support.\par \par \uc2 Sterling which lost 1.6% on Tuesday, was up 0.43% at $1.1543 after lower fuel prices caused an unexpected fall in British inflation in August, according to figures released Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The risk-sensitive Aussie was flat at $0.67375, after a precipitous 2.26% slide overnight. Bitcoin, which also moves in line with investors' attitude to riskier assets, was steady at $20,300 after losing 9.9% on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kevin Buckland, Rae Wee and Alun John; Editing by Kim Coghill, Edmund Klamann, Toby Chopra and Mark Heinrich) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-14T12:26:34.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220914ei9e021ud\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
