\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par 10:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0810 GMT - Talk of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at this juncture may not have material impact due to the market's focus on the Fed's expected hawkishness at the coming FOMC meeting, Joey Chew, senior Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Global Research, says in an email. Chew notes the USD/JPY's sharp fall earlier Wednesday after Nikkei reported that the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  conducted a check on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates with banks, which could be perceived as a precursor to \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . An actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might lead only to a large but short-lived reaction, Chew adds, citing an episode of USD-selling/JPY-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in December 1997. USD/JPY falls 1.1% to 142.94. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0808 GMT - The introduction of a cap on household energy prices means that inflation will likely peak at around 11% in October, sooner than and below the 16% inflation rate previously forecast as a peak, ING's developed markets economist James Smith says in a note. In 2023, base effects from high energy prices are likely to push inflation down more quickly, he says. "We think [inflation] could be more-or-less back to the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 's 2% target by the end of next year, crazy as that currently seems," Smith says. However, the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  isn't likely to pause its interest rates hikes soon as the labor market remains tight and there are concerns over persistent pressures on wage growth, he says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0754 GMT - Malaysia's budget deficit is likely to narrow next year, partly because a lot of the "lumpy spending" that was needed when Covid first hit is no longer required, says Lee Heng Guie, executive director of the Socio-Economic Research Centre. Speaking at an event, Lee said he expects the deficit to narrow to 4.5%-5.5% of GDP in 2023 from a projected 6.0% this year. One driver will the government's expected rationalization of subsidies, including for fuel, as well as higher revenue from petroleum income and regular taxes amid rising oil prices and economic recoveries at home and abroad, he says. Lee thinks that the government should also reintroduce the goods and services tax--scrapped in 2018--but at a lower rate of 4%, compared with 6% previously, and not until 2024. (yingxian.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0750 GMT - The average funding cost for Italian debt is "clearly" manageable for now but it is important that this is not jeopardized by the multiple challenges ahead, says Sylvain De Bus, deputy head of global bonds at Candriam, in a note. He refers to high public debt, labor-market rigidities, the energy crisis, weak demographics, political uncertainty and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's changing monetary policy with front-loading of interest-rate rises. In this context, Candriam maintains its prudent stance toward Italian sovereign debt, with underweight duration. "Unfavorable news on the economical, fiscal or political front could potentially lead to some further spread widening and increased volatility in the short term," he says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0745 GMT - U.K. inflation provided a bit of relief in August but price growth is set to accelerate in the coming months, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  chief U.K. economist \uc2 Paul Dales\uc2  says in a note. Inflation is set to peak at around 11% in October, when the energy price cap will increase to GBP2,500, but afterward the government price freeze means that utility price inflation will drop to zero in a year, he says. However, the upward momentum in services inflation--driven by a tight labor market and strong wage growth--hasn't abated and this is a cause of concern for the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 , Dales says. "That means the BOE will have to continue raising interest rates, from 1.75% now to 3.00% if not higher," he says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0745 GMT - Hong Kong's economic growth outlook is unlikely to get a boost from its relaxed Covid rules, given strong headwinds from China's weakened economy and prolonged border controls, say \uc2 Nomura\uc2  analysts in a note. Hong Kong's decision to shorten its quarantine period for inbound foreign travelers to three days may prove insufficient to boost business visits as many other countries have completely removed quarantine requirements, the Japanese investment bank says. "If Hong Kong scraps the mandatory quarantine outright in coming months, we believe this may improve the local outlook more materially," the analysts say. \uc2 Nomura\uc2  expects Hong Kong's economy to contract 1.0% in 2022. (yiwei.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0729 GMT - The dollar pulls back after rising in reaction to Tuesday's higher-than-expected U.S. inflation data that fuelled expectations for further aggressive interest rate rises by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . The dollar's reaction to the data was due to the fact Fed officials did "quite a lot over the past weeks to strengthen their credibility when it comes to fighting inflation," \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Thu Lan Nguyen says in a note. "From the market's point of view the stubbornly high inflation levels increase the likelihood of more pronounced Fed rate hikes." The DXY dollar index falls 0.1% to 109.754 after hitting a five-day high of 110.012 on Tuesday.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0724 GMT - The euro's scope to rise will remain limited while the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  maintains a restrictive policy stance and this is unlikely to change in light of Tuesday's stronger-than-forecast U.S. inflation data, Unicredit Research says. Evidence that U.S. consumer price inflation hasn't fallen much in August and core inflation rose "dashed EUR/USD recovery attempts," \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  analysts say in a note. That illustrates that factors lifting the euro--including \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials signalling further interest rate rises, positive Ukraine war developments reducing the dollar's safe-haven appeal, and a EU debate to address soaring energy prices--do no more than help the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain resilient rather than triggering a complete turnaround sentiment, they say. EUR/USD rises 0.1% to 0.9983. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0713 GMT - The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  should raise interest rates by another 50 basis points at its September 22 meeting after Wednesday's U.K. inflation data but the overall environment remains sterling negative, ING says. "Running a large current account deficit and having a large financial sector representation in the U.K. economy, slowing growth and weaker equity markets should leave sterling as an underperformer," ING analyst \uc2 Chris Turner\uc2  says in a note. GBP/USD could retest the 1.1406 lows and EUR/GBP could rise to the top of a 0.8650-0.8720 range, he says. GBP/USD falls 0.1% to 1.1485 and EUR/GBP rises 0.1% to 0.8677. Annual U.K. inflation fell to 9.9% in August from 10.1% in July but it remains well above the BOE's 2% target. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0707 GMT - The Malaysian ringgit may continue facing downward pressure with the u.S. Fed expected to continue its aggressive rate hikes due to high inflation, says Socio-Economic Research Centre Executive Director Lee Heng Guie. The weakening quantum of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will largely depend on when the U.S. interest rate will reach its peak and stay in the range of 3%-4%. The ringgit is year-to-date 7.95% weaker against the U.S. dollar. He also thinks the \uc2 Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will maintain its benchmark interest rate at 2.50% this year, before raising it to 3.0% in 1H 2023, to support the recovery of the local market. USD/MYR is up 0.4% at 4.53.(yingxian.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0656 GMT - The persistent nature of services inflation in the U.S. will be a headache for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  for some time, and it will make meeting the 2% inflation target difficult to achieve on a sustained basis, Gautam Khanna, head of multisector fixed income at Insight Investment, says in a note. Insight Investment sees "sticky" sectors keeping headline U.S. CPI around 6% at year-end, he says. U.S. CPI eased to 8.3% on year in August from 8.5% in July, but exceeded economists' consensus forecast of 8.0% in The Wall Street Journal's poll. Insight Investment still expects the terminal rate between 4% and 4.5%, and it doesn't foresee an interest-rate cutting cycle before 2024, he says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0654 GMT - Sterling falls after data showed U.K. inflation eased slightly in August, although it remain elevated. Annual inflation fell to 9.9% in August from 10.1% in July, in line with expectations in a WSJ poll of analysts. Core inflation, however, rose to an annual rate of 6.3% in August from 6.2% in July, as forecast. "The moderation in headline inflation, driven by a reduction in fuel prices, eases the pressure on the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to act more 'forcefully' than in August, while the core print...retains the need to hike rates further towards restrictive territory," Monex Europe \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 Simon Harvey\uc2  writes. GBP/USD falls to 1.1480 after the data, from 1.1508 beforehand. EUR/GBP rises to 0.8685, from 0.8675 previously. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 14, 2022 04:10 ET (08:10 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220914ei9e000j9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
