\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 4-\uc2 BOJ\uc2 's reported  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  rate check\b0\par\par\uc2 866 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par 08:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Sept 14 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has conducted a rate check in apparent preparation for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Nikkei newspaper reported on Wednesday, as policymakers stepped up warnings about sharp falls in the yen.\par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION:\par \par \uc2 The yen rose slightly from a near 24-year low against the dollar after the report, which cited unidentified sources, and was trading around 143.89 at 0520 GMT.\par \par \uc2 COMMENTS:\par \par \uc2 MASAYUKI KICHIKAWA, CHIEF MACRO STRATEGIST, SUMITOMO MITSUI DS ASSET MANAGEMENT, TOKYO:\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably considers recent moves in the yen rate as too sudden and too large."\par \par \uc2 "If the market continues to sell the yen, there is more pressure for the MOF and \uc2 BOJ\uc2  to communicate to the market that the recent move has been too fast."\par \par \uc2 "Without consent from the U.S. side, I don't think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I don't think U.S. authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but I do think that the Japanese side has talked to their U.S. counterparts, and the U.S. side is not opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities."\par \par \uc2 "Of course in the short term \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could work, if the sense (is) that the market is skewed to selling yen, but in order to change the trend, we need to see a change in fundamentals."\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WONG, \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  STRATEGIST, \uc2 OCBC\uc2 , SINGAPORE\par \par \uc2 "Verbal jawboning may help to slow the pace of yen depreciation but is not likely to alter the trend unless USD and UST yields decisively turn lower or BoJ changes or tweaks its policy."\par \par \uc2 "The effectiveness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be maximised if it's a coordinated effort and if the USD uptrend is not so strong."\par \par \uc2 MASAMICHI ADACHI, CHIEF ECONOMIST, \uc2 UBS SECURITIES\uc2 , TOKYO\par \par \uc2 On possibility of \uc2 BOJ\uc2  action, "No. Simple answer is no.\par \par \uc2 "What Kuroda mentioned in the past is that the weaker yen is not bad for the economy. And what he always emphasised \u8212\'20\'14 actually, that's the same as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 saying that they are not commenting on the level, they're commenting on the speed of the depreciation. And the pace of the yen depreciation or a strong US dollar is probably speculative, that's what they've all mentioned.\par \par \uc2 "So the Deputy Minister of Finance, who is in charge of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, is... stating that he'll (take) necessary measures, meaning \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So I think if the yen goes to depreciate (further) again, (over) next two weeks, I think I'm not going to be surprised if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, meaning buying the yen and selling the US dollar held in foreign reserves."\par \par \uc2 KOYA MIYAMAE, SENIOR ECONOMIST, \uc2 SMBC NIKKO SECURITIES\uc2 , TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "Government officials have said they look at the magnitude of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, rather than the level of exchange rates, and the (recent) big move likely prompted the reaction."\par \par \uc2 "A solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can only impact the market near-term. It\u8217\'20\'19s not that it has no impact, but without a coordinated action, the effect doesn\u8217\'20\'19t last long."\par \par \uc2 MASAFUMI YAMAMOTO, CHIEF \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  STRATEGIST, \uc2 MIZUHO SECURITIES\uc2 , TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "(If dollar/yen rises above 145) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may become more likely. It's unlikely.... \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have a desirable effect so I think it's better not to do it. The United States is expected to cut interest rates next year so it's just a matter of waiting until that time. If the United States moves to cutting interest rates, the yen will strengthen even without the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  doing anything."\par \par \uc2 "Currently, the dollar is becoming stronger and the yen weakening due to the big interest rate differentials between the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so it's hard (for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to be effective. That's why I think it's better to wait."\par \par \uc2 "If the dollar rises above 145 yen, the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will rise to about 60% from 10% to 20% before, rather than becoming 100%."\par \par \uc2 ROB CARNELL, HEAD OF RESEARCH, ASIA-PACIFIC, ING, SINGAPORE:\par \par \uc2 On possibility of \uc2 BOJ\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "Never say never. They have been stepping up the rhetoric lately. But I would be cautious about the inevitability of their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a signatory to the G20 and they have got policies about not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 "It is also difficult... unless they stop buying JGBs, unless they raise their cash rate, it just doesn't seem credible for them to be going to the G20 to say the yen is too weak. And \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  might at best win them a couple of hours of respite before the market does what it wants to again."\par \par \uc2 TAKESHI MINAMI, CHIEF ECONOMIST, NORINCHUKIN RESEARCH INSTITUTE, TOKYO:\par \par \uc2 "It's not clear whether the MOF will actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. But by conducting rate checks, it's signalling readiness to step in to keep sharp yen moves in check."\par \par \uc2 "My feeling is that the MOF won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this stage and leave it at verbal warnings. There's still a week before the Fed's rate-setting meeting. I don't think markets believe the MOF will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at current dollar/yen levels."\par \par \uc2 (Compiled by Ana Nicolaci da Costa) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-14T07:31:18.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220914ei9e00rgx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
