\par\par \b \uc2 TIMELINE-History of  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  markets\b0\par\par\uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par 07:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 14 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has conducted a rate check in apparent preparation for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Nikkei website reported on Wednesday, citing unidentified sources, as policymakers stepped up warnings about sharp falls in the yen.\par \par \uc2 It is more than a decade since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and more than two decades since it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which it last did during the Asian financial crisis of 1997 to 1998.\par \par \uc2 Here is a timeline of selected moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Sept. 7, 2022 - Top government spokesman Hirokazu Matsuno expresses concern about "rapid, one-sided" moves seen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after the yen weakens beyond 143 per dollar. He says the government would like to take "necessary steps" if such movements continue. It is the strongest in a series of such official comments made over several months.\par \par \uc2 June 10, 2022 - Japan's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue a rare joint statement saying they are concerned by recent sharp falls in the yen after it weakens beyond 134 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Aug and Oct - 2011 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb gains that officials fear could derail recovery from an economic slump triggered by a massive earthquake and tsunami on March 11, 2011.\par \par \uc2 March 18, 2011 - \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  (G7) nations jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen strength when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  spikes to a record high in the aftermath of the earthquake on speculation that Japanese firms would repatriate foreign assets to pay for reconstruction.\par \par \uc2 Sept. 15, 2010 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time in six years, selling yen to stem a rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the dollar hits a 15-year-low at 82.87 yen.\par \par \uc2 March 2004 - A 15-month campaign to curb the yen's rise comes to an end after Japan has spent 35 trillion yen, or more than $300 billion, on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 May-June, 2002 - The \uc2 BOJ\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen, often supported by the U.S. Federal Reserve and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 ECB\uc2 ). The yen continues to gain.\par \par \uc2 Sept 2001 - The \uc2 BOJ\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen after the Sept. 11 attacks in the United States. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  and New York Federal Reserve both operate on behalf of the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 January 1999 to April 2000 - The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  sells yen at least 18 times, including once via the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and once via the \uc2 ECB\uc2 , due to worries the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength will choke off an economic recovery. The yen continues to strengthen.\par \par \uc2 1997 - 1998 - The Asian financial crisis sees the yen weaken, reaching nearly 148 per dollar in August 1998, even after U.S. authorities join the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  to buy yen.\par \par \uc2 April 1994 - August 1995 - The dollar sinks to a record low against the German mark and a post-war low against the yen. The United States \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly, often with Japanese and European central banks, to prop up the greenback.\par \par \uc2 1993 - The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  sells yen through much of the year to curb its strength.\par \par \uc2 1991 - 1992 - The \uc2 BOJ\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen, selling U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 1988 - On Jan. 4, the dollar falls to 120.45 yen, at that time a post World War Two low, in Tokyo trade. The \uc2 BOJ\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars and sell yen.\par \par \uc2 1987 - In February, six of the G7 nations sign the Louvre Accord, which aims to stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and halt the dollar's broad decline.\par \par \uc2 1985 - The Group of Five industrial nations, the predecessor to the G7, sign the Plaza Accord, in which they agree that the dollar is overvalued and that they will move to weaken it.\par \par \uc2 1973 - Japanese monetary authorities decide to let the yen float freely against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 (Compiled by Tom Westbrook and Daniel Leussink; Editing by Bradley Perrett) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-14T06:17:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220914ei9e00mdl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
