\par\par \b \uc2 Weakening Yen Stokes Concerns About Japan's Reaction -- Talking Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paulo Trevisani \par \uc2 867 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2022\par 21:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 A declining yen is raising concerns about possible actions to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with potential side effects to global markets. Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sticking to its ultra-easy monetary policy, one widely blamed for sending the yen to the lowest level in decades versus the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 With the yen trading at low levels not seen since 1998, the chatter about a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is heating up given \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's standing in the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "Japan's net international investment position gives it Godzilla-like powers in financial markets," said \uc2 Karl Schamotta\uc2 , chief market strategist at Corpay, a payments firm owned by \uc2 Fleetcor Technologies Inc\uc2 . "Even the slightest shift in policy could be sufficient to shake \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  worldwide."\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan\uc2  has kept its policy rates in negative territory even as its peers raise rates aggressively. The bank will meet next week and no meaningful rate increases are expected.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, higher-than-expected U.S. inflation data released Tuesday increased the odds of even tighter monetary moves by the Fed, further boosting the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The BoJ also has a 0.25% target on yields of Japan's 10-year government bonds, a level unlikely to attract capital flows when the U.S. equivalent government debt is paying 3.44%.\par \par \uc2 Some investors think that lifting the yield cap is the next logical move for the BoJ.\par \par \uc2 "To me, that would be the sensible step to take, to loosen up that cap," said Douglas Porter, chief economist at \uc2 BMO Capital Markets\uc2 . "I'm thinking that maybe [the target] has lived its usefulness."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Porter says the BoJ would be pressured to act if the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate reaches 145 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The yen breached the 140 mark earlier this month for the first time since 1998 and was recently trading at 144.50 per dollar, down 25% this year.\par \par \uc2 "If the slide in the yen got too bad, everybody thinks they would lift that cap on JGBs, in effect raising rates to try to bolster the yen a little bit," said Bruce Clark, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Informa Global Markets.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Clark says the possible attempt to shore up the yen could divert capital flows, leading to higher rates in other countries.\par \par \uc2 "The JGB market has served as a yield anchor for global bond markets for many many years," he said. "There's just this bubbling volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets that could really sort of explode to new levels."\par \par \uc2 BoJ\uc2 's governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  has said the bank doesn't target \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in its policy making. Mr. Kuroda has also acknowledged that \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility can destabilize corporate business plans and heighten uncertainty, an assertion that lifted the yen on September 9 but wasn't followed up by any policy change.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kuroda's comments last week "suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have taken things too far, with the yen's decline outpacing any deterioration in underlying fundamentals," Mr. Schamotta said, adding he doesn't expect the BoJ to reverse course for now.\par \par \uc2 "Weak domestic wage pressures paired with low levels of core inflation suggest the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's policy settings remain appropriate," he said.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, Japan's $5 trillion economy could be able to withstand the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  whirlwind, analysts say, while the country's $1.3 trillion in foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves gives the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  enough muscle to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In fact, some pundits believe the yen is likely to bounce back.\par \par \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  economists see the yen strengthening to 125 per dollar by the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 BofA\uc2  analysts also said in a recent analyst report that the current \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  level could prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, selling dollars to strengthen the yen.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, inflation is rising in the country after years of stagnant prices, but at a 2.4% annual rate in July it remains relatively low compared to the U.S.' 8.5% annual pace recorded that month.\par \par \uc2 The BoJ, which spent the years prior to Covid-19 trying to fuel inflation in an aging nation with a sluggish economy, has said current price increases are mostly due to fuel and food imports and likely to slow down without higher interest rates that could hamper economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The BoJ has kept rates near or below zero since 2008. It currently applies a negative policy rate of 0.1% on balances held by financial institutions at the bank.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, the \uc2 BoJ\uc2  began buying 10-year treasury notes, or JGBs, in order to keep their yields low. In April, the bank raised the target to 0.25% from closer to zero.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, rising interest rates around the developed world threaten to further weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 "The market is reluctant to push the greenback above JPY145, but I think it will do so," said \uc2 Marc Chandler\uc2 , managing director of Bannockburn Global \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 Write to Paulo Trevisani at paulo.trevisani@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 13, 2022 15:03 ET (19:03 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220913ei9d002sh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
