\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-\uc2 BOJ\uc2  is nowhere near shifting monetary policy to support yen\b0\par\par\uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2022\par 01:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story from Monday with no changes)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 12 (Reuters) - The yen may be near 24-year lows, but Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not even close to trying to support it with higher interest rates.\par \par \uc2 That is the message from three sources familiar with the thinking of the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ), and it was strongly implied by the country's top \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat last week and indeed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  in July.\par \par \uc2 The government - especially the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) - has repeatedly and strongly expressed dissatisfaction with this year's falls in the yen, which on Sept. 7 dropped as far as 144.990 per dollar, down 30% since the end of 2021.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is independent and by law obliged to attend to inflation and the state of the economy, not the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Its support for the weak economy with ultra-low interest rates is the main factor behind the yen's weakness, since other central banks, notably the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 , are briskly tightening monetary policy, making their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive as destinations for capital.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  has no intention of raising interest rates or tweaking its dovish policy guidance to prop up the yen, the sources said.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  won't directly target \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in guiding policy," said one of them. "It looks at yen moves in the context of how they affect the economy and prices."\par \par \uc2 "Current economic conditions don't justify tweaking ultra-loose policy," that person said, expressing a view echoed by the other two sources.\par \par \uc2 Official statements are in fact consistent with that.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  has joined the finance ministry in warning against sharp falls in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But Kuroda said in July, "It's hard to believe that just by raising rates somewhat, you can stop the yen's decline."\par \par \uc2 That view is still widely shared in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sources said.\par \par \uc2 When the MOF expresses its displeasure with the yen's falls, it is said to be jawboning - dropping a hint that it may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This is intended to make traders cautious in selling the yen.\par \par \uc2 Last week's comments by Masato Kanda, a MOF official who serves as Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat, emphasized the divergence between the government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Both were "extremely worried" about recent rapid yen moves, Kanda said.\par \par \uc2 But he declined to comment on \uc2 BOJ\uc2  policy and said "the government" - instead of "government and the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 " - was ready to use all available tools to battle excessive yen declines.\par \par \uc2 "Each of us has our own mandate. That's why I carefully used 'government' as the subject at times and 'government and \uc2 BOJ\uc2 ' at other times," Kanda told reporters.\par \par \uc2 He was speaking after a meeting between the ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DOVISH POLICY GUIDANCE\par \par \uc2 Japan's economic weakness gives the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  little reason to withdraw the monetary stimulus that is undermining the yen. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to maintain ultra-low interest rates and dovish policy guidance at its Sept. 21 and 22 meeting.\par \par \uc2 As for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the most it can do is to maintain, or perhaps strengthen, a warning it inserted in its policy statement in June. It said then that it would "closely watch financial and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market developments, as well as the impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy and prices."\par \par \uc2 That leaves possible MOF market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the main concern for investors betting against the yen. Yet even that looks improbable, considering that Tokyo would have difficulty getting the necessary consent from other members of the G7 group of large economies.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's dovish policy stance will come under the spotlight as European and U.S. central banks hike rates," said Masahiro Ichikawa, chief market strategist at Sumitomo Mitsui DS Asset Management. "The weak-yen trend will continue."\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's focus on inflation and the economy does not mean it would never act in response to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, the sources said.\par \par \uc2 It could feel compelled to respond in the event of a freefall that became so extreme that the economy and price stability were threatened, they said.\par \par \uc2 That, however, is not the current situation. (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Editing by Bradley Perrett)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-15T23:10:11.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220912ei9c03oy5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
