\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia stocks bounce, dollar cautious before inflation test\b0\par\par\uc2 860 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2022\par 07:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Asian stock markets : {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2zpUAr4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \uc2 * Nikkei rises 1.1%, S&P 500 futures flat\par \par \uc2 * Japanese officials protest 'excessive' yen weakness\par \par \uc2 * Sources say \uc2 ECB\uc2  may lift rates to around 2%\par \par \uc2 By Wayne Cole\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Sept 12 (Reuters) - Asian share markets rallied on Monday on hopes a key reading on U.S. inflation will show some cooling, while the U.S. dollar was restrained by the risk of higher European interest rates and Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Holidays in China and South Korea made for slow trading, while traders were unsure what implications Ukraine's surprising success against Russian forces might have.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan added 0.5%, having bounced modestly from a two-year low hit last week. Japan's Nikkei added another 1.1%, after rallying 2% last week.\par \par \uc2 Chinese blue chips firmed 1.3% ahead of retail and industry data due later in the week that may show some improvement in August after a disappointing July.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street struggled to extend Friday's bounce with S&P 500 futures and Nasdaq futures flat. EUROSTOXX 50 futures gained 0.6% and FTSE futures 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 Bulls are hoping Tuesday's reading on U.S. consumer prices will hint at a peak for inflation as falling petrol prices are seen pulling down the headline index by 0.1%, according to a Reuters poll.\par \par \uc2 The core is forecast to rise 0.3%, though some analysts see a chance of a softer report.\par \par \uc2 "Arguably, with the economy having contracted through the first half, and household discretionary spending capacity under significant pressure, we are due a modest downside surprise," said economists at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "As such, we forecast +0.2% for core and -0.2% for headline," they added. "If achieved though, it should not be assumed that October and beyond will see repeats, with volatility likely to persist."\par \par \uc2 A soft number might revive speculation the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will only hike by 50 basis points this month, though it would likely have to be very weak to have a real impact given how stridently hawkish policymakers have been recently.\par \par \uc2 The market currently implies an 88% chance the Fed will hike by 75 basis points.\par \par \uc2 BofA\uc2  global economist \uc2 Ethan Harris\uc2  fears that by focusing on actual inflation to determine when to stop, central banks may go too far. The bank has lifted its target for the federal funds rate to a range of 4.0-4.25%, with a 75bp hike in September and smaller rises thereafter.\par \par \uc2 "For investors, this means more pressure on interest rates, more weakness in risk assets and further upside for the super-strong dollar," said Harris.\par \par \uc2 "In our view, these trends only turn when markets price the full fury of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hikes and we are not quite there yet."\par \par \uc2 DOLLAR NOT DONE YET\par \par \uc2 For now, the dollar has run into some profit taking from a market that is very long the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a month of sustained gains.\par \par \uc2 So rapidly has the dollar risen on the yen that Japanese authorities are becoming increasingly vocal in protesting their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline, sparking speculation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and putting pressure on the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to moderate its policy of yield curve control.\par \par \uc2 Japan's government must take steps as needed to counter excessive declines in the yen, a senior government official said on Sunday, after it hit its weakest level against the dollar in 24 years.\par \par \uc2 Yet after an early dip, the dollar soon rallied to be up 0.4% at 143.14 yen, though still off last week's top of 144.99.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index stood at 108.770, having reached as high as 110.790 last week.\par \par \uc2 The euro nudged up 0.4% to $1.0080, and away from its recent trough of $0.9865.\par \par \uc2 It was helped in part by a Reuters report that \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers see a growing risk that they will have to raise their key interest rate to 2% or more to curb record-high inflation despite a likely recession.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at ANZ noted the dollar over the past month was up roughly 9% against the euro and the Chinese yuan, 12% against the British pound and 19% against the yen.\par \par \uc2 "The rampant USD is causing strain in developing countries, which are finding imports priced in USD more expensive," they said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "With Fed speakers using every opportunity to hammer home a hawkish message and quantitative tightening looming, the USD is not about to dramatically turn."\par \par \uc2 The ascent of the dollar combined with high bond yields has been a drag for gold, which was hovering at $1,713 an ounce after hitting a low of $1,690 last week.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices have also been trending lower amid concerns about a global economic slowdown, though cuts to supply did prompt a 4% bounce on Friday.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Brent was down $1.29 at $91.55, while U.S. crude shed $1.28 to $85.51 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Wayne Cole; Editing by Himani Sarkar and Ana Nicolaci da Costa) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-12T06:27:34.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220912ei9c00gqt\par }\page {\par\f