te with authorities in other countries on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 Steer highlights USD/JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk \u8226\'20\'22 1.1646 is highest level for GBP/USD since Aug 31 (1.1693 was high that day) \u8226\'20\'22 1.1407 was 37-year low Wednesday (USD/JPY hit 24-year high by 145 that day) AUD/USD climbs to Sept high as greenback longs lighten \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD climbs to 0.6877, highest since Aug 31, as greenback longs lightened \u8226\'20\'22 Position adjustments influenced by Japanese jawboning on yen \u8226\'20\'22 0.6699 was eight-week low for AUD/USD earlier this week \u8226\'20\'22 AUD supported by higher metals prices: iron ore up to two-week highs \u8226\'20\'22 Offers may emerge around 0.69 if AUD/USD rise extends (0.6904 = Aug 31 high) Global tightening will fuel risk aversion in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Central Banks are not just tightening monetary policy they are doing so radically with the size of interest rate hikes going far beyond anything envisaged in financial markets a few months ago. The rapid reduction in liquidity that results from this abrupt and big change is fuel for greater risk aversion that weighs on stocks and commodities and drives cash towards \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are deemed safer. What exacerbates the risk of a very negative period for financial markets is the fact that rallies for risky assets in the last two years were fuelled by stimulus. The rises in prices of stocks and commodities were built on shaky foundations of bad news, such as COVID-19. The rallies in these higher risk assets would not have occurred without stimulus that was triggered by the pandemic. Now, a rush to tighten monetary policy to combat inflation that resulted from the commodity price surge may unwind these gains. There is great scope for a reversal of the trends that arose from stimulus, with notoriously illiquid emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  vulnerable to big falls now most central banks - not just the Federal Reserve - are rushing to withdraw liquidity. Crypto \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which so many remain invested, are also at great risk. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3QAnrx8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3L00y50"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  USD to feel breeze if Japan butterfly flutters \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  wings The U.S. dollar may feel a chill wind if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to shore up the weak yen, courtesy of the position adjustments this could trigger. The risk of \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  ordering the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to sell the dollar against the yen is already having an impact on the greenback, with the USD index falling to a September low of 108.35 on Friday -- two days after scaling a 20-year peak of 110.79. Japan chief cabinet secretary \uc2 Hirokazu Matsuno\uc2  on Friday said the Japanese government will closely coordinate with the BoJ and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities in other countries on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Matsuno's steer came after BoJ Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  joined a chorus of Japanese policymakers lobbing verbal warnings over the yen's recent sharp drop to a 24-year low against the dollar. The Japanese chatter has prompted some profit-taking on USD long positions, to the benefit of other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the euro, pound and Australian dollar, as well as the yen. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3L4Vigv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \p