\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan's top  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  diplomat warns of action to stem 'speculative' yen moves\b0\par\par\uc2 748 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2022\par 12:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Yen's recent falls not justified by fundamentals - MOF's Kanda\par \par \uc2 * Govt won't rule out any options - Kanda\par \par \uc2 * Gov't-\uc2 BOJ\uc2  meeting produces no written statement\par \par \uc2 * Escalated verbal warnings leave analysts unimpressed (Adds Kanda's comment, context on dearth of tools)\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto and Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 8 (Reuters) - Japan is ready to take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and will not rule out any options to address "clearly excessive volatility" seen in the yen, the country's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat said on Thursday, issuing the strongest warning to date on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plunge to 24-year lows.\par \par \uc2 "Volatility is recently heightening in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Especially in the past few days, we've seen one-sided, rapid yen declines driven by speculative moves. It's clearly a move that can be described as excess volatility," vice finance minister for international affairs Masato Kanda told reporters.\par \par \uc2 "The yen's recent rapid moves cannot be justified by fundamentals," he said, adding that the government and the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  were "extremely worried" about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide.\par \par \uc2 "If such moves continue, the government is ready to take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and won't rule out any measures," he said.\par \par \uc2 When asked whether yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a possibility, Kanda said: "As I said, we won't rule out any options."\par \par \uc2 Kanda made the remarks after attending a meeting of officials from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) and the Financial Services Agency (FSA) on Thursday to discuss global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Such meetings are held on an ad hoc basis, usually to signal to markets the government's concern over rapid exchange rate moves. No written statement was issued after the meeting.\par \par \uc2 "We'll look at market moves on a daily basis and if we think something is not right, we'll take appropriate action," Kanda said, when asked how long volatility needed to continue for Tokyo to consider acting.\par \par \uc2 Kanda pointed to the fact that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shed 5 yen against the dollar in just two days on Sept. 6-7, and lost about 25% versus the dollar since March.\par \par \uc2 The dollar dipped as low as 143.43 yen after Kanda's remarks before bouncing back to around 144 yen. It last traded around 144.02 yen, off Wednesday's 24-year high of 144.99 yen.\par \par \uc2 "Kanda's language didn't sound as if Tokyo is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately, which is why the dollar moved back above 144 yen," said Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Once a welcome boost for exporters, the yen's weakness is becoming a headache for Japanese policymakers because it is driving up the cost of already expensive fuel and raw materials imports, threatening to stunt its economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Authorities have turned up their rhetoric over the yen's slide, but have failed to slow its decline as investors continued to focus on the policy divergence between the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's aggressive interest rate hikes and the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's resolve to maintain ultra-loose monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Despite the escalating rhetoric, Japanese policymakers have few options to moderate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's drop, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo would need informal consent from its G7 counterparts to step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the yen. Getting consent would probably be difficult, given Washington's aversion to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say.\par \par \uc2 "Escalating verbal warnings won't have much effect unless \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes specific action signalling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as conducting rate checks in the market," said Atsushi Takeda, chief economist at Itochu Economic Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  has also ruled out the chance of raising interest rates to combat the yen's slide, stressing the bank's resolve to keep monetary policy ultra-loose until inflation stably hits his 2% target accompanied by wage growth.\par \par \uc2 Yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is historically rare in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the authorities have traditionally focused on combatting sharp yen gains that hurt exports.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem the yen's strength in 2011 when a devastating earthquake and tsunami struck the country's north-eastern areas and caused the Fukushima nuclear crisis, triggering a flight to safety. (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto, Leika Kihara, Daniel Leussink and Kantaro Komiya Editing by Chang-Ran Kim, Peter Graff, Tomasz Janowski and Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-8T11:33:26.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220908ei9801fxh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
