\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 British pound falls against the dollar; hits 37-year low\b0\par\par\uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK. -The dollar hit a 24-year high against the yen and a 37-year high against the British pound on Wednesday as Japan's monetary policy and Europe' s economic woes contrasted with a relatively strong U.S. economy and a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  determined to bring inflation down to its 2% target.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soared to 144.99 yen for the first time since August 1998. It is now within striking distance of its 1998 high of 147.43. The dollar was last up 0.9% at 144.09 yen.\par \par \uc2 Against sterling, the greenback hit $1.1407, the lowest since 1985, and was last down 0.1% at $1.1509.\par \par \uc2 Increasingly, it is becoming a growth story and really a crisis story. We have China continuing with a zero covid policy, and if anything continuing to double down by confining more cities," said Erik Nelson, macro strategist, \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Europe and the U.K. look headed for a rough couple of months, with recession very likely for both economies. The U.S., on the other hand, seems to be holding up," he added.\par \par \uc2 The euro fell below 99 cents on Wednesday, after dropping to $0.9864 on Tuesday, its lowest since October 2002. Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last up 0.8 percent at $0.9985.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen as likely to raise interest rates by 75 basis points on Thursday, but these expectations have done little to support the euro, given the ailing European economy and Russia's decision to keep the key Nord Stream 1 gas pipeline closed indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, a report on Tuesday showed that the U.S. services sector unexpectedly rebounded last month, reinforcing the view that the economy is not in recession.\par \par \uc2 At current dollar/yen levels, there is also speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 jm\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220908ei97005a0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
