\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Dollar rises to 24-year high against the yen and 37-year high against the pound\b0\par\par\uc2 368 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 By Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sep 7 (Reuters) - The dollar hit a 24-year high against the yen and a 37-year high against sterling on Wednesday as Japan's monetary policy and Europe's economic woes contrasted with a relatively strong U.S. economy and a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  determined to bring inflation down to its 2 percent target.\par \par \uc2 * The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soared to 144.99 yen for the first time since August 1998. It is now within striking distance of its 1998 high of 147.43. The dollar was last up 0.9% at 144.09 yen.\par \par \uc2 * Against sterling, the greenback hit $1.1407, the lowest since 1985, and was last down 0.1% at $1.1509.\par \par \uc2 * "Increasingly, it's becoming a growth story and really a crisis story. We have China continuing with a zero COVID policy, and if anything continuing to double down by confining more cities," said Erik Nelson, macro strategist, \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * "Europe and the U.K. look like they are headed for a rough couple of months, with a recession very likely for both economies. The U.S., on the other hand, seems to be holding up," he added.\par \par \uc2 * The euro fell below 99 cents on Wednesday, after dropping to $0.9864 on Tuesday, its lowest since October 2002. Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last up 0.8% at $0.9985.\par \par \uc2 * The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen as likely to raise interest rates by 75 basis points (bps) on Thursday, but these expectations have done little to support the euro, given the ailing European economy and Russia's decision to keep the major Nord Stream 1 pipeline closed indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 * In contrast, a report on Tuesday showed that the U.S. services sector unexpectedly rebounded last month, reinforcing the view that the economy is not in recession.\par \par \uc2 * At current dollar/yen levels, there is also speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kevin Buckland in Tokyo, Tom Westbrook in Singapore and Alun John in London; Additional reporting by Daiki Iga; Edited in Spanish by Manuel Far\u237\'00\'EDas)\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220907ei9700iuu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
