\par\par \b \uc2 Resilient U.S. dollar soars to 24-year high vs yen, 37-year peak vs sterling\b0\par\par\uc2 971 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2022\par 21:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Reuters) -The dollar surged to a 24-year peak against the yen and a 37-year high versus sterling on Wednesday, as Japan's dovish monetary policy and Europe's economic problems contrasted with a relatively stronger U.S. economy and a hawkish \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  determined to bring down inflation to its 2% inflation target.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soared as high as 144.99 yen, hitting the level for the first time since August 1998. It is now within a large leap of its 1998 high of 147.43. The dollar was last up 0.9% at 144.09 yen.\par \par \uc2 Against sterling, the greenback hit $1.1407, the lowest since 1985 and last down 0.1% at $1.1509.\par \par \uc2 "Increasingly, it is becoming a growth story and really a crisis story. We have China continuing to have a COVID-zero policy, and if anything continuing to double down, locking down more cities," said Erik Nelson, macro strategist, at \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  in New York City.\par \par \uc2 "Europe and the UK look like they are headed for a challenging couple of months, with recession very likely for both economies. The U.S., on the other hand, looks resilient," he added.\par \par \uc2 The euro fell below 99 cents on Wednesday after dipping as low as $0.9864 on Tuesday, its lowest since October 2002. Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last up 0.8% at US$0.9985.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen likely to deliver a massive 75 basis-point (bp) rate hike on Thursday, but these expectations have done little to support the euro given a battered European economy and Russia's decision to keep the key Nord Stream 1 gas pipeline shut indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, a report overnight showed the U.S. services industry unexpectedly picked up last month, underpinning the view that the economy is not in recession.\par \par \uc2 Also on Wednesday, the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  raised interest rates by 75 bps to a 14-year high on Wednesday, as expected, and said the policy rate would need to go even higher as it battles raging inflation.\par \par \uc2 Despite the \uc2 BoC\uc2  rate hike, the U.S. dollar was little changed against the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at C$1.3141.\par \par \uc2 Moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were most dramatic on the yen, whose tumble, even by its own recent standards, has been precipitous. Since the beginning of August, the yen has plummeted by 10.1% against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 At current dollar/yen levels, speculation is also growing that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Every time the yen weakens, it asks the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  (\uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 ) a question, as to whether now is the time to abandon YCC (yield curve control)?," wrote \uc2 Alan Ruskin\uc2 , macro strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in its latest research note.\par \par \uc2 "When nothing more than verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at hand, the market can read from the lip service that the authorities are still some way from tightening monetary policy."\par \par \uc2 Japan's Chief Cabinet Secretary \uc2 Hirokazu Matsuno\uc2  told a news briefing that the administration would like to take necessary steps if "rapid, one-sided" moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets continue, ratcheting up the rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 However, many analysts see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as difficult given that global central banks are more focused on inflation rather than exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere China's yuan sank to a two-year trough, closing in on the 7-per-dollar mark despite steps by authorities to stem its decline. The onshore yuan weakened to a low of 6.9808, the lowest since August 2020, and the offshore yuan was even closer to the key level, falling as low as 6.997 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Cryptocurrency bitcoin earlier slumped to the lowest since June 19 at $18,540, extending a 5% tumble from Tuesday. It was last up 1.6% at $19,078.\par \par \uc2 ========================================================\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 3:14PM (1914 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Description RIC Last U.S. Close Pct Change YTD Pct High Bid Low Bid\par \par \uc2 Previous Change\par \par \uc2 Session\par \par \uc2 Dollar index 109.7300 110.3400 -0.55% 14.705% +110.7900 +109.6300\par \par \uc2 Euro/Dollar $0.9993 $0.9907 +0.87% -12.09% +$1.0003 +$0.9875\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Yen 144.0550 142.7900 +0.90% +25.15% +144.9850 +142.7500\par \par \uc2 Euro/Yen 143.95 141.41 +1.80% +10.46% +144.0000 +141.3800\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Swiss 0.9778 0.9845 -0.60% +7.28% +0.9869 +0.9772\par \par \uc2 Sterling/Dollar $1.1511 $1.1517 -0.06% -14.89% +$1.1522 +$1.1407\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Canadian 1.3135 1.3148 -0.05% +3.93% +1.3209 +1.3130\par \par \uc2 Aussie/Dollar $0.6757 $0.6732 +0.34% -7.07% +$0.6765 +$0.6699\par \par \uc2 Euro/Swiss 0.9771 0.9749 +0.23% -5.77% +0.9781 +0.9729\par \par \uc2 Euro/Sterling 0.8679 0.8597 +0.95% +3.32% +0.8688 +0.8596\par \par \uc2 NZ $0.6064 $0.6041 +0.37% -11.41% +$0.6070 +$0.5997\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Dollar\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Norway 10.0145 10.0330 +0.02% +13.92% +10.0840 +9.9905\par \par \uc2 Euro/Norway 10.0073 9.9262 +0.82% -0.06% +10.0256 +9.9061\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Sweden 10.7158 10.7886 +0.20% +18.83% +10.8426 +10.6967\par \par \uc2 Euro/Sweden 10.7088 10.6877 +0.20%