\par\par \b \uc2 MORNING BID-Yen freefall\b0\par\par\uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2022\par 12:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A look at the day ahead in U.S. and global markets from Mike Dolan.\par \par \uc2 Bad things tend to happen in world markets when Japan's yen registers wild swings and its latest freefall against the supercharged U.S. dollar leaves many feeling uneasy.\par \par \uc2 With U.S. economic soundings still strong enough to keep the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  talking tough on its inflation fight, the dollar continues to climb against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  where recession worries are more intense or - in the case of Japan - there's no appetite to match monetary tightening elsewhere in the G7.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Canada is expected to match recent Fed rate rises with a 75 basis point (bp) hike of its own later on Wednesday. The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also likely see that on Thursday and the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is shaping for a similar move next week - all before the Fed could pull the 75 bp trigger again later this month.\par \par \uc2 Japan stands alone in standing pat. Dollar/yen, now up more than 31% over 12 months, soared again over the past 24 hours to another 24 year high above 144, drawing official warnings in Tokyo about "rapid, one-sided" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2 But for all the angst there appears to be little sign of any concrete action to halt the yen slide, now homing in on 1998's trough above 147 per dollar. Former Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Hiroshi Watanabe told Reuters on Wednesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  or change monetary policy in response as they would be ineffective in countering broad dollar gains.\par \par \uc2 And as a barrage of Fed speakers hits the circuit again on Wednesday, including Vice Chair \uc2 Lael Brainard\uc2 , there appears to be no change in tack from the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  either.\par \par \uc2 Richmond Fed\uc2  President \uc2 Thomas Barkin\uc2  told the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  more rate hikes were in store, positive real interest rates were a target and the Fed needed to keep rates high until it was convinced inflation was durably subsiding.\par \par \uc2 However resilient U.S. service sector business readings appeared last month, the world's second largest economy is spluttering and China's yuan is also under the cosh against the dollar - hitting another 2-year low on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Despite the yuan's recent weakness, China's export growth dropped sharply in August and well below forecasts - with heatwaves, drought and COVID lockdowns all having an impact. While imports of crude oil and other commodities fell, its overall trade surplus narrowed.\par \par \uc2 With \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  chief executive \uc2 Christian Sewing\uc2  warning a German recession was now inevitable due to the Europe-wide energy crunch, the demand picture outside the United States is darkening by the day.\par \par \uc2 The only silver lining from global recession fears is that crude oil prices continue to fall back and dropped more than $1 at one point on Wednesday to their lowest since before Russia invaded Ukraine in February.\par \par \uc2 U.S. 2-year inflation expectations fell as a result, with 2-year inflation 'breakevens' from the index-linked Treasury market dropping to their lowest since February 2021.\par \par \uc2 Global stock markets were subdued. U.S. futures were mostly flat and \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields off recent highs.\par \par \uc2 Bitcoin, now down almost 60% so far this year, fell below $19,000 for the first time since July.\par \par \uc2 Key developments that should provide more direction to U.S. markets later on Wednesday: * \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's policy interest rate announcement * \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  policymakers testify in parliament * Fed Vice Chair \uc2 Lael Brainard\uc2 ; \uc2 Richmond Fed\uc2  President \uc2 Thomas Barkin\uc2 ; \uc2 Cleveland Fed\uc2  President \uc2 Loretta Mester\uc2 ; and Federal Reserve Vice Chair for Supervision Michael Barr all speak * Fed issues Beige Book of economic condition\par \par \uc2 (By Mike Dolan, editing by Mark Pottermike.dolan@thomsonreuters.com. Twitter: @reutersMikeD) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-7T11:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220907ei9701v4v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
