\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan concerned about 'rapid, one-sided' moves in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  market\b0\par\par\uc2 576 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2022\par 09:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds latest finmin response in paragraph 6)\par \par \uc2 By Kantaro Komiya and Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 7 (Reuters) - Japan's government wants to act if "rapid, one-sided" moves seen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market recently continue, the country's top government spokesperson warned on Wednesday as the yen slid to a fresh 24-year low.\par \par \uc2 The comments marked officials' latest verbal warning against the fall of the yen, which weakened beyond 144 yen per dollar after slumping about 1.5% on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "I'm concerned about rapid, one-sided moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market recently," Chief Cabinet Secretary \uc2 Hirokazu Matsuno\uc2  told reporters at a news briefing, using stronger language about the yen's fall than officials' remarks earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 The government "would like to take necessary steps if such movements continue," he said, adding that sharp fluctuations were not desirable. He made the comments before the yen hit a fresh low.\par \par \uc2 Such official remarks, called jawboning in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, are intended to make traders cautious by implying the authorities may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When asked later on Wednesday whether a government response may include \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  declined to comment, keeping mum on what any possible response may look like.\par \par \uc2 Former top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Hiroshi Watanabe, meanwhile, told Reuters the government did not need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen falls, as such a move would be ineffective in countering broad dollar gains.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which dropped as low as 144.38 per dollar on Wednesday, has lost about 20% since the start of the year, driven lower mainly by divergence in monetary policy between Japan and the United States.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar/yen seems to be overshooting somewhat now and could briefly touch 145 later this month. But such a move likely won't last long," Watanabe said, adding the government did not need to respond even if the dollar did briefly reach 145 yen.\par \par \uc2 The government "also doesn't need to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  (to smooth market volatility) as exchange-rate moves are driven by broad dollar gains," he added.\par \par \uc2 POLICY DIVERGENCE\par \par \uc2 The main reason for the yen's fall from around 115 per dollar since the start of the year has been a growing gap between U.S. interest rates and the ultra-low levels maintained by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has vowed to stick to its powerful monetary stimulus and is widely expected to do so. Ex-\uc2 BOJ\uc2  board member \uc2 Goushi Kataoka\uc2  told Reuters it was unlikely to shift its stance even as inflation is seen reaching 3% in coming months, well above the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  target of 2%.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , on the other hand, is expected to continue raising interest rates for the time being.\par \par \uc2 "As long as the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  maintains its current policy, it'll be very hard for the Japanese side to turn this tide," said Bart Wakabayashi, co-branch manager for State Street Bank.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling the dollar and buying the yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in June 1998 when the yen fell beyond 146 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has often proved to be a temporary break, and not so much to change the market mindset," Wakabayashi added. (Reporting by Kantaro Komiya, Tetsushi Kajimoto and Daniel Leussink; Writing by Daniel Leussink; Editing by Chang-Ran Kim and Bradley Perrett) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-7T08:32:52.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220907ei9700dta\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
