\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits fresh peak against yen on bets for aggressive Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2022\par 03:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 7 (Reuters) - The dollar hit a 24-year peak against the yen and reached new highs versus the Australian and New Zealand dollars on Wednesday after U.S. economic data reinforced the view that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will continue aggressive policy tightening.\par \par \uc2 The euro continued to languish not far from Tuesday's two-decade low, well below parity, as \uc2 European Union\uc2  ministers prepare to meet this week to discuss the energy crisis that is hammering industry and squeezing households.\par \par \uc2 A report overnight showed the U.S. services industry unexpectedly picked up last month, reinforcing the view that the economy is not in recession and giving the Fed leeway for another super-sized 75 basis-point rate rise on Sept. 21.\par \par \uc2 Markets currently give that scenario 73% probability, with 23% odds for a half-point hike.\par \par \uc2 The dollar jumped as high as 143.57 yen early in the Asian day, before trading 0.34% stronger than Tuesday at 143.28, as \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields also rose.\par \par \uc2 The yen is extremely sensitive to moves in long-term U.S. rates, and the yield on the 10-year Treasury note climbed as high as 3.365% in Tokyo trading, a level not seen since June 16.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  said on Tuesday that the recent market volatility was high and that authorities are watching the yen with a great sense of urgency.\par \par \uc2 But Rikiya Takebe, senior strategist at \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2 , said the comments were not new.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts have said the fact that Suzuki has not reiterated the strong language used in July, when he said he was "concerned" after the yen fell beyond 139 to the dollar, suggested \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market may not be imminent.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or policy revisions by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to be difficult, and it will not be easy to stop the yen from falling," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Foreign central banks are prioritizing dealing with inflation, and cannot afford to worry about exchange rate fluctuations."\par \par \uc2 Takebe added that there could be some defense at the 145 yen per dollar level because it is likely that a considerable amount of knockout triggers for domestic financial institutions' structured bonds are stacked up at that level.\par \par \uc2 The euro wallowed 0.2% lower at $0.9888 after dipping as low as $0.9864 overnight.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen likely to deliver a 75 bps rate hike on Thursday, but traders seem more focused on Russia's decision to keep the key Nord Stream 1 gas pipeline shut indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 EU\uc2  energy ministers meet on Friday and will discuss options including gas price caps and emergency credit lines for energy market participants, a document seen by Reuters showed.\par \par \uc2 Sterling shed 0.3% to $1.1483, approaching the $1.1444 low from Monday, a level last seen in March 2020, with Britain also entangled in the energy crisis, despite new prime minister Lizz Truss's plans for a massive support package.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar index, which measures the greenback against six major peers, strengthened 0.08% to 110.43, sticking close to Tuesday's 20-year high of 110.57.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Aussie declined 0.23% to $0.67165, and earlier touched $0.67105 for the first time since July 14, garnering little support from Tuesday's as-expected Reserve Bank rate increase.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand's kiwi dropped 0.43% to $0.6015, the lowest since May 2020.\par \par \uc2 Cryptocurrency bitcoin stood around $18,822 after dipping to $18,666 earlier for the first time since June 30. It tumbled more than 5% on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kevin Buckland; Additional reporting by Daiki Iga; Editing by Ana Nicolaci da Costa) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-7T02:47:22.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220907ei97008et\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
