\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan finmin says watching rising  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  volatility as yen hits new lows\b0\par\par\uc2 550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2022\par 12:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds analyst comments, updates dollar/yen rates)\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves must reflect economic fundamentals - finmin\par \par \uc2 * Suzuki reiterates watching \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility with sense of urgency\par \par \uc2 * Suzuki's verbal warning not seen as strong as in July - analyst\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 6 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  on Tuesday reiterated that sharp yen moves were "undesirable" and that he was watching rising volatility in the exchange market with a "great sense of urgency" as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a fresh 24-year low.\par \par \uc2 It was Suzuki's latest verbal warning on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but it was not considered as strong as comments made in July, when a fall beyond 139 to the dollar prompted him to say he was "concerned". Suzuki's comments on Tuesday were made before the yen hit the new low.\par \par \uc2 The yen weakened on Tuesday beyond 141 per dollar for the first time since August 1998. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slumped nearly 20% since the start of the year, on diverging monetary policies between Japan and the United States.\par \par \uc2 While the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) has vowed to stick to powerful monetary stimulus to back a fragile economy, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is expected to continue raising rates for the time being.\par \par \uc2 "It's important for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move stably, reflecting economic fundamentals," Suzuki told reporters at the finance ministry.\par \par \uc2 When pressed to comment on the impact of a weak yen on the economy, Suzuki said "a weak yen has both merit and demerit, but sharp moves are undesirable."\par \par \uc2 The yen, which weakened to as low as 141.88 per dollar earlier, last traded at 141.77.\par \par \uc2 The yen came under pressure after the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (\uc2 RBA\uc2 ) raised its cash rate by 50 basis points, while investors were also expecting a big rate increase from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week.\par \par \uc2 "The trigger was the \uc2 RBA\uc2 's 0.5 percentage point rate hike, which was expected but nevertheless faster than a regular pace," said Masahiro Ichikawa, chief market strategist at Sumitomo Mitsui DS Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "With the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's meeting also awaiting this week, where a big 75 basis point hike and a further hawkish turn are expected, the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's ultra-easy stance has brought about yen selling," he added.\par \par \uc2 Suzuki said on Friday that Tokyo will take "appropriate" action as needed, after the dollar broke above the psychologically-important 140 threshold last week.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts said the fact that Suzuki did not use the word "concerned" then suggested \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market may not be imminent.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be possible technically but it's difficult politically to sell the dollar at a time when the U.S. is fighting inflation," said Daisaku Ueno, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "That may be a reason why the minister toned down a tad on the verbal warning."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling the dollar and buying the yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in June 1998 when the yen fell to beyond 146 to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Additional reporting by Kantaro Komiya and Daniel Leussink; Editing by Muralikumar Anantharaman and Ana Nicolaci da Costa) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-6T11:08:34.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220906ei9601kv9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
