\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Japan's tool-kit to combat sharp yen declines\b0\par\par\uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2022\par 11:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 6 (Reuters) - Japanese policymakers are struggling to combat the yen's rapid fall to a 24-year low with little sign of them taking any significant action beyond verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Below are possible steps the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could take to address further yen weakness, which gives exports a boost but hurts households and retailers by inflating already rising import costs for fuel and food.\par \par \uc2 ESCALATE VERBAL \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  - HIGHLY LIKELY\par \par \uc2 As the yen hit a fresh 24-year low beyond 140 to the dollar this month, policymakers have warned that recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were "undesirable," and that they were watching moves with "a great sense of urgency."\par \par \uc2 They have so far refrained from repeating stronger warnings made in July, when Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  voiced "concern" over what he saw as "speculative" moves in the market after the yen slid below 139 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 If policymakers become more alarmed over volatile yen moves, they could escalate their warning by describing yen moves as "one-sided," and saying authorities are "ready to take decisive steps" as needed.\par \par \uc2 Such remarks would signal that Tokyo is edging closer to directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 OFFER FISCAL SUPPORT - HIGHLY LIKELY\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2 's administration plans to compile a fresh package of measures to cushion the blow to households and retailers from rising living costs.\par \par \uc2 While such measures won't directly slow the yen's slide, they will ease the pain on households and take some pressure off the government to take more direct steps to stem yen declines.\par \par \uc2 BOJ TWEAKS DOVISH GUIDANCE - LESS LIKELY\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  has vowed to keep interest rates ultra-low to support the economy. The pledge is partly driving the yen's decline as markets focus on the policy divergence between the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 , which is hiking rates aggressively.\par \par \uc2 Some market players speculate the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  may allow interest rates to rise, or tweak its dovish guidance by moving from pledges to keep rates at "present or lower levels" to adopt a more neutral tone in order to slow the yen's decline.\par \par \uc2 Many \uc2 BOJ\uc2  policymakers are cautious of taking such steps with inflation still moderate and the economy's recovery fragile.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  also has no intention of using monetary policy tools to directly curb the yen's decline - a move that could be interpreted as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation and would go beyond its remit.\par \par \uc2 That means the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  will consider tweaking its guidance only if inflation rises for longer than expected, and prods firms to raise wages and prices on a sustained basis.\par \par \uc2 CONDUCT YEN-BUYING \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  - HIGHLY UNLIKELY\par \par \uc2 Some analysts and lawmakers see 140 yen to the dollar as Tokyo's line-in-the-sand which, if breached, could raise the alarm among \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. The dollar hit 141.88 yen on Tuesday, the highest since August 1998.\par \par \uc2 But yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be costly as authorities must tap \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves for dollars to sell.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo would also need consent from other major economies, particularly the United States, to ensure the scale of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is big enough to turn around the markets' tide.\par \par \uc2 Japanese delegates, led by Suzuki, will have a chance to convince their G7 counterparts when they attend the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  meeting in Washington D.C. in October.\par \par \uc2 Suzuki said he did not raise the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a G7 virtual finance leaders' meeting on Sept. 2.\par \par \uc2 Gaining consent won't be easy with Washington traditionally opposed to the idea of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , except in cases of extreme market volatility.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. administration also has little to gain from a weaker dollar as the greenback's strength helps moderate red-hot inflation, which is turning into a political headache. (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Additional reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Ana Nicolaci da Costa) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-6T10:25:37.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220906ei9601fb9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
