\par\par \b \uc2 No  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  needed in yen as it stages recovery, 'Mr. Yen' Sakakibara says\b0\par\par\uc2 399 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2022\par 19:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, July 29 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as the sharp depreciation in the yen won't continue, due to the changing economic environment in the United States and Japan, the country's former top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Eisuke Sakakibara said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "It might reach 140, and it could exceed even 140. But I don't think it would go much further, like 150, 160," Sakakibara told the Reuters Global Markets Forum (GMF).\par \par \uc2 Sakakibara, who is known as "Mr. Yen" for masterminding several \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 1990s, said the fall in the yen had mostly been driven by divergent monetary policies between the United States and Japan.\par \par \uc2 "That is what has happened up till now, but things are now changing," said Sakakibara, who is essentially the sole \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat in Japan who had experience of both yen-selling and yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 From the beginning of 2022, the Japanese yen fell around 21% to a multi-year low of 139.39 to the dollar on July 14. It has since recovered to 133.42, triggered by a lower adjustment in U.S. yields that reflects narrowing expectations for policy divergence between the Fed and the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ), \uc2 Mizuho\uc2  strategists wrote.\par \par \uc2 The gap between yields on 10-year U.S. Treasuries and equivalent Japanese government debt has narrowed by 70 bps since the beginning of June, as signs of slowing U.S. growth and interest rate rises pushed Treasury yields lower.\par \par \uc2 Sakakibara said the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  may be forced to continue its easy monetary policy for the next couple of years due to developing recessionary pressures in the global economy.\par \par \uc2 However, he did not see it as a "missed opportunity" for the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 , whose dovish stance stands out in a recent flurry of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate hikes to combat soaring inflation.\par \par \uc2 Sakakibara's views are closely watched by markets as he retains close contact with incumbent policymakers, has experience of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and has a knack for interpreting finance authorities' stance on yen moves. (Join GMF, a chat room hosted on Refinitiv Messenger: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://refini.tv/33uoFoQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://refini.tv/33uoFoQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 ))\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Divya Chowdhury in Mumbai; Additional reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto in Tokyo, Savio Shetty in Mumbai and Nishara Pathikkal in Bengaluru; Editing by David Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-7-29T18:15:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220729ei7t03ryh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
