\par\par \b \uc2 LIVE MARKETS-U.S. stocks step gingerly, close mixed\b0\par\par\uc2 2,614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2022\par 22:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Dow ekes out a gain, S&P 500, Nasdaq post tiny losses\par \par \uc2 * Energy weakest S&P 500 sector; healthcare leads gainers\par \par \uc2 * Dollar up; gold, bitcoin slip; crude off >2%\par \par \uc2 * U.S. 10-Year Treasury yield slides to ~3.09%\par \par \uc2 June 29 - Welcome to the home for real-time coverage of markets brought to you by Reuters reporters. You can share your thoughts with us at markets.research@thomsonreuters.com\par \par \uc2 U.S. STOCKS STEP GINGERLY, CLOSE MIXED (1605 EDT/2005 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Wall Street wavered on Wednesday as investors limped toward the finish line of a downbeat month, a dismal quarter, and the worst first-half for the S&P 500 since President Richard Nixon's first term.\par \par \uc2 With this, a number of the major averages saw especially small changes.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 ended down just 0.07%, and the Nasdaq finished off just 0.03%. It was the S&P 500's smallest net change, on an absolute basis, since May 20. It was the Nasdaq's smallest net change since April 27.\par \par \uc2 Chips and energy stocks saw more spirited action. The Philadelphia Semiconductor index ended down more than 2%.\par \par \uc2 And the S&P energy sector slid more than 3%. This, as crude futures reversed; after being up more than 2%, crude oil is now posting a more than 2% loss.\par \par \uc2 Here is Wednesday's closing snapshot:\par \par \uc2 (Terence Gabriel)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 LONG-DELAYED YEN RECOVERY INEVITABLE ONCE U.S. HITS RECESSION (1345 EDT/1745 GMT)\par \par \uc2 There's light at the end of the tunnel for the long suffering yen. \uc2 Ulrich Leuchtmann\uc2 , head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commodity research at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 , writes in a research note that increasing global economic risks due in part to a protracted Ukraine war as well as tightening by central banks around the world are unlikely to lead to significantly more aggressive interest rate hike expectations elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 This should be good news for the yen which has struggled given that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is the lone \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has maintained an ultra-easy monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Since the beginning of the year, the yen has fallen more than 15% versus a resurgent U.S. dollar, on track for its worst yearly performance since 2013. Against the euro, the Japanese unit has dropped by nearly 9% so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Leuchtmann says the yen would certainly benefit should the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's aggressive monetary stance drive the world's largest economy into recession. The Fed has raised interest rates by 150 basis points since March.\par \par \uc2 More and more banks are calling for the U.S. to enter into recession by 2023.\par \par \uc2 "As the probability of a U.S. recession increases, the realization that the Fed will have to cut its key rate again in the foreseeable future should increasingly mature," the \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  strategist says. "This should make the yen's long-term carry disadvantage appear less pronounced."\par \par \uc2 Leuchtmann also believes there is no reason for the BoJ to change its monetary policy despite an uptick in Japanese inflation. At 1.2%, it most recent reading, Japan's inflation has reached its highest level since 2018.\par \par \uc2 But Japan's import-driven increase in producer prices is hardly being passed on to consumer prices. "Inflation dynamics in Japan continue to be massively disrupted," says Leuchtmann.\par \par \uc2 Overall, he expects the dollar-yen exchange rate to stabilize without the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen was last down around 0.3% on the day at just under 136.60 yen per dollar.\par \par \uc2 (Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 CHIP STOCKS TUMBLE AS BOFA PREDICTS INDUSTRY DOWNTURN (1250 EDT/1650 GMT)\par \par \uc2 U.S. semiconductor stocks are tumbling on Wednesday, and BofA Global Research is highlighting worries the industry is in for a downturn as it cuts price targets across the board.\par \par \uc2 The Philadelphia Semiconductor Index is dropping 3.3%, putting it on track for its lowest close since late 2020, and leaving it down 35% year-to-date.\par \par \uc2 After \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  on Tuesday said easing chip shortages were starting to offset supply chain bottlenecks and rising costs, \uc2 BofA\uc2  is now cutting estimates and price targets on several chipmakers. Rising interest rates, geopolitical turmoil and a weaker consumer could pressure chip demand.\par \par \uc2 "Semi downturns happen every 3-4 years, and we could be due for another one," \uc2 BofA\uc2  analyst Vivek Arya wrote.\par \par \uc2 BofA\uc2  cut its ratings on \uc2 Skyworks Solutions\uc2  and \uc2 Qorvo\uc2  to "underperform" from "neutral", citing a slowing 5G smartphone cycle, high inventories and competition from \uc2 Qualcomm\uc2 . As well, it cut \uc2 Texas Instruments\uc2  to "neutral" from "buy" on China smartphone weakness.\par \par \uc2 BofA\uc2  also lowered price targets for over 20 chip stocks, including \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , \uc2 Broadcom\uc2 , \uc2 Intel\uc2 , \uc2 Advanced Micro\uc2 , \uc2 Applied Materials\uc2 , \uc2 Analog Devices\uc2  and \uc2 Micron Technology\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , AMD and \uc2 Broadcom\uc2  are each down between 2% and 5%, weighing heavily on the SOX.\par \par \uc2 Arya now expects chip industry sales growth of just under 10% this year, followed by a 1% dip in 2023. He previously expected 13% growth this year and 7% growth next year. Since 1995, chip industry downturns have amounted to average sales declines of 9%, Arya writes.\par \par \uc2 However, semi stocks tend to bottom well before industry sales troughs, with forward PEs on average declining by 26%.\par \par \uc2 "In the current cycle, the SOX forward PE has fallen 42% from peak levels, potentially suggesting valuations may bottom shortly," Arya writes.\par \par \uc2 (Noel Randewich)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 A BEAR MARKET RALLY CAN BE QUITE THE TRICKSTER (1205 EDT/1605 GMT)\par \par \uc2 A bull market in the S&P 500 ended in early January of this year, and with this, consumer, small business and investor sentiment have fallen to, or near, all-time lows.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Julian Emanuel\uc2 , senior managing director at Evercore ISI, sees it, gloom is pervasive, and a degree of capitulation may have occurred in crypto and equity trading volumes.\par \par \uc2 A sufficient fall in gasoline prices signaling inflation is under control and a tightening of credit spreads, suggesting recession risks are abating, "could \u8211\'20\'13 but likely won\u8217\'20\'19t \u8211\'20\'13 materialize over the Summer to turn 6/17\u8217\'20\'19s low into 'The' Bottom vs. 'A' bottom, tradable Bear Market rallies such as those seen throughout the 1970s, occur regularly."\par \par \uc2 For example, here is a recreation of Emanuel's Dow Jones Industrial Average chart showing that in the 1970's, at times, even short sellers got the blues:\par \par \uc2 According to Emanuel, a \u8220\'20\'1CBear Market rally\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 can only be known with hindsight. In any event, he looked at advances of 5% or more over 5 or more trading days, or 10% or more over any timeframe, since 1960.\par \par \uc2 "Consistently, Bear Market rallies that begin from ever lower lows tend to bounce 'harder'."\par \par \uc2 He says that the current rally, which began on June 17, down 24.5% from the peak, started in the lower quartile of where bear market rallies begin, and that the "average" bounce for these rallies from extreme falls is 17.6% with an average duration across all observations of 28 trading days.\par \par \uc2 Emanuel also says that Bear Market rallies, which by definition are dangerous prior to the \u8220\'20\'1Cfinal\u8221\'20\'1D bottom, end suddenly and without a catalyst.\par \par \uc2 "This Bear Market rally likely will end suddenly as well; we believe it could extend into the 7/27 FOMC meeting."\par \par \uc2 Among the approaches Emanuel favors is to carefully and selectively add to risk by screening for stocks \u8211\'20\'13 with strong FCF yield, high short interest, and whose share prices have declined more than the SPX from its Pandemic peak, while diverging from the SPX by not making new price lows in June.\par \par \uc2 (Terence Gabriel)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 S&P BOTTOM AROUND 3,100 AS FED KEEPS HIKING \u8211\'20\'13 SANDERS MORRIS HARRIS (1122 EDT/1522 GMT)\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 still has a lot of room to fall as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  continues to hike rates in an effort to stem soaring inflation, with the index likely to bottom around 3,100, according to investment firm Sanders Morris Harris.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's aggressive, but necessary inflation-fighting measures are likely to depress corporate earnings and push stocks lower,\u8221\'20\'1D George Ball, chairman of Sanders Morris Harris said in a note, adding that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to hike rates by 50 or 75 basis points at each of its meetings for the rest of the year.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 entered a bear market on June 13 for the first time since March 2020 when it dropped to 3,750, down more than 20% from its record closing high.\par \par \uc2 The index has since clawed back to 3,824. A fall to 3,100 would be a 19% decrease from current levels.\par \par \uc2 Ball sees the Fed maintaining its hawkish policy even if growth weakens. \u8220\'20\'1CInflation at current levels is not acceptable, and the Fed's rate hiking measures, albeit painful short-term for the markets and economy and could cause a mild recession this year, are necessary to bring prices back to normal levels,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 (Karen Brettell)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 COOLING DAYS: GDP, MORTGAGE DEMAND (1055 EDT/1455 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Data released on the last hump day of the second quarter told us winter's economic contraction was steeper than originally thought.\par \par \uc2 The Commerce Department took its third and final stab at GDP for the first three months of 2022, that long-ago ancient era when folks were saying things like "omicron" and "transient inflation."\par \par \uc2 As it turns out, the economy contracted by 1.6% at a quarterly annualized rate, 0.1 percentage point worse than previously reported. But beneath the headline, the revisions grow more interesting.\par \par \uc2 On the plus side, fixed investment - which includes construction and capex - contributed 1.3 percentage points (pps) to the total, more than previously reported. And private inventories were revised to show a much smaller drag, detracting 0.4 pps rather than 1.1 pps.\par \par \uc2 On the downside, trade lopped 3.3 pps from the total (previously 3.2 pps).\par \par \uc2 But the most eye-opening element was consumer spending growth - which accounts for around 70% of the U.S. economy - being slashed to 1.8% from 3.1%. American consumers contributed a mere 1.2 percentage points to the total, down from 2.1 pps.\par \par \uc2 "Our baseline remains that the economy will continue to expand this year. But the pace will moderate substantially and the risks around a potential recession are rising," writes Rubeela Farooqi, chief U.S. economist at \uc2 High Frequency Economics\uc2 . "In terms of Fed policy, a move to slightly restrictive policy is essential to contain extraordinarily high inflation, even as growth slows this year."\par \par \uc2 The two graphics below show GDP contributions by broad category, along with a more granular look at consumer expenditures:\par \par \uc2 Jaunting over to the housing market, a dip in mortgage rates - interrupting their fairly steady uphill climb - was enough to increase home loan demand, according to the \uc2 Mortgage Bankers Association (MBA)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The average 30-year fixed contract rate shed 14 basis points to 5.84%, backing down from its highest level since November 2008, when the housing market was in mid-collapse amid the Financial Crisis.\par \par \uc2 That was enough to prompt a nominal 0.1% increase in applications for loans to purchase homes and a more substantial 1.9% jump in refi demand.\par \par \uc2 "Rates are still significantly higher than they were a year ago, when the 30-year fixed rate was at 3.2 percent," said Joel Kan, Associate Vice President of Economic and Industry Forecasting at MBA. "Overall purchase activity has weakened in recent months due to the quick jump in mortgage rates, high home prices, and growing economic uncertainty."\par \par \uc2 Home loan interest rates have tracked benchmark Treasury yields on their upward trajectory, adding insult to injury caused by sky-rocketing home prices. Together, they are increasingly removing home ownership from the realm of possibility for many potential buyers, particularly at the lower end if the market.\par \par \uc2 Over the past 12 months the fixed 30-year contract rate has risen by 2.64 pps, while overall mortgage demand is down 49.5% from the same week a year ago:\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks are last pale green, having wobbled close to the zero line since the opening bell.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  are the upside muscle, while economically sensitive chips small caps and transports are holding gains in check.\par \par \uc2 (Stephen Culp)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 WALL STREET SHARES RISE IN CHOPPY TRADE (1005 EDT/1405 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Shares on Wall Street are rising early on Wednesday in a very choppy session, with investors nervous overall amid comments from \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials advocating for hefty interest rate hikes to bring down stubbornly high inflation even as a slew of economic data points have begun to show an economy plunging into a downturn.\par \par \uc2 There is also quarter-end rebalancing of portfolios that has been stoking market volatility, traders say.\par \par \uc2 Cleveland Federal Reserve Bank\uc2  President \uc2 Loretta Mester\uc2  pushed for another 75 basis points (bps) interest rate hike at the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's July meeting, if economic conditions remain the same.\par \par \uc2 Her comments came in the wake of data on Wednesday showing that the final reading of first quarter U.S. gross domestic product showed the economy fell 1.6%, compared with the consensus estimate of a 1.5% contraction.\par \par \uc2 "The market continues to weigh up growth fears," says \uc2 Fiona Cincotta\uc2 , senior financial markets analyst, at City Index. "Fears that an aggressive Fed could tip the U.S. into recession have been lingering since the FOMC meeting in June."\par \par \uc2 Declining issues outnumbered advancing ones on the NYSE by a 1.75-to-1 ratio; on Nasdaq, a 2.11-to-1 ratio favored decliners.\par \par \uc2 Here is Wednesday's market snapshot:\par \par \uc2 (Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 VIX IN A VISE (0900 EDT/1300 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Since spiking to a late-January high the CBOE Volatility Index has been trapped in a contracting range. A breakout may signal the next major thrust for the market's "fear" gauge.\par \par \uc2 The VIX, which is an implied volatility measure based on S&P 500 index options, is confined in a range defined by two converging trend lines: a support line from November of last year and a resistance line from the late-January high:\par \par \uc2 Shorter-term, there is also a support line from early April and a resistance line from early May.\par \par \uc2 With all of these lines converging, the VIX is being squeezed into a tighter and tighter range. In fact, one historical volatility measure on the VIX has actually been putting in lower peaks since early December, and on a weekly basis, is now flirting with seven-month lows.\par \par \uc2 Many market watchers look for "capitulation" to call a market bottom, and perhaps rightly so. With this, they are expecting a wild spike higher in the VIX, to "extreme" levels, to signal the end of the decline.\par \par \uc2 Of note, however, the pattern which is already in place of lower VIX highs against lower SPX lows can also potentially fit with a bottoming process.\par \par \uc2 Looking back from 1998 to 2020, on an intraday basis, there were nine major S&P 500 declines: three corrections (-10%-20%) and six bear markets (-20%+). In only two of those instances (22% of the time), did the day of the VIX intraday high coincide with the day of the S&P 500 intraday low - click here for more:\par \par \uc2 That said, the VIX's 200-day moving average, now around 23.75, continues to bubble up, suggesting a still elevated volatility regime - click here:\par \par \uc2 Traders will be watching closely to see in what direction key VIX levels ultimately start to give way.\par \par \uc2 (Terence Gabriel)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 FOR WEDNESDAY'S LIVE MARKETS' POSTS PRIOR TO 0900 EDT/1300 GMT - CLICK HERE:\par \par \uc2 (Terence Gabriel is a Reuters market analyst. The views expressed are his own) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-6-29T21:25:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220629ei6t03ljx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
