\par\par \b \uc2 The Yen's Weakness Is Another Sign That Something Isn't Right in the Global Economy -- Barrons.com\b0\par\par\uc2 By Reshma Kapadia \par \uc2 815 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 2022\par 10:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Investors looking for trouble spots as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raises interest rates by 0.75 percentage point -- the biggest hike since 1994 -- may want to keep an eye on the sharp decline in the Japanese Yen against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Not only could the weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exacerbate the inflation the Fed is so worried about, but it could also create another destabilizing force for the global economy.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese Yen has weakened against the U.S. dollar by about 32% since January 2021, and 14% since March. That is a sharp move for the staid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, underscoring the level of volatility facing investors beyond just stocks and bonds. "It could become disorderly," says Exante Data founder Jens Nordvig, cautioning that domestic Japanese investors haven't even started selling yet.\par \par \uc2 The yen's weakness is a byproduct of diverging monetary policy. While the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is tightening much more aggressively than expected even a couple of months ago, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is one of the few sticking with easier monetary policy and even adding stimulus through balance sheet measures, says veteran \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 Marc Chandler\uc2  of Bannockburn Global \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 , who sees the moves as another sign of a bond bear market.\par \par \uc2 While much of the world is fixated on inflation, Japan has waged decades long battle with deflation -- one reason it hasn't been as hawkish as its central banking peers in the U.S. and Europe. But it too doesn't want inflation. After all its aging population is reliant on fixed income. Inflation is kryptonite to those assets, and weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  can exacerbate inflationary pressures, says \uc2 Jack McIntyre\uc2 , a manager on the global fixed income team at Brandywine Global.\par \par \uc2 Plus, as an energy and food importer, Japan now is contending with sharp spikes in crucial commodities and a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pay for them with. With Japan's election next month, political pressure for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to "do something" to staunch the declines is increasing.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has to choose between controlling its exchange rate or its interest rate and possibly abandoning its yield curve controls of a 25 basis point target for 10-year Japanese government bond yields. In recent days, the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already tried to verbally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but McIntyre says an actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be likely.\par \par \uc2 That too could be dangerous -- and expensive in the reserves Japan may need to spend, especially at a time the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't that out of whack with fundamentals, says Nordvig. If Japan needs to sell Treasuries, that would push Treasury yields higher, and put even more pressure on dollar/yen, adding another source of volatility. Plus, an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also draw speculators that exacerbate the situation, requiring even more of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves to stabilize the situation.\par \par \uc2 Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely require a change in policy. Since Japanese government bonds are the anchor for global bond yields, any change would ripple through markets.\par \par \uc2 Could the yen's slide be a canary in the coal mine? Possibly, McIntyre says, noting the inflationary backdrop globally. "Nobody really wants a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this environment. From that standpoint, maybe we will start to see other governments, central banks in particular, push back on weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," McIntyre says.\par \par \uc2 A competitive devaluation among Japan's exporting rivals in Asia, including China, would only add stress to a fragile economy. China's yuan, for example, has been a source of low volatility acting as a stabilizer in global trade. A move away from that would be another major source of volatility.\par \par \uc2 U.S. companies will feel the ripples of a strong dollar, with the U.S. Dollar Index (DXY) hitting its highest level relative to major peers since late 2002, as they translate foreign earnings back into U.S. dollars. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  (MSFT) already warned of a $460 million hit to the fourth quarter because of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations.\par \par \uc2 In theory, dollar strength could mean foreign equities are more attractive than U.S. stocks. But in reality, TS Lombard strategists say in a note, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  backdrop adds to cost pressures to already squeezed margins and "creating a destabilizing force in an already tenuous global economy."\par \par \uc2 The concern: Further dollar strength could hurt international trade, squeeze balance sheets of emerging markets that have borrowed in dollars and intensify what is already a significant tightening in the global financial conditions, according to TS Lombard.\par \par \uc2 The yen should be on the list of what could "break" as a result of diverging \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. That means all eyes will be on Friday's \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  meeting.\par \par \uc2 Write to Reshma Kapadia at reshma.kapadia@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 16, 2022 04:14 ET (08:14 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220616ei6g000ln\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
