\par\par \b \uc2 Factbox-Who are candidates to become next \uc2 BOJ\uc2  governor?\b0\par\par\uc2 765 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2022\par 05:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Reuters) - Japan's selection of a new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor is likely to intensify after an upper house election in July and could determine how quickly the country will follow global counterparts in withdrawing monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2  will decide from a short-list, usually crafted by financial bureaucrats, a successor to \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  whose second five-year term ends in April next year.\par \par \uc2 The two deputy governor posts, currently filled by career central banker \uc2 Masayoshi Amamiya\uc2  and academic \uc2 Masazumi Wakatabe\uc2 , will also open up in March.\par \par \uc2 Below are possible candidates for the post:\par \par \uc2 MASAYOSHI AMAMIYA - VERY STRONG CONTENDER FOR GOVERNOR\par \par \uc2 Currently deputy governor, Amamiya has spent most of his career at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  drafting monetary policy ideas and is nick-named "Mr. \uc2 BOJ\uc2 " for masterminding many of the bank's unconventional monetary easing ideas.\par \par \uc2 He played a key role in drafting Kuroda's huge asset-buying programme in 2013 and consistently called for keeping ultra-low interest rates.\par \par \uc2 An avid fan of classical music, Amamiya is known for his deep contacts with lawmakers and bureaucrats that help him read which way the political wind is blowing in steering policy.\par \par \uc2 HIROSHI NAKASO - VERY STRONG CONTENDER FOR GOVERNOR\par \par \uc2 A career central banker who served as deputy governor until 2018, Nakaso played a key role in navigating an exit from the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's first spell of quantitative easing in 2006.\par \par \uc2 With long experience overseeing the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and international affairs, Nakaso has repeatedly warned of the drawbacks of prolonged monetary easing such as the distortion its huge presence could create in bond and money markets.\par \par \uc2 MASATSUGU ASAKAWA - STRONG CONTENDER FOR GOVERNOR\par \par \uc2 Like Kuroda, Asakawa moved up the ranks at the finance ministry and became Japan's top financial diplomat in 2015, a job he kept for four years during which he battled a strong yen and engaged in tough negotiations with then U.S. President Donald Trump's administration.\par \par \uc2 After retiring from the ministry, he became president of the \uc2 Asian Development Bank\uc2  in 2020 - a job Kuroda also took before becoming \uc2 BOJ\uc2  governor.\par \par \uc2 In his book, Asakawa praised Kuroda's stimulus programme as a right move to beat deflation. He also said yen declines would be harder to stop than yen rises via \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because the amount of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can sell to prop up the yen would be limited to the size of its foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 DARK HORSE CANDIDATES\par \par \uc2 Traditionally, the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  and the finance ministry take turns filling the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor post, holding the post for only a single five-year term. That broke when Kuroda was appointed in 2018 to serve a second five-year term.\par \par \uc2 If history is any guide, the next \uc2 BOJ\uc2  governor would be chosen from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ranks. But there is a chance Kishida opts for a dark horse candidate.\par \par \uc2 Among names floated by analysts are \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  professor Takatoshi Ito, who is close to Kuroda, and former top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Takeo Nakao.\par \par \uc2 Given Kishida's close ties with the finance ministry, he may hand-pick a former top bureaucrat from the institution, though there is no consensus on who could be among strong candidates.\par \par \uc2 SHINICHI UCHIDA - POSSIBLE DEPUTY GOVERNOR\par \par \uc2 A career central banker who spent most of his career at the elite monetary affairs department, Uchida is a rising star who landed a rare reappointment for a second, four-year term as the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's executive director overseeing monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts see the reappointment as a sign he could fill one of the two deputy governor posts.\par \par \uc2 Together with Amamiya, Uchida played a key role in drafting many of Kuroda's policies including the introduction of negative interest rates and yield curve control in 2016.\par \par \uc2 People who know him say Uchida is a firm believer that the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  must maintain ultra-loose monetary for the time being to reflate growth and tighten the job market enough so that wages rise more.\par \par \uc2 TOKIKO SHIMIZU - POSSIBLE DEPUTY GOVERNOR\par \par \uc2 Currently the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's executive director overseeing international affairs, Shimizu is among the few female central bankers who rose up the ranks in the male-dominated institution.\par \par \uc2 While her career at the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  centred on international and market affairs rather than monetary policy, Shimizu is considered a dark horse candidate as deputy governor or even governor if Kishida wants more diversity in the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  leadership.\par \par \uc2 No female has yet served as \uc2 BOJ\uc2  deputy governor or governor.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Editing by Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-6-14T03:17:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220614ei6e00a9h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
