\par\par \b \uc2 EXPLAINER-What are the consequences of the yen's fall to a 24-year low?\b0\par\par\uc2 900 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2022\par 08:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates with Monday's market moves)\par \par \uc2 By Saikat Chatterjee\par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 13 (Reuters) - The Japanese yen fell past the psychological 135 line to levels against the U.S. dollar last seen in October 1998.\par \par \uc2 The scale of the move has repercussions for the domestic economy as yen-based import prices are surging at a record annual pace, heaping pressure on household balance sheets.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan and the Japanese government on Friday gave a rare joint statement that they may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if weakness persists.\par \par \uc2 So far the fallout from the weakening yen has been minimal for broader financial markets, but that could change if the sell-off accelerates.\par \par \uc2 Below are key questions about what a sliding yen means for Japan's economy and international markets:\par \par \uc2 WHY IS THE YEN WEAK?\par \par \uc2 The yen, the third most-traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  globally, fell as low as 135.22 yen after starting 2022 at 115. With the dollar up more than 16% so far this year, the yen is on track for its biggest annual drop since 2013.\par \par \uc2 The weakness primarily stems from widening interest rate differentials between Japan and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 While the rest of the world, led by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 , is raising rates aggressively to tame soaring inflation, the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  has doubled down on its easy policy stance.\par \par \uc2 The gap between Japanese 10-year government bond yields and those in the United States is 293 basis points -- a near 3-1/2-year high -- while the gap with German yields is at 8-year highs.\par \par \uc2 WILL AUTHORITIES \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENE\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 They certainly say they might.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said they were concerned by the recent sharp falls, the strongest warning to date that Tokyo could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen quickly bounced away from its two-decade lows, but not everyone is convinced actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely.\par \par \uc2 Given the economy's reliance on exports, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has historically focused on arresting sharp yen rises and taken a hands-off approach to yen weakness, which is more difficult because yen-buying requires Japan to draw on limited foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 The last time \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was 1998, when the Asian financial crisis triggered rapid capital outflows from the region. Before that, Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter yen falls in 1991-1992.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is costly and could easily fail given the difficulty of influencing the yen's value in global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 WHAT CAN STOP THE DECLINE?\par \par \uc2 A marked improvement in growth prospects as the country reopens its borders post-COVID and higher inflation could alter the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's dovish stance.\par \par \uc2 Japan's core consumer prices in April were 2.1% higher than a year earlier, exceeding the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's 2% inflation target for the first time in seven years.\par \par \uc2 "The yen's fall could stop if the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  changes tack and becomes hawkish," said Francesca Fornasari, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  solutions at Insight Investments.\par \par \uc2 Any sign that rates outside of Japan are peaking might also prompt a relief rally. There are no signs of that yet though, with U.S. rates set to peak at 3.5% in mid-2023, according to futures markets.\par \par \uc2 DOES A WEAKER YEN BOOST THE ECONOMY?\par \par \uc2 The yen has weakened back towards recent 7-year lows versus the Chinese yuan and is hitting new multi-year lows against the Korean won and the Taiwanese dollar, which should provide some relief for Japan's widening trade deficit.\par \par \uc2 Some like \uc2 John Vail\uc2 , chief global strategist at \uc2 Nikko Asset Management\uc2 , say \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness is crucial for Japan's economy to maintain its competitiveness as a secure source of supply-chain diversification.\par \par \uc2 The yen's decline also boosts the attractiveness of its stock market among foreign investors who consider it undervalued versus European and U.S. markets. Japanese stocks have outperformed rivals in 2022, although they are still down as investors globally dump riskier assets.\par \par \uc2 WHAT DOES IT MEAN FOR \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKETS?\par \par \uc2 The yen has long been the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of choice for investors undertaking so-called carry trades, which involve borrowing in a low-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the yen to invest in higher yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like U.S. or Canadian dollars.\par \par \uc2 A strategy borrowing in yen and investing in an equal basket of U.S., Australian and Canadian dollars would have yielded more than 13% so far in 2022, according to Refinitiv data.\par \par \uc2 But the speed of the yen's drop and questions about policymaker \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fuelling unease among investors, especially with short bets against the yen near six-month highs.\par \par \uc2 Further volatility and weakness could undermine its appeal as a funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WHAT ABOUT DOMESTIC INVESTORS?\par \par \uc2 The yen's weakness puts Japanese investors in a bind.\par \par \uc2 Yields are high and rising, which makes foreign bonds much more attractive. But that also means the cost of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging is climbing.\par \par \uc2 So Japanese investors can often only capture the higher yields if they buy foreign bonds unhedged.\par \par \uc2 But with the yen at such depressed levels it is difficult for investors to stomach such \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, such as the yen appreciating. Even a modest move back to 115-120, where we were 4 months ago, would eat up years worth of yield advantage. \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Saikat Chatterjee and Sujata Rao; Editing by Tommy Wilkes, Kirsten Donovan) \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-6-13T07:43:59.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220613ei6d00mop\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
