\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Trading: Yen Takes Beating in Global Rout -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 1,091 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2022\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  nears its weakest point against the dollar since 2002 as U.S. inflation surges \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Julia-Ambra Verlaine in New York and Megumi Fujikawa in Tokyo \f28 \par \par \uc2 A surge of U.S. inflation is hammering the Japanese yen, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that typically strengthens on bad economic news.\par \par \uc2 The yen is approaching its weakest point since 2002, stuck between a strong U.S. dollar and Japan's easy-money policy. The dollar rose as the global economy's prospects dimmed, hurt by Russia's invasion of Ukraine, a jump in U.S. inflation and an economic slowdown in China. An index that measures the dollar against a basket of 16 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is up more than 12% over the past year. The dollar has advanced 22% against the yen during that time.\par \par \uc2 As the dollar has made broad gains, the yen has taken a beating in part because the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has pledged to continue its low-interest-rate policy despite Japan seeing slight inflation. It has rejected calls to tighten borrowing conditions.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  sits at the other end of the spectrum: It has lifted rates in half-percentage-point increments to curb what has become the highest U.S. inflation in more than four decades. The Fed meets this week to consider additional increases, and any moves it makes will cascade further into the economy.\par \par \uc2 As the world's third-most-traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the spending mechanism of a key economy, the yen is an important piece of financial markets. Some have even voiced concern that the yen's weakness might inflict pain on the sprawling \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 A cheap yen is typically a boon for the Japanese economy, which is driven by exports such as automobiles. Japan's exporters, however, are starting to show concern over the yen's latest slide because it is happening at the same time as rises in commodity prices and supply shortages.\par \par \uc2 "In normal times, a weak yen brings benefits through exports of cars and increases profits on the whole," said Seiichi Nagatsuka, vice chairman of the \uc2 Japan Automobile Manufacturers Association\uc2 , at a news conference. "The downside of a weak yen is getting bigger, given surging prices of resources and parts."\par \par \uc2 Tadashi Yanai\uc2 , chief executive of Uniqlo operator \uc2 Fast Retailing Co\uc2 ., said a weak yen isn't good for the country's economy.\par \par \uc2 "There is no merit of a weak yen at all" for the Japanese economy, he said in April. "Because Japanese companies make products using raw materials from all over the world, add value and sell them; it can't be a plus that our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued."\par \par \uc2 Typically when markets are falling, the yen is a haven asset that investors run to, similar to the dollar. But investors didn't seek shelter in the yen during the market's latest rout, analysts say, in part because of the monetary-policy divergence between the U.S. and Japan, and because of Japan's trade deficit in recent months, which some say could persist. Analysts also said the energy shock created by the war in Ukraine means Japanese importers need more dollars to buy oil and gas.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, hedge funds and investors that have sold the yen are becoming wary that the dollar's rally might be nearing an end, particularly if the U.S. economy slows as borrowing costs increase and consumers feel the effects of inflation.\par \par \uc2 Some hedge funds betting on an economic slowdown in the U.S., or an eventual recession, are buying options that pay out if the yen appreciates. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  analysts estimate the dollar is overvalued by 30% against the Japanese yen. The Wall Street bank in a note is advising clients to buy an option that pays out if the dollar depreciates below 115 against the Japanese yen in six months. One dollar currently costs around 134 yen.\par \par \uc2 "The trade-weighted yen is at its weakest since the Reagan administration," said \uc2 Zach Pandl\uc2 , \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  co-head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , interest rates and emerging markets strategy. "The dollar's level against the Japanese yen is not sustainable."\par \par \uc2 Inflation is the biggest reason for the diverging positions of the two countries' central banks. In April, overall consumer prices in Japan rose 2.5% from a year earlier. The index excluding volatile fresh-food and energy prices rose just 0.8%. In the U.S., meanwhile, consumer inflation reached an 8.6% annual rate in May as energy and food prices surged.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and the \uc2 Financial Services Agency\uc2  on Friday expressed concern over the yen's rapid weakening.\par \par \uc2 "The government and the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will closely coordinate and monitor the developments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and their effects on the economy and prices with a stronger sense of urgency," the agencies said, noting they would take action if necessary.\par \par \uc2 But investors don't expect the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- which has a history of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at the behest of the finance ministry -- to step in. If it did, it would be through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases rather than tightening policy -- a move unlikely to make much of an impact unless the U.S. joined in, which is doubtful given its inflation situation.\par \par \uc2 "The official pain threshold to provoke direct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still a long way off," said \uc2 Alan Ruskin\uc2 , a strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become progressively less \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last decade."\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in October 2011. The last time it sold dollars and bought yen was in June 1998.\par \par \uc2 Steve Englander\uc2 , head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  research and North America macro strategy at \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2 , said the yen's recent bout of weakness is different from previous episodes because it stems from a broader move in the dollar, and he expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen.\par \par \uc2 "Most such moves have happened from a point of severe yen overvaluation, which is not the case here," said Mr. Englander. "The sort of spike that we are seeing does not last long."\par \par \uc2 Write to Julia-Ambra Verlaine at Julia.Verlaine@wsj.com and Megumi Fujikawa at megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (June 13, 2022).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 13, 2022 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220613ei6d000fc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal