\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Kazaks says gradual is not slow, inflation unacceptably high \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Holzmann, asked if he wants a 50 bps increase in Sept, says that's not completely wrong \u8226\'20\'22 Canada jobless rate hits record low, bolstering case for 75-bps rate hike \u8226\'20\'22 Italian Finance Minister Franco says increases in interest rates per se do not affect very much the macro-economic scenario \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 Bank of Spain\uc2  says risks are tilted to the downside regarding growth and to the upside regarding inflation \u8226\'20\'22 Brexit: UK PM Johnson's spokesman says Northern Ireland legislation will be presented on Monday along with summary of legal advice Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 No major econ data scheduled Looking Ahead - Events, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 08:00 Belgium \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues economic growth forecasts with \uc2 ECB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Wunsch Macro Themes In Play \u8226\'20\'22 The dollar index rose nearly 1% on Friday after hopes that U.S. inflation had peaked were dashed by febrile CPI data, sending 2-year Treasury yields up 23bp to their highest since 2008 and stocks tumbling toward this year's lows. \u8226\'20\'22 Market anxiety about upcoming \uc2 ECB\uc2  hikes and U.S. CPI favoring a string of four more 50bps rate rises by the Fed was augmented by \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  consumer sentiment's current conditions index plunge to a record low, expectations to its lowest since 1980, largely on inflation concerns, with 1-year and 5-year inflation expectations increased. \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD's 0.9% plunge followed Thursday's 0.9% post-\uc2 ECB\uc2  meeting dive. \u8226\'20\'22 The \uc2 ECB\uc2  is far behind the curve with euro zone inflation at a record 8.1%, and markets fear that raising rates out of negative territory enough to tame price growth will increase fragmentation risk, causing bund-BTP yields spreads to surge further. \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD broke below the 50% Fibo of May's rebound just above 2017's 1.0340 nadir, and probed the 61.8% at 1.0516 that is a gateway to May's 1.0349 bottom. \u8226\'20\'22 Sterling was even worse off, falling 1.47%, as Thursday's post-\uc2 ECB\uc2  EUR/GBP drop reversed and prices plunged below the 61.8% Fibo of the May rebound from 1.2156. \u8226\'20\'22 Two-year gilt yields soared along with Treasury yields ahead of the June 15 and 16 Fed and \uc2 BoE\uc2  meetings, but sterling remains risk-sensitive and still dealing with Brexit issues and political uncertainty. \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY was little changed, having recovered from an early dip prompted by a warning from Japanese officials about the yen's slide and the potential for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8226\'20\'22 But that dip waned as the market doubted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was plausible near-term and a rebound was further supported by surging Treasury-JGB yield spreads after CPI remained on the rise. \u8226\'20\'22 However, the rebound toward Thursday's 134.56 trend high and the more important 2002 peak at 135.20 was slowed by broader haven yen gains as stocks and risk acceptance tumbled. \u8226\'20\'22 Without that push back, Friday's more than 20bps surge in 2-year Treasury-JGB yields would have been more than enough carry trade inducement to reach levels not seen since 1998. \u8226\'20\'22 The dollar was higher against almost every other notable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  particularly weak due to surging U.S. and European rates and falling commodity prices, save for precious metals. \u8226\'20\'22 Not surprisingly, bitcoin and ether were also down by roughly 3% and 6% respectively, and with bitcoin breaking this year's lows to trade at its lowest since March 2021. \u8226\'20\'22 The U.S. data calendar is light until Wednesday's retail sales and \uc2 NAHB\uc2  ahead that afternoon's Fed meeting, which will provide new economic projections. \u8226\'20\'22 The market is focused on whether 50bps hikes are more likely well beyond July and where the dot plotted fed funds rates land between now and 2024. That will all be compared to new forecasts for inflation, unemployment and GDP. Commentary & Analysis EUR/USD-Hot running inflation gives shorts control \u8226\'20\'22 After 1.0642 was hit on EBS overnight sellers emerged, NY opened near 1.0595 \u8226\'20\'22 Pair falls hard as May CPI indicates inflation still runs hot \u8226\'20\'22 US rates gains as higher terminal Fed rate expected \u8226\'20\'22 Risk sours; equities drop while safe-havens US$ and yen rally \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY hit 140.82 on EBS, EUR/USD hit a 16-session low of 1.0506 on EBS \u8226\'20\'22 Techs are bearish; RSIs falling, 50% & 61.8% Fibs of 1.0349-1.0787 pierced \u8226\'20\'22 US CPI surprise leaves EUR/USD at risk of testing 2022 low USD/JPY-Fiery U.S. inflation keeps 2002 highs in play, risk-off slows rise \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY shook off \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threat as CPI sent Tsy yields soaring \u8226\'20\'22 Rebound from 133.37 low and recent steep up TL eyes 2002's 135.20 EBS peak \u8226\'20\'22 But rate hike, inflation angst hit risk hard and helped the haven yen \u8226\'20\'22 Global risk trades funded with extremely cheap yen were pruned back \u8226\'20\'22 But 2-yr Tsy yields clearing 2018's prior cycle peak by 3% says buy USD \u8226\'20\'22 The focus is now on Wed's retail sales and FOMC meeting \u8226\'20\'22 Record low UofM sentiment due to inflation to keep Fed hiking \u8226\'20\'22 If 135.20 is cleared, 136.50 is the next nearby Fibo target \u8226\'20\'22 Close below 133 is needed weaken the uptrend, despite high RSIs Diverging Fed-BoE rate expectations crater sterling after US CPI rise \u8226\'20\'22 GBP$ drops 1.54% to 3-wk low 1.2312; NorAm range 1.2460-1.2304 \u8226\'20\'22 Fed pause talk scuttled after CPI ticks higher \u8226\'20\'22 US CPI brings sterling one step closer to 2022 lows \u8226\'20\'22 UK inflation-growth concerns, post-Brexit uncertainties add to GBP slide \u8226\'20\'22 Close below key Fib at 1.2411 shifts momentum to GBP bears \u8226\'20\'22 Supt at 1.2304 Fri low, 1.2272 lwr 30-w Bolli, 1.2209 lwr 30-d Bolli \u8226\'20\'22 Res 1.2386 10-HMA, 1.2445 21-DMA, pair capped by recent highs by 1.26 \u8226\'20\'22 Focus shifts to MPC meet June 16 for hints at \uc2 BoE\uc2  normalization path AUD/USD-Safe-havens rule as risk sours after CPI \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD hit 0.7137 in Europe but sellers emerged, NY opened near 0.7120 \u8226\'20\'22 Above estimate US CPI rallied US rates \u8226\'20\'22 Investors price in a higher terminal rate for the Fed hiking cycle \u8226\'20\'22 Risk sours; equities, copper fall while US$, yen rally \u8226\'20\'22 Slower growth concerns drive AUD/JPY down below 94.35, AUD/USD follows \u8226\'20\'22 Pair broke the 61.8% Fib of 0.6829-0.7282, hit 0.7038 then bounced meagerly \u8226\'20\'22 Techs are bearish; RSIs dropping, pair below 21-DMA, key Fibo breaks Fed-led dollar's run at 23-year highs vs yen slowed by risk-aversion USD/JPY rebounded on Friday near its 2022 high after surging U.S. inflation raised Fed rate hike pricing, and it may have further room to run if it can withstand warning signs like falling Michigan consumer sentiment. Risk aversion and official Japanese warnings against further fast yen falls were also delaying USD/JPY's potential rise to its highest since 1998. Friday's CPI report sent 2-year Treasury yields just above their 2018 prior Fed tightening cycle high by 3.0% and to their highest since 2008. USD/JPY's rally from May's 126.37 low is also being slowed overbought pressures on the approach to 2002's 135.20 EBS peak and likely 135 profit-taking targets. But unless Wednesday's FOMC and the bond market take more notice of \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 's current conditions index plunge to a record low and expectations to its lowest since 1980, sharply higher Treasury-JGB yields spreads should keep USD/JPY rising. Though coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the yen is highly unlikely, the record 43.3% rise in yen-based import prices could force more threats and eventually \uc2 BOJ\uc2  tinkering with its yield curve control bands if core-core CPI gets above 2% on a sustained basis. Charts: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3NKqDWo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3NKqDWo}}}\uc2  & {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3xAYWd1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3xAYWd1}}}\uc2  US inflation surprise leaves EUR/USD at risk of visiting EUR/USD fell on Friday, piercing the 50% Fibo of the 1.0349-1.0787 rally on its way to a 16-session low after a U.S. CPI report showed no signs of moderating inflation, with Fed policy expectations and global growth concerns likely to inspire further declines that could test this year's low. Investors drove U.S. rates sharply higher as they lifted their expectations for the terminal Fed rate. The more hawkish Fed view bolsters expectations that other major central banks will follow suit, raising the probability of slower global growth, which the euro is typically positively correlated with. Key barometers of global growth highlight the concerns. CPI data helped  U.S. 2s-10s yield curve break key support and flatten sharply to levels not seen since early April. Emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened broadly against the dollar as higher rates have a greater negative impact on EM GDP. China's yuan struck a 10-session low against the dollar due to growth concerns. Falling daily and monthly RSIs, breaking the 50% and 61.8% Fib of 1.0349-1.0787 are tech signs that reinforce downside risks that could lead to a test of EUR/USD's 2022 low struck in May. Charts: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3QeCGNh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3QeCGNh}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3tsKYY4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3tsKYY4}}}\uc2  & {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/39dNUkC"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/39dNUkC}}}\uc2  Chart of the Day (GBP/USD) GBP/USD fell below 1.24 to three-week lows after above-forecast U.S. inflation increased expectations of a more hawkish Fed, greater divergence with the \uc2 BoE\uc2  and downward pressure on cable that could test 2022 lows by 1.2156. With little joy on the relative rate front and Brexit uncertainties also rising, GBP/USD is likely to remain capped by recent highs near 1.26. Its break on Friday below 1.2411, the 50% Fib of May's 1.2156-1.2667 range, shifts momentum away from GBP bulls, opening the way for a test of the May 17 low by 1.2321 and eventually the 2022 lows by 1.2156. Next week's \uc2 BoE\uc2  meeting is gaining significance as traders await guidance on the UK \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's approach to its growth-inflation conundrum. The rise in headline U.S. annual inflation to 8.6% from 8.3% -- and the more hawkish Fed outlook it implies -- adds to sterling bulls' troubles stemming from EU-UK tensions over Northern Ireland. Britain will present legislation to parliament on Monday to unilaterally revise the post-Brexit trade arrangements for Northern Ireland, raising the risk of a trade war and a new clash with Brussels. Chart: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/39eKTAw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/39eKTAw}}}\par \par \par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-6-13T19:33:57.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220610ei6a03bwp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
