\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks slide, dollar gains on hot U.S. inflation data\b0\par\par\uc2 832 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2022\par 22:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds close of U.S. market)\par \par \uc2 * CPI surges 8.6% year-on-year, largest gain since 1981\par \par \uc2 * Hot U.S. inflation seek keeping Fed hawkish\par \par \uc2 * Short-end yields jump on hot CPI data\par \par \uc2 * S&P 500 again nears bear market confirmation\par \par \uc2 By Herbert Lash\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, June 10 (Reuters) - Global equity markets slumped and the dollar strengthened on Friday after a bigger-than-expected U.S. inflation spike in May raised concerns the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  may tighten policy for too long and cause a sharp slowdown.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. consumer price index increased 8.6% last month, the largest year-on-year increase since December 1981, the Labor Department said. Economists polled by Reuters had expected CPI to rise 8.3% annually.\par \par \uc2 Many economists and market participants expected the data to show inflation had peaked, but gasoline prices hit a record high, the cost of food soared and rental prices surged.\par \par \uc2 "It was pretty hot. This report suggests that underlying inflation pressures remain quite strong," said Aichi Amemiya, senior U.S. economist at \uc2 Nomura\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose to a near four-week high against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  prices tumbled and short- and intermediate-dated yields hit their highest levels in more than a decade. Two-year yields, which are highly sensitive to rate hikes, spiked to 3.065%, the highest since June 2008.\par \par \uc2 Stocks on Wall Street and in Europe fell more than 2% as investors feared \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts to control inflation would be so harsh it would slow growth and squeeze corporate earnings.\par \par \uc2 The pan-European STOXX 600 index fell 2.69% and MSCI's gauge of global equity markets shed 2.79%.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 2.73%, the S&P 500 lost 2.91% and the Nasdaq Composite dropped 3.52%. The three indices posted their biggest weekly declines since January, tumbling roughly 5% each.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 is now down more than 18% from its Jan. 3 record closing peak, a drop that again puts it near to confirming a bear market as defined by a 20% drop on a closing-price basis.\par \par \uc2 The stronger-than-expected CPI data has changed the calculus for what the Fed does in September after "most assuredly" raising rates 50 basis points next week and in July, said \uc2 Art Hogan\uc2 , chief market strategist at National Securities.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  and Jefferies now expect the Fed to deliver its first 75 basis point increase in 28 years next week.\par \par \uc2 Fed funds futures traders expect the Fed's benchmark rate to increase to 3.69% next May, from 0.83% now.\par \par \uc2 The Fed still has a chance of engineering a softer landing as there's mounting evidence a slowdown is happening, said Rhys Williams, chief strategist at Spouting Rock Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "At least in the goods economy, there are signs that demand is really slowing," Williams said. "Houses are on the market much longer, auto sales are not so hot and shipping rates have collapsed coming from Asia to here."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  HINTS AT YEN \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Concerns also mounted about demand and growth in China, the world's second-largest economy, after Shanghai and Beijing imposed new COVID-19 lockdown restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The yen rose off 20-year lows after Japanese policymakers made rare comments about its weakness. Japan's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said they were concerned by recent sharp falls in the yen in a rare joint statement, the strongest warning to date that Tokyo could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen has been plumbing 20-year lows against the dollar and seven-year troughs against the euro on expectations the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  will continue to lag other major central banks in exiting its stimulus policy.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen later weakened 0.05% at 134.41 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index rose 0.852%, with the euro down 0.9% to $1.0519.\par \par \uc2 Overnight in Asia, MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan fell 0.9%.\par \par \uc2 Continued strong buying by foreign investors and cautious hopes of regulatory easing on tech firms lifted China stocks, despite lockdown alerts.\par \par \uc2 China's blue-chip CSI300 index was up 1.5%, while Hong Kong shares trimmed earlier losses to end off 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices fell on concerns rising prices will force consumers to cut demand, and as China imposed new COVID-19 lockdown measures.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude futures fell 84 cents to settle at $120.67 a barrel, and Brent settled down $1.06 at $122.01.\par \par \uc2 Gold prices rebounded strongly in volatile trade as focus turned to economic risks of elevated inflation.\par \par \uc2 U.S. gold futures settled up 1.2% to $1,875.50 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Herbert Lash; Additional reporting by Carolyn Cohn in London, Shreyashi Sanyal in Bengaluru; Editing by Chizu Nomiyama, Kirsten Donovan, Alex Richardson and Cynthia Osterman) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-6-10T21:34:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220610ei6a03h01\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
