\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Japanese yen jumps after authorities sound  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  warning\b0\par\par\uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2022\par 13:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Rare joint statement from \uc2 BOJ\uc2  and govt on yen\par \par \uc2 * Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounces off 20-year lows vs dlr\par \par \uc2 * Euro/dollar heads lower before U.S. CPI data (Updates with latest prices)\par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 10 (Reuters) - The Japanese yen jumped on Friday after the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a rare joint statement expressed concern about its recent slide to hit two-decade lows, while the dollar edged higher ahead of key U.S. inflation data.\par \par \uc2 After a meeting with his \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) counterpart, the country's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Masato Kanda told reporters that Tokyo will take appropriate action as needed, a sign \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be edging closer to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in a bid to arrest the yen's declines.\par \par \uc2 The yen has been battered by traders wagering that the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  will stick with its ultra-accommodative policy stance just as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and other central banks accelerate their tightening to tame soaring inflation.\par \par \uc2 "Generally if you look back at \uc2 BOJ\uc2  behaviour, if they say they will take appropriate action that is seen as the top level of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  they go through before physical \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So, they have gone as far as they can go for now," \uc2 Adam Cole\uc2 , chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 , said.\par \par \uc2 The latest comments "should be a lid on dollar/yen in the short term," Cole added, but "in the long term, there's little that they can do as it flies in the face of the policy stance".\par \par \uc2 The yen rallied to as strong as 133.37 yen per dollar , up 0.7% on the session, before settling at 133.79. On Thursday it plumbed as weak as 134.56, levels last seen in early 2002.\par \par \uc2 The yen also rallied versus the euro, with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last down 0.8% at 141.65 yen.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere on Friday the dollar inched higher as traders braced for U.S. inflation data that should guide the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's policy tightening path.\par \par \uc2 U.S. core consumer price growth is expected to cool a fraction. This could give the Fed some wiggle room to raise rates less aggressively later in the year as it tries to rein in inflation without tipping the economy into recession.\par \par \uc2 Markets expect the Fed next week to announce the second of its three consecutive 50-basis-point interest rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which measures the greenback against six peers, rose 0.2% to 103.52 but was still up this week as growth concerns encouraged investors to seek safety in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "For the U.S. dollar to become even more overvalued it will likely require some combination of intensifying global growth concerns and/or another significant hawkish repricing of Fed rate hike expectations," \uc2 MUFG\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The euro weakened, which followed a fall on Thursday after the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  said it would end quantitative easing on July 1, then raise interest rates by 25 basis points on July 21. It flagged a bigger rate increase in September.\par \par \uc2 The euro was last down 0.3% at $1.0587 having touched $1.0611 early in the session, its lowest since May 23.\par \par \uc2 The Norwegian crown gained, helped by the Norwegian statistics office reporting that the country's economy was growing faster than expected this year and inflation accelerating. The crown traded up 0.4% at 10.149 per euro .\par \par \uc2 The risk-sensitive Australian dollar climbed 0.4% to $0.7125 but was still down on the week, hurt by declines in equity markets. Sterling dropped 0.4% to $1.2441.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tommy Wilkes Additional reporting by Alun John and Dhara Ranasinghe; Editing by Frank Jack Daniel, David Evans and Andrew Heavens) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-6-10T12:39:40.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220610ei6a01v7i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
