\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-World stocks hit two-week lows on inflation jitters, policymakers boost yen\b0\par\par\uc2 844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2022\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * World stocks eye 2% loss for week\par \par \uc2 * Dollar drops 0.64% vs yen\par \par \uc2 * S&P futures up 0.19%, European stocks down 1.1%\par \par \uc2 By Carolyn Cohn and Stella Qiu\par \par \uc2 LONDON/BEIJING, June 10 (Reuters) - World stocks hit a two-week low on Friday as rate hike guidance from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and jitters over upcoming U.S. inflation data stoked concerns about global growth, while verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted the yen\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ECB\uc2  said on Thursday it would deliver its first interest rate rise since 2011 next month, followed by a potentially larger move in September.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at Deutsche and \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  lifted their euro zone rate hike forecasts on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Investors expect the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates by 50 basis points next week, especially if U.S. consumer price data on Friday confirms elevated inflation.\par \par \uc2 The consensus forecast sees a year-over-year inflation rate for May of 8.3%, unchanged from April.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of focus is on this current number, it's not going to be a big move," said Matthias Scheiber, global head of portfolio management for multi-asset solutions at Allspring.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think it will derail what central banks currently have on their minds."\par \par \uc2 However, rate rises may hit growth, Scheiber said, adding that he had turned slightly underweight on equities in recent weeks as a result of this concern.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's world equity index fell 0.22% to its lowest since May 26, and was heading for a 2% fall for the week.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock index futures ticked up 0.19% after the S&P 500 and Nasdaq fell more than 2% on Thursday in their biggest daily percentage declines since mid-May.\par \par \uc2 European stocks fell 1.1% to three-week lows.\par \par \uc2 It was the 17th week in a row of outflows for European equities in the week to Wednesday, according to \uc2 BofA\uc2 , with $2.1 billion leaving the space, as the sector has been hit hard by the Russia-Ukraine war.\par \par \uc2 Britain's FTSE 100 fell 0.75% to 2-1/2 week lows.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  said on Friday it was satisfied that Britain's top banks could be shut down without putting at risk the stability of the financial system or disrupting customers, but it found shortcomings at Lloyds, \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  and \uc2 HSBC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Pressure is rising on other central banks to tighten, with the \uc2 BoE\uc2  and \uc2 Sweden's Riksbank\uc2  expected to hike rates again next week.\par \par \uc2 "There\u8217\'20\'19s currently a sense that inflation may be peaking, but that only applies to goods, and a better image is of rolling inflationary waves, as supply/demand pinch points shift," said Kit Juckes, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell 0.64% to 133.48 yen after Japan's government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement they were "concerned" about recent sharp yen declines and stood ready to respond as needed on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 The yen has been ploughing 20-year lows against the dollar and seven-year troughs against the euro on expectations the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) will continue to lag behind other major central banks in exiting stimulus policy.\par \par \uc2 The dollar eased 0.18% against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pulling away from its highest level in three weeks set in the previous session. The euro was steady at $1.0622.\par \par \uc2 The two-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yield, which rises with traders' expectations of higher Fed fund rates, continued its climb to hover around the highest level since early May. It touched 2.8352% compared with a U.S. close of 2.817%.\par \par \uc2 The yield on benchmark 10-year Treasury notes dipped to 3.0401% compared with its U.S. close of 3.042%.\par \par \uc2 Ten-year \uc2 German government\uc2  bond yields inched lower to 1.425%, after hitting their highest since 2014 on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan fell 0.9%, weighed down by a 1.2% drop in resources-heavy Australia and a 1.1% retreat in South Korea. Japan's Nikkei fell 1.5%.\par \par \uc2 However, continued strong buying by foreign investors and cautious hopes of regulatory easing on tech firms lifted China stocks, despite news that the cities of Beijing and Shanghai were back on COVID-19 alert.\par \par \uc2 China's blue-chip CSI300 index was up 1.5%, while Hong Kong shares trimmed earlier losses to be off 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices slipped but remained within touching distance of three-month highs as fears over new COVID-19 lockdown measures in Shanghai outweighed solid demand for fuels in the United States, the world's top consumer.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude dipped 0.52% to $120.88 a barrel. Brent crude fell 0.5% to $122.45 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold eased 0.12% to $1845.73 per ounce.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Alun John in Hong Kong; Editing by Bradley Perrett, Kim Coghill, Elaine Hardcastle) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-6-10T09:23:29.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220610ei6a0141p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
