\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-Inflation-shy investors try on \u8220\'20\'1Cleast dirty shirt\u8221\'20\'1D\b0\par\par\uc2 1,100 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2022\par 14:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are his own.)\par \par \uc2 By Edward Chancellor\par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 9 (Reuters Breakingviews) - Interest rates across the developed world look set to remain below the level of inflation for years to come. This prolonged bout of financial repression should help pay down the excessive debts that have accumulated in recent decades. But domestic savers will pay this inflation tax unless they can take their capital abroad to traditional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  safe havens, such as Switzerland. This time, emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may play an unlikely role in protecting investors against the ravages of inflation.\par \par \uc2 During the 1970s, interest rates in Europe and the United States trailed the annual increase in the price level. The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets rewarded countries with the soundest monetary policies. The best-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in that era \u8211\'20\'13 the Swiss franc and Deutschmark \u8211\'20\'13 were issued by countries with relatively stable prices. Inflation-ravaged Britain, by comparison, saw the pound slide.\par \par \uc2 History appears to be repeating itself. Once again, short-term interest rates across the developed world are failing to keep pace with inflation. Once again, the British pound is looking wobbly, having fallen by more than 7% against the U.S. dollar since the beginning of the year. The Swiss franc has appreciated while the greenback has also put in a strong performance.\par \par \uc2 The trouble is that both the Swiss and American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  now look overvalued. At the start of this year, they ranked first and third, respectively, on\u8239\'20\'2FThe Economist\u8217\'20\'19s\u8239\'20\'2FBig Mac Index, which compares the price of \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s hamburgers around the world and is widely used as a proxy for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s fair value. Strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have made the exports of Switzerland and the United States less competitive.\u8239\'20\'2F\par \par \uc2 Monetary authorities won\u8217\'20\'19t allow this process to continue indefinitely. In 1985, the United States arranged the international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreement, known as the Plaza Accord, to bring down the value of the dollar. During the last decade, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  devoted a lot of effort to preventing the country\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating against the euro.\u8239\'20\'2FBesides, the United States is not an obvious safe haven from financial repression. Its non-financial debt is close to an all-time high as a proportion of GDP, while the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's benchmark interest rate trails current U.S. inflation by a massive 7 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Japan\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks to be a potentially more interesting bet. Since the start of this year, the yen has fallen sharply against the dollar as the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  hasn\u8217\'20\'19t yet followed its developed-market peers in tightening monetary policy. However, investors may be overreacting to small relative movements in nominal interest rates. Over the long run, real interest rates are what matter. Here Japan has an advantage: its inflation rate is close to 2%, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s short-term interest rate is minus 0.1%. That means that real short-term rates are far less negative than in the United States. The Japanese bond market\u8217\'20\'19s inflation expectations over the next decade are also somewhat lower than in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Nor is the yen overvalued. According to Japan expert\u8239\'20\'2FPeter Tasker {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.petertasker.asia/articles/finance/how-the-weak-yen-will-re-energize-japan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.petertasker.asia/articles/finance/how-the-weak-yen-will-re-energize-japan}}}\uc2 , the trade-weighted value of the yen has fallen by more than half in real terms from its peak in 1995, making Japanese exports highly competitive. This gives the yen room to appreciate before the authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Still, given \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s level of public sector debt \u8211\'20\'13 the world\u8217\'20\'19s highest at over 220% of GDP \u8211\'20\'13 the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  may have trouble hiking rates aggressively if inflation takes hold.\par \par \uc2 The term financial repression was originally coined in the 1970s to describe the habit of authorities across much of the developing world of keeping interest rates well below inflation. Stanford economist Ronald McKinnon associated financial repression with the state control of credit, the discouragement of personal savings and lacklustre economic growth. Now the developed and developing world are trading places.\par \par \uc2 As the West embraces financial repression, central bankers in emerging markets appear more aware of how easily confidence is lost in the battle against inflation. Look at Argentina\u8217\'20\'19s most recent financial crisis in 2019, which was accompanied by soaring prices, a collapsing peso and sharply rising interest rates. This helps explain why central bankers in many emerging markets are responding much more vigorously to the return of inflation.\par \par \uc2 For instance, Brazil\u8217\'20\'19s policy rate has risen from 2% early last year to 12.75% today \u8211\'20\'13 a figure slightly above the latest inflation print. As interest rates have climbed more steeply in emerging markets than elsewhere, the real yield on a basket of bonds issued by the major debt issuers - Brazil, Mexico, South Africa and Indonesia - has reached a 15-year high relative to the post-inflation yield on \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bonds.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of these large emerging markets also appear relatively undervalued, having fallen by around 30% in the 10 years to the end of 2021. A Big Mac sold by \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  in Jakarta costs a third as much as its equivalent in Zurich. Thanks to a marked improvement in export competitiveness, emerging markets are now enjoying their largest current account surpluses in 15 years, according to the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s widely assumed that emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  underperform when the United States tightens monetary policy. That was the case in the mid-1990s and during the more recent \u8220\'20\'1Ctaper tantrum\u8221\'20\'1D of 2013. But in the early years of this century, emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  performed well even as the Fed raised rates. This year, their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have rallied even as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has hiked. Besides, emerging markets are now less dependent on overseas borrowing and are better able to fund their borrowing requirements in local markets.\par \par \uc2 Russia\u8217\'20\'19s recent expulsion from the global financial markets is a grim reminder of the dangers of investing in emerging-market bonds. Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s ongoing financial crisis reinforces this message. But in a world of financial repression, the game is to find what \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders call the \u8220\'20\'1Cleast dirty shirt.\u8221\'20\'1D Emerging-market linen may not be spotless, but it\u8217\'20\'19s a lot cleaner than some.\par \par \uc2 Follow @Breakingviews {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Breakingviews"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/Breakingviews}}}\uc2  on \uc2 Twitter\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS\par \par \uc2 Edward Chancellor\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1CThe Price of Time: The Real Story of Interest\u8221\'20\'1D will be published by Penguin on July 7. (Editing by Peter Thal Larsen and Streisand Neto)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-6-12T12:02:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220609ei6901w7l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
