\par\par \b \uc2 Same-old, same-old, Japan sticks to  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  stance even as yen slide steepens\b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2022\par 14:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Reuters) -Japanese policymakers held fast to their usual line on yen weakness on Wednesday, stating that rapid moves were undesirable, but confounded expectations they may escalate warnings about the sliding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it fell to fresh 20-year lows.\par \par \uc2 "Various macro models suggest (the weak yen) is a plus to the economy," as long as its moves are stable, \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  told parliament on Wednesday, reiterating his stance.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  said in parliament that the weak yen has both positives and negatives for the economy, although it could be negative if wages remain stagnant, which would mean potential harm to households because of higher costs of living.\par \par \uc2 The yen weakened beyond 133 to the dollar <JPY=EBS> on Wednesday and slumped to a seven-year trough below 143 against the euro , with an expected rate move by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to leave Japan as the sole major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sticking to an ultra-easy monetary policy in the face of surging inflation.\par \par \uc2 Speculation in financial markets has lingered that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may somehow \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to arrest the yen weakness, which is pushing up import prices and households' cost of living, by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars - a switch from its traditional stance of trying to weaken the yen.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since it sought to tame a surge in the yen after a devasting earthquake and tsunami in March 2011.\par \par \uc2 In the past, policymakers have preceded any action in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by escalating their verbal warnings, which have drawn close attention from the markets.\par \par \uc2 "There's no clear threshold as to when policymakers may escalate warning against weak yen. They may wait until it tops 140 yen," said Daisuke Karakama, chief market economist at \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "With voters' support staying high, policymakers appear complacent. Therefore they did not want Kuroda to say something unnecessary to rock the boat."\par \par \uc2 Kuroda was on the defensive on Wednesday over a remark he made earlier this week that Japanese households were becoming more accepting of higher prices.\par \par \uc2 He retracted the comment in his appearance before parliament, after drawing criticism for apparent insensitivity to the impact of higher living costs for consumers.\par \par \uc2 "My expression that households are becoming more accepting of price hikes was not appropriate at all, so I withdraw it," he said.\par \par \uc2 "What's most important is for firms with better profits from the weak yen to boost capital expenditures and raise wages, to drive a positive cycle of greater incomes leading to more spending."\par \par \uc2 Kuroda said moves in the dollar may not be affected much by U.S. rate hikes unless they are stronger than expected, according to a recording of an online interview by the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto and Daniel Leussink; Editing by Jacqueline Wong, Edmund Klamann and Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-6-8T12:57:24.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220608ei6801tl6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
