20\'22 ANALYSIS-Food inflation pain puts emerging markets between rock and hard place Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 06:00 GB Apr Claimant Count -42.5k f\u8217\'20\'19cast, prev -46.9k \u8226\'20\'22 06:00 GB Mar ILO Unemployment 3.8% f\u8217\'20\'19cast, prev 3.8%; change +5k, prev +10k \u8226\'20\'22 09:00 EZ Q1 GDP Flash QQ 0.2% f\u8217\'20\'19cast, 0.2% prev; YY 5.0% f\u8217\'20\'19cast, 5.0% prev \u8226\'20\'22 09:00 EZ Q1 Employment Flash QQ 0.5% f\u8217\'20\'19cast, 0.5% prev; YY 2.7% f\u8217\'20\'19cast, 2.2% prev Looking Ahead - Events, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 06:30 ECB Enria speaks at Institut Montaigne in Paris \u8226\'20\'22 10:05 BoE DepGov Cunliffe speaks to WSJ \u8226\'20\'22 11:15 \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  DepGov Ohlsson speaks at \uc2 Uppsala University\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 13:15 \uc2 Philadelphia Fed\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Harker speech in Newark \u8226\'20\'22 17:00 ECB Pres Lagarde speaks at Darmstadt event Commentary and Analysis USD/JPY boosted by flows, official-speak, AUD/JPY leads \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY, JPY crosses and especially AUD/JPY see good bounces early \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY from 128.85 EBS early Asia to 129.35, some more to 129.44 in PM \u8226\'20\'22 Combination of importer demand into Tokyo fix, offshore hedge fund buys \u8226\'20\'22 Surges in some JPY crosses helped AUD/JPY especially but CAD, MXN too \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  off'l-speak helped, BoJ policy on hold, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  not eyed \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY hedge fund bids still @128.70-80, offers 129.30-50 \u8226\'20\'22 Many eyeing range-trade for now, 128.50-129.50 or 129.00-130.00 \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/JPY surges on more hawkish \uc2 RBA\uc2 , 89.74 to 90.69, away from recent lows \u8226\'20\'22 Bounce in commodities prices help, as some concerns over China growth abate \u8226\'20\'22 CAD/JPY 100.25-100.89, MXN/JPY 6.4303-6.4644, track away from recent lows \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY 134.55 to 135.15 and GBP/JPY 158.73 to 159.99 EUR/USD-Steady in Asia as settling markets weigh on USD \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD opened +0.22% at 1.0435 as USD eased against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 Equity markets were firm in Asia with the AXJ index rising 1.4% \u8226\'20\'22 USD weakened against risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while the EUR/USD was steady \u8226\'20\'22 It traded in a 1.0429/55 range and is 1.1440/45 into the afternoon \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD trending lower with the 5. 10 and 21-day MAs in a bearish alignment \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is at 10-day MA @ 1.0498 and break would warn momentum is slowing \u8226\'20\'22 Support @ yesterday's 1.0384 low and break would increase downward pressure AUD/USD-Moves higher on China optimism and hawkish \uc2 RBA\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened +0.49% at 0.6971 after AUD outperformed in USD weakness \u8226\'20\'22 It moved higher in early Asia and traded up to 0.6991 before \uc2 RBA\uc2  minutes \u8226\'20\'22 It was around 0.6980 when \uc2 RBA\uc2  minutes were viewed as hawkish \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD broke above 0.7000 to 0.7010 before easing back again \u8226\'20\'22 It is trading around 0.6995 heading into the afternoon session \u8226\'20\'22 AUD benefiting from hopes China will end lockdowns to bolster economy \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD briefly broke above 10-day MA at 0.7003 \u8226\'20\'22 A close above that reading would suggest a shift in momentum \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is at the 21-day MA at 0.7105 and break would suggest a bottom is in place \u8226\'20\'22 Bids tipped at 0.6965/70 and break below 0.6960 would ease upward pressure GBP/USD-Resilient at key resistance, as USD strength stalls \u8226\'20\'22 +0.1% in a 1.2321-1.2343 range with occasional flurries of interest on D3 \u8226\'20\'22 UK moving towards trade conflict with \uc2 EU\uc2  on N. Ireland protocol \u8226\'20\'22 PM Johnson's concept of 'sovereignty' potentially very expensive for the UK \u8226\'20\'22 UK employment data leads event risk in London - polls, claimant count -42.5 \u8226\'20\'22 Charts; momentum studies base, 10 & 21 day and week moving averages slide \u8226\'20\'22 21 day Bollinger bands contract - bearish bias holds - needs soft close \u8226\'20\'22 1.2333 10 DMA tested early May - under pressure, 1.2531 21 DMA pivotal \u8226\'20\'22 Long term support at 1.2082, 76.4% 2020-2021 rise major support AUD/USD may be basing as market looks past China slowdown The Australian dollar may be forming a base after trending lower for the past five weeks. The price action following China's dismal monthly activity data on Monday might be a starting point for an AUD/USD recovery. Much weaker-than-expected Chinese retail sales and industrial output for April laid bare the deleterious impact of Shanghai's lockdown on the economy. The AUD/USD fell 0.80% to 0.6873 in a knee-jerk reaction to the data before reversing higher to close at 0.6971 - up 0.49% from Friday's close. What's more, the AUD ended Monday higher against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  The stunning turnaround was mainly driven by the view that China is finally taking steps to exit the lockdown and once ended, the pent-up demand will spur a strong bounce in China's data in the coming months.The commodity market appeared to have come to the same conclusion, as Dalian iron futures gained over 2.0%, while oil and copper also closed higher on the day. With the worst-case scenarios already baked into the AUD/USD price, the bar is set low for bargain-hunters following a 10.8% decline since April 5. Resistance at the 10-day moving average at 0.7003 was briefly breached following hawkish \uc2 RBA\uc2  minutes Tuesday and a close above that level would warn the downtrend has lost momentum. A break above the 21-DMA at 0.7105 would confirm a bottom is in place. USD/JPY in consolidation mode between May high and low USD/JPY appears to be consolidating after coming off from 131.35 on May 9 to a 127.54 low on May 12. Tokyo players expect a core range of either 128.50-129.50 or 129.00-130.00. USD/JPY moves lower continue to attract demand from Tokyo. Japanese importers and some speculative players remain in dip-buying mode, the former faced with higher raw materials prices and costs stemming from supply disruptions. Energy prices have risen significantly too, adding to demand from this bloc. Helping to cap USD/JPY are sales from Japanese exporters and specs looking to pick a top. Moves from this latter group appear closely tied to the ups and downs of the U.S. dollar (safe-haven flows, paring of longs), which is in turn driven by \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  expectations and gyrations in U.S. yields U.S. yields have come off recent highs and look to consolidate ahead of more inflation data and fresh hints of Fed policy. The 10-year Treasury yield has retreated from a 3.203% high on May 9 and is currently consolidating below 3%. The market remains extremely short JPY, as reflected by IMM positioning data, but this will not deter fresh Japanese USD/JPY buys given importer demand and as investors opt to take advantage of Japan-U.S. interest rate differentials at their highest since 2018, especially if U.S. yields head even higher. \uc2 RBI\uc2  walks tight rope in push to contain INR volatility The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  will find its work cut out in its efforts to limit volatility in the rupee while simultaneously attempting to manage government bond yields, fight runaway inflation and sustain India's nascent economic recovery. The INR traded to a record low of 77.63 last Thursday and appears set for further weakness. Oil prices at near two-month peaks, high inflation worldwide, concerns about a global slowdown amid tightening monetary conditions, risk aversion, a broadly stronger U.S. dollar and a weakening yuan will continue to exert downward pressure on the INR. The \uc2 RBI\uc2  is ostensibly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit "jerky" movements but in reality appears keen to control excessive INR depreciation to avoid imported inflation. India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have declined for nine straight weeks and hit a one-year low as of May 6. With consumer price inflation reaching eight-year highs, the \uc2 RBI\uc2  could be forced into raising rates aggressively, jeopardising a still fragile economic recovery and undermining the rupee further. A government source told Reuters the \uc2 RBI\uc2  will use various instruments to help lower bond yields, which have hit their highest since 2019. This could complicate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of withdrawing liquidity from the market and is at odds with its shift towards a more realistic tightening stance. With the \uc2 RBI\uc2  engaged in a difficult juggling act, buying USD/INR dips towards 77.00-10 looks prudent. To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-5-20T05:39:27.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220517ei5h00p8d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
