\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen set to snap nine weeks of losses, dollar holds firm\b0\par\par\uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2022\par 07:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, May 13 (Reuters) - The yen is poised to snap a nine-week losing streak on Friday on weakening risk sentiment, while another Wall Street selloff drove flight-to-safety bids to the dollar, which held near 20-year peaks.\par \par \uc2 The dollar regained a little ground on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and was last at 128.9 per dollar after hitting a two-week low of 127.5 overnight.\par \par \uc2 However, the dollar is still down 1.2% against the yen this week, its first week of declines since early March. The euro has fallen 2.6% versus the yen this week, its biggest drop also since early March as the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a major victim of the "risk off" mood.\par \par \uc2 "The yen is perhaps the most obvious signal of a shift from a world where yields were dominant and risk was resilient (yen negative), to a world this week where the dominant force is sour risk appetite driving yields lower (yen positive)," said \uc2 Alan Ruskin\uc2 , macro strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in a note.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark U.S. 10-year yield was 2.8877% on Friday, slightly higher, but still down sharply from Monday's high of 3.203%.\par \par \uc2 Rising U.S. yields at a time when the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep Japanese benchmark yields pinned down caused the yen to soften this year.\par \par \uc2 Investors are continuing to move towards safe-haven assets fearing \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate hikes to constrain inflation could hit global economic growth while MSCI's gauge of stocks around the world fell to its lowest overnight since November 2020.\par \par \uc2 Asian shares and U.S. futures edged a little higher on Friday, but analysts saw little sign of a broader recovery.\par \par \uc2 Investors are still assessing how aggressive the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's policy path will be after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its benchmark overnight interest rate by 50 basis points last week, the largest hike in 22 years.\par \par \uc2 Expectations are completely priced in for another hike of at least 50 basis points at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's June meeting, according to \uc2 CME\uc2 's FedWatch Tool.\par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.0394, up 0.16%, holding above its 2017 low of $1.034.\par \par \uc2 The weak euro kept the dollar index at 104.63, just off its overnight 20-year peak of 104.92.\par \par \uc2 Sterling hunkered down at $1.2221, hurt after data Thursday showed Britain's economy unexpectedly shrank in March, and the Aussie dollar was also bruised at $0.6886.\par \par \uc2 Crypto markets were steadier on Friday after a week of turmoil, as the risk-off mood combined with the spectacular collapse of stable coin TerraUSD.\par \par \uc2 The sell-off has taken the combined market value of all cryptocurrencies to $1.2 trillion, less than half of where it was last November, based on data from CoinMarketCap, and sent bitcoin to as low as $25,401.05 on Thursday, its lowest level since Dec. 28, 2020.\par \par \uc2 But things were calmer in early trading on Friday with bitcoin up 5.3%, trading around $30,400.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Sam Holmes and Jacqueline Wong) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-5-13T06:42:57.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220513ei5d00m2h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
