\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Market nerves prop up safe-haven dollar, yen\b0\par\par\uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2022\par 03:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Alun John\par     HONG KONG, May 13 (Reuters) - The yen held most of its\par overnight gains on Friday, after falling U.S. yields and market\par jitters propped up the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  while another Wall\par Street selloff drove flight-so-safety bid to dollar, which\par remains near 20-year high peaks.\par     The yen was at 129.14 per dollar on Friday morning,\par softening on the day after it had reached a two-week peak of\par 127.5 overnight.\par     Thursday's 1.2% decline for dollar/yen was its biggest daily\par percentage fall this year. The euro/yen cross declined 2.5%, its\par biggest daily percentage fall since 2016 as the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par a victim of the "risk off" mood.\par     "The yen is perhaps the most obvious signal of a shift from\par a world where yields were dominant and risk was resilient (yen\par negative), to a world this week where the dominant force is sour\par risk appetite driving yields lower (yen positive)," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alan\par Ruskin\f28 \uc2 \f2 , macro strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a note.\par     The benchmark U.S. 10-year yield was 2.8822%\par having declined each session this week from Monday's high of\par 3.203%.\par     Rising U.S. yields at a time when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep Japanese benchmark yields pinned down caused\par the yen to soften this year. \par     Investors are continuing to move towards safe-haven assets\par fearing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate hikes to constrain inflation could hit\par global economic growth while MSCI's gauge of stocks around the\par world fell to its lowest level overnight since\par November 2020.\par     After the Fed raised its benchmark overnight interest rate\par by 50 basis points last week, the largest hike in 22 years,\par investors are assessing how aggressive the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy\par path will be.\par     Expectations are completely priced in for another hike of at\par least 50 basis points at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's June meeting,\par according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 CME\f28 \uc2 \f2 's FedWatch Tool.\par     The euro was at $1.038 approaching its 2017 low of\par $1.034. A break past that would be its lowest in nearly 20\par years. \par     The weak euro kept the dollar index at 104.75, just\par off its overnight 20-year peak of 104.92.\par     Sterling hunkered down at $1.2206, and the Aussie\par dollar was also bruised at $0.6887.\par     Crypto markets were steadier on Friday after a week of\par turmoil, as the risk-off mood combined with the spectacular\par collapse of stable coin TerraUSD.\par     The sell-off has taken the combined market value of all\par cryptocurrencies to $1.2 trillion, less than half of where it\par was last November, based on data from CoinMarketCap, and sent\par bitcoin to as low as $25,401.05 on Thursday, its lowest level\par since Dec. 28, 2020. \par     But things were calmer in early trading on Friday with\par bitcoin up 1.73% trading around $29,400. \par     \par     \par     ========================================================\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 0124 GMT\par  Description      RIC         Last           U.S. Close  Pct Change     YTD Pct     High Bid    Low Bid\par                   