\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ\uc2  rules out widening yield band to stem yen fall\b0\par\par\uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2022\par 11:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has no plan to allow long-term interest rates to move more widely around its 0% target to stem sharp falls in the yen, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  executive said on Tuesday, saying such a move would be tantamount to a rate hike.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  currently offers to buy unlimited amounts of 10-year Japanese government bonds (JGB) to defend an implicit 0.25% cap set around its 0% interest rate target, maintaining a dovish policy as part of efforts to reflate the economy.\par \par \uc2 However, steep falls in the yen as other economies eye rate hikes prompted market speculation the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  may widen the 50-basis-point band and tolerate further rises in long-term interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "We have no plan to widen the band," \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Executive Director \uc2 Shinichi Uchida\uc2  told parliament. "Doing so would be tantamount to an interest rate hike" and therefore undesirable for the economy, he added.\par \par \uc2 The remarks underscore the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's resolve to maintain its massive stimulus programme and keep ultra-loose interest rates, a stance that could further weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 The yen hit a fresh 20-year low of 131.34 per dollar on Monday, before recovering to around 130.10 on Tuesday, as the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  policy runs counter to a global shift toward monetary tightening. Central banks in the United States, Britain and Australia raised rates last week.\par \par \uc2 Still, Uchida said recent sharp moves in the yen were undesirable, a view echoed by Finance Minister Shunichi Suzuki.\par \par \uc2 "Stability is important and rapid moves as seen recently are undesirable," Suzuki told a briefing on Tuesday, repeating recent verbal warnings to keep sharp yen falls in check.\par \par \uc2 He said Japan will closely communicate with the United States and other \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  (G7) countries in any actions it takes in response to a rapidly weakening yen.\par \par \uc2 He declined to comment, when asked in parliament whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem yen falls.\par \par \uc2 G7 countries have agreed that exchange rates should be determined by markets, that disorderly movements can hurt economies, and that countries will consult on any actions they take in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Once welcomed for its boost to exports, a weak yen has emerged as a source of concern for Japanese policymakers as it inflates already rising import costs for fuel and raw material.\par \par \uc2 However, despite complaints by lawmakers, \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  has stressed a weak yen is beneficial for the economy and will not trigger a rate hike.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Leika Kihara and Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Sam Holmes and Richard Pullin) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-5-10T09:05:21.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220510ei5a01hs5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
