\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ\uc2  bolsters commitment to ultra-easy policy, triggers yen sell-off\b0\par\par\uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2022\par 14:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  on Thursday strengthened its commitment to keep interest rates ultra-low by vowing to buy unlimited amounts of bonds daily to defend its yield target, triggering a fresh sell-off in the yen and sending government bonds rallying.\par \par \uc2 Reinforcing its resolve to support a fragile economy even as sharp rises in raw material costs push up inflation, the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  maintained its ultra-loose monetary policy and a pledge to keep interest rates at "present or lower levels."\par \par \uc2 It also said it would buy unlimited amounts of 10-year government bonds to defend an implicit 0.25% cap around its zero yield target every market day, instead of on an ad-hoc basis.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's commitment to its zero-rate programme puts it at odds with other major economies that are shifting toward tighter monetary policy to combat surging prices, although inflation in Japan is expected to creep up towards the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2% target.\par \par \uc2 "The key announcement is the commitment to conducting fixed-rate \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  every day," said Bart Wakabayashi, co-branch manager at State Street Bank in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "I think they are trying to make the point here that we're ready to act at any second. They've quadrupled down on their commitment to this."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's strengthened commitment to keep policy accommodative drove the yen well below the psychological threshold of 130 to the dollar for the first time since 2002.\par \par \uc2 Yields on the benchmark 10-year Japanese government bonds slipped to a more than three-week low of 0.215%.\par \par \uc2 "We want to prevent Japan's long-term interest rates from rising in line with overseas bond yield increases," \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  told a news conference after the policy decision.\par \par \uc2 Kuroda said there was no change to his view that a weak yen benefits Japan's economy, a sign further falls in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely won't prompt the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  to tweak its ultra-easy policy.\par \par \uc2 But he warned that excess market volatility could hurt the economy by making it difficult for firms to set business plans.\par \par \uc2 An official of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry\uc2 , which decides whether to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told reporters Tokyo will take "appropriate" action as needed and warned that recent sharp yen moves were "extremely worrying".\par \par \uc2 As widely expected, the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  left unchanged its -0.1% target for short-term interest rates and a pledge to guide the 10-year bond yield around 0%.\par \par \uc2 RISING INFLATIONARY PRESSURE\par \par \uc2 In fresh quarterly forecasts, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  projected core consumer inflation to hit 1.9% in the current fiscal year before moderating to 1.1% in fiscal 2023 and 2024 - a sign it sees current cost-push price rises as transitory.\par \par \uc2 In a nod to rising inflationary pressure, however, the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  said wage and price increases were expected to broaden as the economy continues to recover.\par \par \uc2 "The rise in underlying inflation is likely to further push up medium- and long-term inflation expectations," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its quarterly outlook report.\par \par \uc2 But Kuroda said he did not expect conditions to fall in place for the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  to seek an exit from its loose policy in the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 BOJ\uc2  officials see developments in long-term price expectations as crucial to judging whether inflation becomes embedded and warrants withdrawing monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Core consumer inflation, which hit 0.8% in March, is set to accelerate to around 2% from April, though the rise will be driven largely by rising fuel costs and the dissipating effect of past cellphone fee cuts - rather than from higher wages, or underlying demand.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts say the markets could challenge the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's easy policy commitment.\par \par \uc2 "There's no sign at all that prices are stably going to rise to 2% so everyone is wondering whether it's really good to keep going as it is," said \uc2 Takeshi Minami\uc2 , chief economist at Norinchukin Research Institute in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "Markets could attack (the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's unlimited bond buying)," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Additional reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto, Daniel Leussink and Kantaro Komiya; Editing by Shri Navaratnam and Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-4-28T12:03:33.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220428ei4s00fid\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
