\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Markets Open - April 25\b0\par\par\uc2 2,133 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2022\par 21:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 EUR/USD by 2-year lows despite more \uc2 ECB\uc2  hike pricing, sterling slammed Market Briefs \u8226\'20\'22 Fed's Mester: I'd favor doing 50 bps hikes earlier on in rate hike path \u8226\'20\'22 Mester: shock of 75 bps rate hike is not needed \u8226\'20\'22 U.S.\par \par \uc2  services sector activity eases in April - survey \u8226\'20\'22 US Apr S&P Global Comp Flash PMI, 55.1, 57.7 prev \u8226\'20\'22 US Apr S&P Global Mfg PMI Flash, 59.7, 58.2 f'cast, 58.8 prev \u8226\'20\'22 US Apr S&P Global Svcs PMI Flash, 54.7, 58.0 f'cast, 58.0 prev \u8226\'20\'22 Traders price in over 85 bps of \uc2 ECB\uc2  rate hikes by year-end \u8226\'20\'22 Germany cuts 2022 economic growth forecast to 2.2% - source \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's Kuroda vows to 'persistently' continue aggressive monetary easing \u8226\'20\'22 Canada March producer prices up 4.0%, biggest monthly gain in 66 years \u8226\'20\'22 CA Mar Producer Prices MM, 4.0%, 3.1% prev, 2.6% r'vsd; YY, 18.5%, 16.4% prev, 15.8% r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 CA Feb Retail Sales MM, 0.1%, -0.4% f'cast, 3.2% prev, 3.3% r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 Kyiv accuses Moscow of 'imperialism' after general's southern Ukraine comment Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 24 Apr JP 23:50 Mar Service PPI, 1.10% prev \u8226\'20\'22 25 Apr JP 05:00 Feb Leading Indicator Revised, -1.6 prev Looking Ahead - Events, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 12:30 \uc2 Philadelphia Fed\uc2  issues Nonmanufacturing Business Outlook Survey for April \u8226\'20\'22 12:40 BoE's Sam Woods to speak at City Week 2022 'Developments in prudential regulation in the UK' \u8226\'20\'22 12:55 \uc2 BOC\uc2 's Timothy Lane participates in Financial Times Live's Crypto and Digital Assets Summit \u8226\'20\'22 15:00 \uc2 BOC\uc2 's \uc2 Tiff Macklem\uc2  Carolyn Rogers will testify at a parliamentary finance committee Macro Themes In Play \u8226\'20\'22 While EUR/USD sank back toward trend lows and USD/JPY eyed its trend highs, sterling's slide, amid a sour stew of weak UK data, major chart support losses and political uncertainty, suffered the most from dollar gains among the top \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the Fed still in the lead in expected policy tightening \u8226\'20\'22 Friday\u8217\'20\'19s poor UK data reinforced the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's forecast from Wednesday that British economic growth will slow sharply to the weakest of any major economy next year. That, as \uc2 BoE\uc2  Governor Andrew Bailey claimed the \uc2 BoE\uc2  can pull off a soft landing for UK economy \u8226\'20\'22 Meanwhile, euro zone April PMI data came in above forecast, helping to lift \uc2 ECB\uc2  rate hike expectations to new highs and 2-year Bund-Treasury yield spreads by 6bps to their highest since April 14, but that couldn't stop EUR/USD's drop toward April's 1.0758 trend lows \u8226\'20\'22 US ISM data was mixed, with services missing and manufacturing beating \u8226\'20\'22 The euro's case wasn't helped by a Reuters report that \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  told policymakers to hold back on dissenting views on decisions for several days after meetings \u8226\'20\'22 And Lagarde's comments about H2 policy tightening prospects didn't add much to Friday's existing surge in rate hike expectations, now priced at nearly 90bps and a year-end implied rate near 30bps \u8226\'20\'22 This week's 30bp rise in 2-year bund yields to almost 0.3%, on top of the Fed, \uc2 BoE\uc2  and other rate hike pricing jumps this week, triggered risk-off flows that favor the haven dollar and Treasuries, especially with the 10-year yield near to 3% versus 0.97% for Bunds and JGB yield capped at 25bps by the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 Adding to risk aversion is this week's 2.3% USD/CNH surge to its highest in a year, amid Chinese growth concerns and the ongoing war in Ukraine \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD was down 0.5%, with the current 1.0771 low on EBS just above April's 1.0758 trend low, with word Germany will cut its 2022 growth forecast to 2.2% from 3.6% the latest weight on prices. Earlier Friday the Bundesbank warned German GDP could shrink 2% this year if the war in Ukraine escalates \u8226\'20\'22 Sterling was down 1.5% after the dam of support by 1.3000 gave way early in the day. Ahead of the week's close, prices are probing the 50% Fibo of 2020-21's recovery at 1.2831, a close below which would next target 1.2729, a Fibo-projection off January's major top \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY was up 0.13% after spiking briefly above 1.2900 as BOJ Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 's comments Friday confirmed there's no need for tightening policy, leaving this week's 20-year peak at 129.43 back in play \u8226\'20\'22 Safe-haven dollar buying, falling commodities and ongoing Chinese growth concerns sent AUD/USD and NZD/USD down 1.7% and 1.66%, respectively \u8226\'20\'22 USD/CAD gained 1%, with above forecast Canadian retail sales and March PPI's 4% m/m surge the biggest in 66 years keeping further \uc2 BOC\uc2  rates hikes at 205bps, for a year-end level just above 3%, slightly above the Fed's year-end pricing \u8226\'20\'22 EM and high-beta \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were broadly lower, and risk-off flows also weighed on bitcoin and ether, as did fresh concerns about the trend in hacking and theft in the space \u8226\'20\'22 Next week's U.S. calendar is light on first-tier reports until Thursday's Q1 GDP and core CPI reports\par \par \uc2 Commentary and Analysis EUR/USD-Dollar, risk-off trump tighter spreads \u8226\'20\'22 US rates rally on expectations for a hawkish Fed \u8226\'20\'22 US$ gains on rate increases while risk-off drives flight to safety \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY falls to 138.34 on EBS in NY, after an early rise EUR/USD drops \u8226\'20\'22 Pair trades 1.0845-1.0771 on EBs in NY, bounces & nears 1.0795 late \u8226\'20\'22 Reuters report on cut to German growth weighs down the euro \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD trades lower despite tighter German-US 2-yr spreads \u8226\'20\'22 Techs are bearish; RSIs falling, pair below falling 10- & 21-DMAs \u8226\'20\'22 French presidential election is a risk, polls favor Macron USD/JPY-Stalks 20-year highs after \uc2 BOJ\uc2  defends ultra-easy policies \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY spiked past 129 on BOJ Kuroda's dovish comments \u8226\'20\'22 Japan's inflation pressures modest vs US, Europe and others \u8226\'20\'22 But Wed's 129.43 20-year peak on EBS not retested yet \u8226\'20\'22 Heavily O/B prices, biggest spec long since 2018 beg for consolidation \u8226\'20\'22 Uptrend also slowed by 10-yr Tsy-JGB yield spreads cresting \u8226\'20\'22 Rapid Fed rates hikes lift S-T spreads, but Tsy curves is flattening \u8226\'20\'22 Eventually Fed hikes, QT will lower inflation and GDP growth \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightening beyond \uc2 BOJ\uc2  stasis already hitting riskier assets \u8226\'20\'22 Also concern yen's broad plunge will weaken Japan's economy \u8226\'20\'22 MOF might eventually try to break its fall, but G7 window now past \u8226\'20\'22 Daily on-close pivot pt & 10-DMA props are at 126.98-96 \u8226\'20\'22 Uptrend's intact while closes are above those levels GBP/USD-Clobbered by bad data, Downing Street risk and chart breakdown \u8226\'20\'22 Sterling slammed by dour UK data and PM's predicament \u8226\'20\'22 Early crash below major range base and tech props by 1.3000 \u8226\'20\'22 Prices down 1.5% toward close, probing 50% of 2020-21 rise @1.2831 \u8226\'20\'22 Close below would target 161.8% Fibo off Jan's top at 1.2729 \u8226\'20\'22 Sept 2020 swing low is at 1.2676, 61.8% Fibo is by 1.2500 \u8226\'20\'22 Specs already second-most net short since Oct 2019 \u8226\'20\'22 Weekly RSIs are their most oversold since March 2020 \u8226\'20\'22 Issue now is if Fed or \uc2 BoE\uc2  can engineer soft landings AUD/USD-Rates, risk-off give shorts the advantage \u8226\'20\'22 Expectations for a hawkish fed rallies US rates, US$ \u8226\'20\'22 Risk sours and investors seek out safe assets like US$ & the yen \u8226\'20\'22 Equities, copper, iron-ore trade down on the day \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened NY near 0.7310 and then extended the overnight fall \u8226\'20\'22 Pair dipped below 0.7240 late, drop aided by AUD/JPY nearing 93.00 \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD techs are bearish; RSIs fall, 200-DMA & daily cloud top break \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD probability of trading below 0.7000 increasing Yen's rebound attempt vs dollar runs into \uc2 BOJ\uc2  reality USD/JPY resumed its uptrend after Wednesday's one-day correction, as \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 's comments on Friday confirmed there's no need for tightening policy. Meanwhile, multiple rate hikes from the Fed, \uc2 BoE\uc2 , \uc2 RBA\uc2 , and even the \uc2 ECB\uc2 , are being priced in. USD/JPY sprinted up from today's 127.74 low on EBS toward Wednesday's 20-year peak at 129.43. There has been speculation that the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  might raise its 25bp yield curve control cap on 10-year JGB yields because of constant market pressure recently to clear it amid soaring competing rates elsewhere. But, as Kuroda notes, Japan does not face the same inflationary pressures as the U.S. and Europe. Even today's March inflation report showing a 0.8% yr/yr increase overstates the issue, as the core-core rate fell 0.7% yr/yr. There will be a jump in CPI in April due to a base effect, but underlying inflation will remain relatively tame. USD/JPY's rebound toward new highs is being slowed somewhat by longer-term Treasury-JGB yields spreads having gone sideways since Wednesday, as the Treasury yield curve flattens on the hope that Fed tightening will lower inflation over time. Incredibly overbought prices and the worry further yen losses will do more harm than good to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy, eventually forcing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are also headwinds. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3jZ6U8h"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3jZ6U8h}}}\uc2  AUD/USD probability of trading below 0.7000 increasing AUD/USD dropped to a 1-month low Friday as it probes key 0.7290/70 support but the probability of trading at sub-0.7000 levels is growing given China growth and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns, as well as Fed expectations and technicals. China\u8217\'20\'19s zero-covid policy lock downs have driven downgrades to China's GDP outlook and increased concerns over global growth. Economic uncertainty is driving investors out of China's yuan and into safer assets. USD/CNH rallied to a 1-year high and may test 6.6000. Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are dealing with similar weakness as investors rein in their global growth expectations. U.S. interest rates continue to hit new highs as investors expect the Fed to take an aggressive stance to fight inflation. Eurodollars have fallen to fresh lows and the U.S. 2-year yield hit fresh highs as investors price in a higher Fed terminal rate. That pricing is helping to underpin the U.S. dollar. Technicals also highlight downside risks for AUD/USD. Daily charts show a head-and-shoulders top forming. Falling daily and monthly RSIs imply downside momentum is in place, and AUD/USD has pierced the 50% Fibo retracement of the 0.6968-0.7661 rally. These bearish factors put the wind at the backs of AUD/USD shorts, and a test of the January monthly low at 0.6968 cannot be ruled out. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3jYQCw6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3jYQCw6}}}\uc2  Chart of the Day (GBP/USD) Sterling finally broke repeated range lows near 1.3000 after a poor showing for UK data on Friday, and now has scope to finish retracing half of its 2020-21 pandemic recovery to 1.2831 and reaching the next Fibo target at 1.2729, if 1.2831 is closed below. Sell stops were run after the UK data misses, with prices last down 1.3% to their lowest since November 2020. The drop is being backstopped by broader dollar gains, as the market has now priced in an additional 250bps of Fed rate hikes by year-end versus 162bps of added \uc2 BoE\uc2  rate hikes, putting the Fed and \uc2 BoE\uc2 's respective policy rates at 2.82% and 2.3% at year-end. Today's breakdown followed several weeks of failed attempts to post a daily close below 1.3000, and no breaches of the 100% Fibo objective taken off January's 1.3749-3359 drop, at 1.2970. With today's plunge, and close, set to be well below there, the 161.8% Fibo at 1.2729 now looks within reach. But first the 50% retracement at 1.2831 must be closed below. Given the 38.2% Fibo was by December's major lows, the 50% is a quite credible target, as is the 61.8% Fibo at 1.2497. A slip in U.S. April services activity and surprise rise in manufacturing is weighing on Treasury yields and already wobbly stocks, the latter a drag on the risk-sensitive pound and boost for the haven dollar. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3OuJDcf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3OuJDcf}}}\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-4-25T19:38:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220422ei4m03lbl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
