\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-World stocks hit five-week low, yields soar on rate hike outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 810 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2022\par 13:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * S&P futures down 0.25%, European stocks off 1.3%\par \par \uc2 * Yuan heading for worst week since 2018\par \par \uc2 * U.S. 2-year Treasury yields hit 2018 highs\par \par \uc2 * German 2-year yields hit 2013 highs\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 By Carolyn Cohn\par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 22 (Reuters) - World stocks hit five-week lows, U.S. stock index futures indicated a lower Wall Street open and bond yields soared to multi-year highs on Friday as investors brace for rate hikes in the United States, Britain and the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 The yuan struck a nine-month low, meanwhile, as lockdowns in Shanghai hit China's growth prospects.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said on Thursday that a half-point interest rate increase would be "on the table" when the Fed meets in May, adding it would be appropriate to "be moving a little more quickly".\par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials said on Thursday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might start hiking euro zone rates as early as July, while \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  interest rate-setter Catherine Mann said that borrowing costs would probably have to rise further.\par \par \uc2 Euro zone money markets now fully price in a 25 basis point rate hike by July.\par \par \uc2 "The Fed, the \uc2 ECB\uc2  and the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  were pushing hawkish commentaries on the markets and markets have reacted," said Monica Defend, head of Amundi Institute, though she added:\par \par \uc2 "For the euro area, we are more sceptical on the fragility of the economic cycle, there is big potential for a recession to take place in Germany and Italy."\par \par \uc2 U.S. purchasing managers' data is due later on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Euro zone business growth unexpectedly accelerated this month, with the bloc's dominant services sector seeing a sharp increase in activity as consumers shrugged off soaring prices, a survey showed on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturers, however, struggled as supply chain disruptions caused by the pandemic have been exacerbated by Russia's invasion of Ukraine and renewed lockdowns in China.\par \par \uc2 Russia pressed its new offensive in eastern Ukraine while in the port city of Mariupol, teams of volunteers collected corpses from the ruins after Moscow declared victory there despite Ukrainian forces holding out.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's world equities index was down 0.49% at its lowest since mid-March, and was heading for a 0.75% drop on the week.\par \par \uc2 S&P futures were down 0.25% after Wall Street indexes fell on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 European stocks dropped 1.3%, with France's CAC 40 down 1.63% ahead of Sunday's presidential run-off vote. Britain's FTSE fell 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 Selling pressure persisted in bond markets, driving five-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields to 3.049% and two-year yields to 2.776%, both at their highest since late 2018.\par \par \uc2 German two-year yields rose to 0.277%, their highest since 2013.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the yuan hit a nine-month low and was on course for its worst week since 2018.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  cut its forecast for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, adding to the increasingly gloomy view on the yuan among top investment banks.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was steady at 128.35 yen after talk of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The euro was flat against the dollar to $1.0822, giving up Thursday's bounce as nerves about Sunday's French presidential election creep in.\par \par \uc2 "Whatever the outcome, economic momentum should remain against Europe\u8217\'20\'19s favor at this time, preventing the euro from a material repricing upward in the next couple of months,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 UBS\uc2  analysts said in a note.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar index rose 0.2% towards recent two-year highs.\par \par \uc2 Sterling fell to its lowest since late 2020 against the dollar, after British retail sales dropped in March by more than expected.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan fell 1.1% to a five-week low. Japan's Nikkei declined 1.63%.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices weakened, burdened by the prospect of interest rate hikes, weaker global growth and COVID-19 lockdowns in China hurting demand, even as the \uc2 European Union\uc2  weighed a ban on Russian oil.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude futures were down $1, or 1.35%, at $106.87 a barrel, while U.S. West Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude futures declined $1.50, or 1.42%, to $102.32.\par \par \uc2 The oil price has been increasingly volatile in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Since the creation of the Brent futures contract, there have only been 29 days where the spread between the intra-day high and low was $8 a barrel or more. Of those, 16 have occurred this year.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold fell 0.9% to $1,933.73 per ounce.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Marc Jones in London, Stella Qiu in Shanghai and Tom Westbrook in Singapore; Editing by Christian Schmollinger, Tomasz Janowski and Chizu Nomiyama) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-4-22T12:45:50.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220422ei4m01vld\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
