\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks strike five-week lows on rate hike view, lockdowns hit yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2022\par 10:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * S&P futures down 0.18%, European stocks off 1.06%\par \par \uc2 * U.S. 2-year Treasury yields hit 2018 highs\par \par \uc2 * German 2-year yields hit 2014 highs\par \par \uc2 * MSCI Asia ex-Japan index hits 5-week low\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 By Carolyn Cohn and Stella Qiu\par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 22 (Reuters) - World stocks fell to five-week lows and bond yields rose on Friday as investors fretted about rate hikes in the United States and the euro zone, while the yuan struck a seven-month low as lockdowns in Shanghai hit China's growth prospects.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said on Thursday that a half-point interest rate increase would be "on the table" when the Fed meets in May, adding it would be appropriate to "be moving a little more quickly".\par \par \uc2 His remarks strengthened market expectations of at least another half-percentage-point rate hike next month, and \uc2 Nomura\uc2  now expects 75-basis-point hikes at the Fed's June and July meetings, which would be the biggest since 1994.\par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials said on Thursday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might start hiking euro zone rates as early as July, while \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  interest rate-setter \uc2 Catherine Mann\uc2  said that borrowing costs would probably have to rise further.\par \par \uc2 Euro zone money markets now fully price in a 25 basis point rate hike by July.\par \par \uc2 "The Fed, the \uc2 ECB\uc2  and the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  were pushing hawkish commentaries on the markets and markets have reacted," said Monica Defend, head of Amundi Institute, though she added:\par \par \uc2 "For the euro area, we are more sceptical on the fragility of the economic cycle, there is big potential for a recession to take place in Germany and Italy."\par \par \uc2 The euro zone is feeling the impact of the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The mayor of Mariupol made a new appeal on Friday for the "full evacuation" of the southern Ukrainian city which President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  says is now controlled by Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 Markets are also watching out for euro zone and U.S. flash purchasing managers' data for April, with French data showing business activity grew at the fastest pace in more than four years, helped by fewer COVID-19 restrictions.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's world equities index was down 0.41% at its lowest since mid-March, and was heading for a 0.7% drop on the week.\par \par \uc2 S&P futures were 0.18% softer after Wall Street indexes fell on Thursday, with the S&P 500 down 1.5% and the Nasdaq down 2%.\par \par \uc2 European stocks dropped 1.06%, with France's CAC 40 down 1.39% ahead of Sunday's presidential run-off vote. Britain's FTSE fell 0.52%.\par \par \uc2 Selling pressure persisted in bond markets, driving five-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields to 3.049% and two-year yields to 2.7620%, both at their highest since late 2018.\par \par \uc2 German two-year yields rose to 0.211%, their highest since early 2014.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the yuan hit a seven-month low and was on course for its worst week since 2019, as lockdowns in Shanghai take a bite out of growth.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  say a comprehensive easing package on all fronts, both monetary and fiscal, from Beijing is needed, including loosening measures in the property sector, which has been hit hard by restrictions on access to credit.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was down 0.25% against the yen at 128 after talk of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though the euro fell 0.29% against the dollar to $1.0805, giving up Thursday's bounce as nerves about Sunday's French presidential election creep in.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar index rose 0.26% towards recent two-year highs.\par \par \uc2 Sterling fell to its lowest since late 2020 against the dollar, after British retail sales dropped in March by more than expected.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan fell 1% to a five-week low, weighed down by a 1.6% loss for Australia's resource-heavy index and a 0.86% drop in South Korean shares.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei declined 1.63%.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices weakened, burdened by the prospect of interest rate hikes, weaker global growth and COVID-19 lockdowns in China hurting demand, even as the \uc2 European Union\uc2  weighed a ban on Russian oil.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude futures were down 55 cents, or 0.57%, at $107.86 a barrel, while U.S. West Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude futures declined 50 cents, or 0.48%, to $103.30.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was last down 0.09% to $1,949.90 per ounce.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Tom Westbrook in Singapore; Editing by Edwina Gibbs, Christian Schmollinger and Tomasz Janowski) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-4-22T09:20:26.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220422ei4m014w9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
