US yields higher \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD firmed in Asia and traded to 1.0846 despite US yields moving higher \u8226\'20\'22 Talk of EUR/AUD short-covering helped to underpin EUR/USD \u8226\'20\'22 Heading into the afternoon it is trading around 1.0840 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD getting some support on expectations Macron will win French election \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD holding around 10-day MA at 1.0833 with bids tipped at 1.0785/1.0800 \u8226\'20\'22 Support is at the April 14 trend low at 1.0758 \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is @ the 21-day MA @ 1.0913 and break would ease downward pressure AUD/USD-Falls in Asia as risk assets sold and US yields firm \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened -1.07% at 0.7371 after reversing lower on hawkish Fed \u8226\'20\'22 After trading at 0.7377 the AUD/USD came under heavy pressure \u8226\'20\'22 US yields continued to march higher in Asia while AXJ equity index fell 1.2% \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD broke below support at 0.7335/45 before settling above 0.7340 \u8226\'20\'22 The 55-day MA & a trendline are @ 0.7335/45 and close below would be bearish \u8226\'20\'22 Key support is at the 200-day MA at 0.7295 with bids eyed around 0.7300 \u8226\'20\'22 Key commodities showing signs of topping and adding pressure on AUD \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is at the 21-day MA at 0.7458 and break would ease pressure GBP/USD-Stable, despite soured risk and higher UST yields \u8226\'20\'22 -0.05%, with U.S. dollar little changed, despite higher UST yields in Asia \u8226\'20\'22 Traded a 1.3009-1.3033 range with only occasional bursts of interest on D3 \u8226\'20\'22 British consumer morale plunged near all-time low in April: GfK \u8226\'20\'22 Charts, positive momentum studies, 5, 10 & 21 day moving averages edge lower \u8226\'20\'22 21 day Bolli bands contract - negative bias after a week of consolidation \u8226\'20\'22 Close above 1.3073 21 day moving average would be a bullish signal \u8226\'20\'22 Wednesday's 1.2995 low and last week's 1.2973 2022 base first supports \u8226\'20\'22 1.3167-1.3174 is major resistance, April high and 61.8% March-April fall USD/JPY looks stuck between 127.50-129.50 till FOMC USD/JPY looks to have settled for now in a core range of 127.50-129.50, and the thinking in Tokyo is that it will largely stay within that span until the next FOMC meeting on May 3-4. The slight change in Japan official-speak on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks to have struck a chord, and Japanese players are looking to hunker down for Golden Week holidays beginning next Friday, April 29. USD/JPY traded to a fresh multi-decade high of 129.43 on April 20. It fell back to 127.47 during the same session, and this 127.50 area looks to be the base for now. Very good Japanese importer demand appeared on the dip on April 20; that should be the case again with subsequent dips. Higher prices for raw materials and the weaker JPY have put a squeeze on importers, especially since upside hedges became history as option barriers were taken out one by one on the way to 129.43. Asymetric risk for EUR/USD over the weekend EUR/USD heads into the weekend with the chances of a sharp fall far higher than sustained strength. President Macron holds a lead in the polls of 55.5%-57.5%. While lead is strong, there is concern regarding voter turnout. Election polls in recent years have a mixed track record, so Macron is no certainty. Should Le Pen win, the whole outlook for EU policy would change, as she is sceptical of a federal Europe. A Macron victory will likely result in a knee-jerk euro bounce on Monday, while a Le Pen win would see the euro sharply lower on major uncertainty surrounding future EU policy. The euro has been the bellwether for sentiment on the Ukraine-Russia conflict. There is no sign of a diplomatic compromise, as the war intensifies, which will continue to cap euro strength. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy remains a negative for EUR/USD, as the \uc2 ECB\uc2  ponders the timing of ending QE and raising rates, while the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  appears to have declared war on inflation. A Le Pen victory could see a test of 1.0636 2020 low, which would be a 1.87% fall, while a Macron win would perhaps trigger another test of 1.0921, 38.2% of the March-April fall, currently a 0.75% bounce. AUD/USD may lose allure as equity flows signal shift The Australian dollar has been one of the best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the Russian invasion of Ukraine due to the large gains in energy and metal prices since the start of the conflict. Investor flows in the past 24 hours, though, may be tipping an end to commodities and commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a safe-haven. Despite a small rise in oil and base metals on Thursday, the energy and material equity market sectors fell sharply due to fears of demand destruction emanating from a slowdown in China and aggressive tightening by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 . The S&P material sector fell 1.68% on Thursday to complete a bearish outside day, while one of the best performing stock markets, the Toronto Stock Exchange, had its worst day in three months with the energy sector of that market falling 3.6%. If flows out of commodity safe-haven destinations are sustained, it will leave the Australia dollar in a vulnerable position due to a divergence of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expectations. While the Fed is expected to hike rates by 50 basis points at their May meeting and perhaps follow that up with two more jumbo hikes, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  is expected to take a far more cautious approach. Support for the AUD/USD is at 0.7335/45 where the 55-day moving average converges with a trend-line. A break below 0.7335 targets the key 200-day moving average at 0.7295. Those support levels will likely be tested while resistance at the 21-day moving average at 0.7458 caps rallies.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest