\par\par \b \uc2 Japanese Yen Could Weaken Further on US Rate Rise Bets -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2022\par 09:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0757 GMT - The Japanese yen could extend its recent losses against the dollar on market bets the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will raise interest rates aggressively, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. "If market participants become more confident that the Fed will need to raise rates to 3.00% and well beyond to regain control of inflation it will increase the possibility of USD/JPY retesting the highs from back in 2002 when it hit a high of 135.15 and then from 1998 when it hit a high of 147.66," \uc2 MUFG\uc2  analysts say. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 1997 and 1998 when USD/JPY reached such levels, they say. USD/JPY falls 0.3% to 128.510 after hitting a 20-year high of 129.400 overnight, according to Refinitiv. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0752 GMT - Lacking more pressing data releases today, credit investors will wait for key eurozone data slated for release on Thursday, including inflation, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  's research team. "The economic calendar does not contain pivotal data releases, with the focus being on tomorrow's final eurozone Consumer Price Index and consumer confidence data," analysts at the Italian bank say. Economists polled by the Wall Street Journal expect annual inflation in the 19-member single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  zone to have jumped to 7.5% in March from 5.9% in February. Meanwhile, war in Ukraine is expected weigh further on consumer confidence, with the WSJ poll predicting the Flash Consumer Confidence Indicator to fall to minus 20 in April from minus 18.7 the month before. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0741 GMT - The DXY dollar index should remain strong and hold above 100 as recent comments by \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials have backed up market expectations for a string of U.S. interest-rate rises, ING analysts say in a note. "A half-point hike [in U.S. rates] now looks nailed on for 4 May and dissent seems unlikely," they say. "The fact that U.S. equity markets in particular have held up well has allowed Fed tightening expectations to remain strong...and the dollar to stay bid." The DXY falls 0.2% to 100.738, having hit a two-year high of 101.0350 overnight. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0714 GMT - USD/SGD is trading steadily in the Asian afternoon session, but may be weighed by possible unwinding of USD longs by market participants pricing in expected quantitative tightening by the Fed, analysts say. The key focus is on the FOMC meeting in early May when a vote is expected, with balance-sheet rundown likely starting as soon as May or June at the latest, says Maybank in a research report, which doesn't rule out the case of 'buy rumor, sell fact' on a QT announcement. USD/SGD is little changed at 1.3675. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0712 GMT - Sterling has limited scope to rise versus the euro as the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is unlikely to raise interest rates by as much as the market expects due to weak economic growth, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. "We are not convinced the BOE will be in a position to deliver what is priced into the market with growth likely to be very weak through the remainder of this year," \uc2 MUFG\uc2  analysts say, noting the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  cut its U.K. economic growth forecasts significantly on Tuesday. The \uc2 BOE\uc2  will probably deliver a further two 25 basis points rate rises and then pause, they say. EUR/GBP rises 0.1% to 0.8306 but GBP/USD rises 0.1% to 1.3016. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0711 GMT - The continuing selloff in eurozone government bonds pushed the 10-year Bund yield above 0.90% on Tuesday, leaving it on track to reach 1%, Michael Leister, head of interest rates strategy at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  , says. "We stick with our short bias with the market set to target the 1% in 10y Bund yields," he says in a note. The next stage in nominal rates will be almost entirely driven by real rates, he adds. The 10-year Bund yield is trading 1.3 basis points higher at 0.940%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0710 GMT - The rare tap of Germany's 2.50% August 2046 Bund, an off-the-run ultra-long Bund first sold in 2014, is expected to attract solid demand, Michael Leister, head of interest rates strategy at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  , says in a note. The tap "should be smoothly absorbed as demand for Bund collateral remains high," he says. The German Finance Agency last tapped the August 2046 Bund in June 2019, while on Wednesday it will offer EUR1 billion. The August 2046 Bund is trading at yield of 1%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT - The FTSE 100 is seen opening higher after gains on Wall Street overnight with spreadbetting firm IG expecting London's index of blue-chip stocks to rise 10 points. U.S. stocks were buoyed by the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  's relatively modest downgrade to U.S. economic growth on Tuesday and by Federal Reserve officials \uc2 Charles Evans\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Bostic\uc2  expressing the ability of the U.S. economy to stand on its own even as interest rates rise, \uc2 CMC Markets\uc2  analyst Michael Hewson says in a note. "This strong finish looks set to translate into a similarly positive European open." (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0636 GMT - Incumbent French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  is expected to secure a narrow win over far-right candidate \uc2 Marine Le Pen\uc2  in the second round of presidential elections on Sunday, but Wednesday's TV debate will be key, and it could also impact French government bonds on Thursday, \uc2 Citi\uc2  's strategists say in a research note. "The incumbent has consolidated his lead in recent polls (around 56%-44% for the last three polls...but today's debate could help undecided voters make up their mind towards either candidate or abstention," \uc2 Citi\uc2  's strategists say. This could drive early reaction in French government bonds, known as OATs, on Thursday morning, the strategists say, adding that OATs seem largely priced for a Macron win. The 10-year OAT yield is trading 0.8 basis points higher at 1.409%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0633 GMT - US inflation-protected securities, known as TIPS, are the best asset if investors follow the stagflationary narrative, Julius Baer's analysts say in a research note, nonetheless, maintaining a neutral rating on them. "TIPS are still sensitive to moves in real yields, and current high inflation expectations will be dampened with further rate hikes by the Fed, eventually leading to higher real rates from here," Julius Baer's analysts say. Therefore, despite the appealing characteristics of a real coupon, they hold on to the neutral rating of TIPS. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0608 GMT - Julius Baer's analysts remain positive over eurozone peripheral government bonds on assumption that fragmentation will be avoidable, they say in a research note. "We remain optimistic that the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will find a balance between price stability and the risk of fragmentation, and we therefore maintain our call for peripheral debt," the analysts say. Eurozone peripheral government bonds have a significant yield advantage over core eurozone debt, Julius Baer's analysts add. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0445 GMT - The Chinese yuan is being pressured by deteriorating market sentiment, analysts say. The first surprise was Fed's hawkish shift and surging Treasury yields, says \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2  's senior Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Ken Cheung in an email. The second surprise was the resurgence of Covid-19 in China and the strict lockdowns, he adds. USD/CNY is up 0.07% at 6.3981 after earlier touching 6.4056, the highest intraday level since Nov. 11, while USD/CNH is little changed at 6.4188 after earlier touching 6.4384, highest intraday level since Oct. 18, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 20, 2022 03:57 ET (07:57 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220420ei4k0018s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
