\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei Gains 0.7%, Led by Auto Stocks -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,481 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2022\par 02:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 2017 ET - Japanese stocks are higher in morning trade, led by gains in auto stocks as USD/JPY hits a new 20-year high and crude-oil prices fell overnight. \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  is up 2.3% and \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2  is 3.0% higher. USD/JPY is at 129.18 after rising to 129.40 earlier, its highest level since April 2002, sharply up from 128.16 as of Tuesday's Tokyo stock-market close. Meanwhile, oil explorer \uc2 Inpex\uc2  is down 0.9% and energy company \uc2 Eneos Holdings\uc2  is 0.6% lower after crude-oil prices dropped overnight. Movements of the yen and commodity prices remain in focus. The Nikkei Stock Average is up 0.7% at 27176.66. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 1920 ET - New Zealand's 1Q consumer price index, due Thursday, is expected to show continued acceleration in inflation that will keep the door open for another 50 bps RBNZ rate increase in May. The index is forecast to rise 2.0% from the previous quarter and 7.1% from a year earlier, according to the median of seven economists in a Wall Street Journal survey. The inflation rate reached 5.9% in the October-December quarter, its highest since 7.6% in mid-1990, due to fiscal and monetary stimulus and snarled global supply chains. The \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  accelerated its monetary tightening last week, with a 50 bps rate increase, but did not signal a higher eventual peak for its cash rate. (stephen.wright@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1739 ET - The odds of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  are rising though still not a baseline scenario, says \uc2 CBA\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 Joseph Capurso\uc2  , after USD/JPY and AUD/JPY continued to gain overnight. "We do not know what level of USD/JPY is the line in the sand. Expect more comments from senior official about the need for stability in the yen," he says. "If there is to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities, we judge it will most likely happen this month." USD/JPY is up 6.0% this month after a 5.8% gain in March while AUD/JPY has gained 12% in the past four weeks. "Japanese government officials are more concerned about the speed of JPY weakness rather than the weakness," Capurso says.(stephen.wright@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1704 ET - The WSJ Dollar Index gains 0.3% as the dollar strengthens 1.5% against the yen and is slightly weaker against the euro. Corpay's \uc2 Karl Schamotta\uc2  says policy makers in Japan are showing concern about the pace of the yen's decline "but so far haven't signaled they'd be willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  just yet." The chief market strategist says the other big story today with regard to the dollar were comments from Chicago Federal Reserve President \uc2 Charles Evans\uc2  in a talk at the Economic Club of New York in which he said he didn't think rate hikes larger than 50 basis points would be necessary to tame high inflation. Schamotta says that means "50 bp moves are likely to happen." (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1653 ET - The IPC stock index rises 0.9% to close at 54633 points, gaining in line with US equities. \uc2 Cemex\uc2  shares close up 5%, and Televisa shares rise 4.3%. The peso gives back some of its recent gains, trading at 20.02 to the US dollar from 19.83 yesterday. In its latest world economic outlook, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  cut its 2022 economic growth estimate for Mexico to 2% from 2.8%, and lowered the 2023 forecast to 2.5% from 2.7%. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1519 ET - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reconfirmed last week that its Asset Purchase Program will end in 3Q, leaving credit "very unsupported" from September, says ING in a research note. Reinvestments will still be "decent" in September, at around EUR2.5bn under the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Corporate Sector Purchase Program (CSPP) but will be relatively low thereafter for the remainder of the year, the Dutch bank says. "The \uc2 ECB\uc2  has already reduced its normal 40% orders in primary market purchases in CSPP down to 30%. We may even see this drop to 20% in May/June, before the programme ends," it says. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1414 ET - About one-third of all homeowners, including those without mortgages, have a rate under 4%, substantially below today's level of 5%, according to \uc2 Redfin\uc2  . "With rates now at their highest level in over a decade, many of these homeowners may be incentivized to stay put because selling their home and buying another could mean giving up their ultra-low mortgage rate and increasing their monthly housing bill," says the real estate brokerage. This may be contributing to a decline in home listings, \uc2 Redfin\uc2  adds. The higher average 30-year fixed mortgage rate has helped push the typical homebuyer's monthly mortgage payment to a record high of $2,288, up 35% from about a year ago, \uc2 Redfin\uc2  says. (chris.wack@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1348 ET - Canadian energy company \uc2 Cenovus\uc2  is expected to report 1Q in line with expectations, \uc2 Greg Pardy\uc2  of \uc2 RBC\uc2  says in a research note. Output in 1Q is expected to be around 800,200 barrels of oil equivalent a day, down from an average of 825,300 barrels in the previous quarter. Canada and US refining operating cash flow before tax is expected at C$290M. The analyst also pegs cash flow at C$2.2B, or C$1.12 a share, and adjusted free cash flow of C$1.4B. Pardy also expects \uc2 Cenovus\uc2  to better define shareholder return plans. \uc2 Cenovus\uc2  will report its first-quarter results on April 27. Shares are down 0.6% at C$22.73. (adriano.marchese@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1331 ET - Freight handling delays at Southern California big seaports are growing again after several months of improvements. The average wait time for inbound containers to get on trucks at the ports of Los Angeles and Long Beach rose to 6.26 days in March, up from 5.84 days the month before after three straight monthly declines in so-called dwell time. The wait for rail service also rose sharply to 7.7 days in March, the longest rail dwell time since August 2021. The deterioration came as the neighboring ports handled 922,476 loaded inbound containers, measured in 20-foot equivalent units, last month. That was the busiest month for imports since May 2021. (paul.page@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1326 ET - Mexican economic activity likely expanded 0.8% in March from the previous month, according to statistics institute Inegi's nowcast estimate, a fifth straight monthly rise that points to about a 1.6% expansion in 1Q GDP after a flat 4Q. "If these increases are confirmed, economic activity in March will have reached its highest level postpandemic, but still 2.1% below the prepandemic level of January 2020," former Inegi president Julio Santaella says in a tweet. Inegi is scheduled to releases its flash 1Q GDP estimate on April 29. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1135 ET - The Swiss franc's depreciation Tuesday suggests rising global yields are finally making their mark on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Saxo Bank\uc2  says. The franc's recent strength despite rising yields and Switzerland joining other countries in sanctioning Russia suggests there is some residual safe-haven seeking in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some of it possibly linked to the French presidential election, \uc2 Saxo Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 John Hardy\uc2  says in a note. If far-right candidate \uc2 Marine Le Pen\uc2  fails to secure a victory in the French election Sunday and yields continue to rise globally, the franc "could be in for some catch-up weakening," he says. USD/CHF rises to a 21-month high of 0.9498 and EUR/CHF rises to a two-and-a-half-week high of 1.0246, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1107 ET - \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  , who holds an average nine-point lead in the polls ahead of the second round of presidential elections, looks likely to be the first French president in two decades to win a second term, \uc2 Eurasia Group\uc2  says in a note. The political-risk consulting firm has adjusted its basecase to give Macron an 80% chance of reelection, from 70% previously. Macron has swung sharply toward the interests of left and green voters in the last nine days, stepping back from his plan to increase the French retirement age to 65 and promising that his second term will be dominated by state planning for a low-energy future and climate change, in the hopes of strengthening the ratio of left-wing transfers in his favor, \uc2 Eurasia Group\uc2  says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 19, 2022 20:17 ET (00:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220420ei4k00007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
