\par\par \b \uc2 No 'good or bad' in exchange rates, Japan official says, as yen slips\b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2022\par 23:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Reuters) -A senior Japanese government official said on Wednesday that there was no "good or bad" in exchange rates, in remarks that suggested Tokyo was not ready to take immediate action to shore up the weakening yen. In an interview with Reuters, Deputy Chief Cabinet Secretary \uc2 Seiji Kihara\uc2  reiterated authorities' common refrain that sharp moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates were undesirable and that the government would closely watch the impact of a softer yen on the economy.\par \par \uc2 However, asked about growing concerns that the falling yen was problematic for Japan -- including from its own finance minister, \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  -- Kihara said: "There's no such thing as good or bad" in exchange rates. "Stability is important."\par \par \uc2 A weak yen, once a blessing for the export-led economy, has now added to import costs, particularly for fuel amid the war in Ukraine. That is threatening to derail Japan's frail economic recovery as rising prices hit consumers and companies.\par \par \uc2 The dollar at one point scaled a fresh two-decade peak to the yen of 129.43 yen on Wednesday, compared with around 114 yen at the beginning of March.\par \par \uc2 Some investors said a fall beyond 130 yen could be a trigger for authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But others doubt such \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  could halt its downtrend for long, with the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  about to tighten policy and the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) set to keep its policy super-loose for some time.\par \par \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 , who has long argued that a weak yen was positive for the economy as a whole, tweaked his remarks this week to warn sharp yen weakening was negative.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reverse its massive stimulus and raise interest rates to boost the yen, Kihara would not comment. Instead, he said it was up to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to choose whatever tools were available to meet its 2013 joint statement with the government, including a 2% inflation goal.\par \par \uc2 Kihara declined to speculate on the reasons behind the yen's weakness, and whether current moves were rapid enough to warrant authorities' action. He said he was not in a position to answer as it was up to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities to act appropriately on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 Yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been very rare. The last time \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in 1998 in the wake of the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. Japan has stayed away from the market since 2011 when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to stem yen strength after the devastating earthquake triggered a nuclear crisis.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto and Kentaro Sugiyama; Editing by Chang-Ran Kim and Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-4-20T21:03:50.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220420ei4k03mmt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
